orized by the resource that they primarily affect. For example, you can find endpoints relating to issues in REST API endpoints for issues.\n285: Getting started with the REST API\n286: If you are new to REST APIs, start with the Quickstart or Getting Started guide:\n287: Quickstart for GitHub REST API\n288: Getting started with the REST API\n289: If you are familiar with REST APIs but new to GitHub's REST API, see the authentication documentation:\n290: Authenticating to the REST API\n291: If you are building scripts or applications that use the REST API, the following guides can help:\n292: Scripting with the REST API and JavaScript\n293: Scripting with the REST API and Ruby\n294: Building a GitHub App that responds to webhook events\n295: Building a CLI with a GitHub App\n296: Automatically redelivering failed deliveries for a repository webhook\n297: For a list of libraries to facilitate scripting with the REST API, see Libraries for the REST API.\n298: If you are building scripts or applications that use the REST API, you might also be interested in using webhooks to get notified about events or a GitHub App to access resources on behalf of a user or in an organization. For more information, see About webhooks and Deciding when to build a GitHub App.\n299: Further reading\n300: Comparing GitHub's REST API and GraphQL API\n301: Best practices for using the REST API\n302: Keeping your API credentials secure\n303: Troubleshooting the REST API\n304: Back to top\n305: Help and support\n306: Was this Doc helpful?\n307: YesNo\n308: Help us make GitHub Docs great!\n309: All Docs are open source. See something that's wrong or unclear? Submit a pull request.\n310: Make a contribution\n311: Still need help?\n312: Ask the GitHub communityContact supportExpert servicesBlog\n313: GitHub Inc. © 2026TermsPrivacyStatusPricing"
