      "numbered_text": "1: Managing your personal access tokens - GitHub Docs\n2: Skip to main content\n3: Skip to content\n4: GitHub Docs\n5: Search or ask Copilot/\n6: Menu\n7: Collapse sidebar\n8: Expand sidebar\n9: Home\n10: Authentication\n11: Account security\n12: Manage personal access tokens\n13: Authentication\n14: Account security\n15: Authentication to GitHub\n16: Create a strong password\n17: Switching between accounts\n18: Verifying devices on sign in\n19: Update access credentials\n20: Manage personal access tokens\n21: Reviewing your SSH keys\n22: Deploy keys\n23: Token expiration\n24: Revoke your credentials\n25: Review security log\n26: Security log events\n27: Remove sensitive data\n28: About anonymized URLs\n29: GitHub's IP addresses\n30: SSH key fingerprints\n31: Sudo mode\n32: Unauthorized access\n33: Viewing and managing sessions\n34: Secure your account with 2FA\n35: About 2FA\n36: About mandatory 2FA\n37: Configure 2FA\n38: Configure 2FA recovery\n39: Access GitHub with 2FA\n40: Countries supporting SMS\n41: Change 2FA method\n42: Troubleshooting 2FA\n43: Recover an account with 2FA\n44: Disable 2FA\n45: Authenticate with a passkey\n46: About passkeys\n47: Manage your passkeys\n48: Sign in with a passkey\n49: Connect with SSH\n50: About SSH\n51: Check for existing SSH key\n52: Generate new SSH key\n53: Add a new SSH key\n54: Test your SSH connection\n55: SSH key passphrases\n56: SSH agent forwarding\n57: Managing deploy keys\n58: Troubleshooting SSH\n59: Use SSH over HTTPS port\n60: Recover SSH key passphrase\n61: Deleted or missing SSH keys\n62: Error: Host key verification failed\n63: Permission denied (publickey)\n64: Error: Bad file number\n65: Error: Key already in use\n66: Permission denied other-user\n67: Permission denied other-repo\n68: Agent failure to sign\n69: ssh-add \"illegal option\" error\n70: SSL certificate problem\n71: Error: Unknown key type\n72: SSH key audit\n73: Verify commit signatures\n74: Commit signature verification\n75: Existing GPG keys\n76: Generating a new GPG key\n77: Add a GPG key\n78: Tell Git about your signing key\n79: Associate email with GPG key\n80: Signing commits\n81: Signing tags\n82: Displaying verification for all commits\n83: Troubleshoot verification\n84: Check verification status\n85: Use verified email in GPG key\n86: In this article\n87: Managing your personal access tokens\n88: You can use a personal access token in place of a password when authenticating to GitHub in the command line or with the API.\n89: Copy markdown\n90: In this article\n91: About personal access tokens\n92: Creating a fine-grained personal access token\n93: Pre-filling fine-grained personal access token details using URL parameters\n94: Creating a personal access token (classic)\n95: Deleting a personal access token\n96: Using a personal access token on the command line\n97: Further reading\n98: Warning\n99: Treat your access tokens like passwords. For more information, see Keeping your personal access tokens secure.\n100: About personal access tokens\n101: Personal access tokens are an alternative to using passwords for authentication to GitHub when using the GitHub API or the command line.\n102: Personal access tokens are intended to access GitHub resources on behalf of yourself. To access resources on behalf of an organization, or for long-lived integrations, you should use a GitHub App. For more information, see About creating GitHub Apps.\n103: A token has the same capabilities to access resources and perform actions on those resources that the owner of the token has, and is further limited by any scopes or permissions granted to the token. A token cannot grant additional access capabilities to a user. For example, a personal access token can be configured with an admin:org scope, but if the owner of the token is not an organization owner, the token will not give administrative access to the organization.\n104: Types of personal access tokens\n105: GitHub currently supports two types of personal access tokens: fine-grained personal access tokens and personal access tokens (classic). GitHub recommends that you use fine-grained personal access tokens instead of personal access tokens (classic) whenever possible.\n106: Note\n107: Fine-grained personal access tokens, while more secure and controllable, cannot accomplish every task that a personal access token (classic) can. See the section on Fine-grained personal access tokens limitations below to learn more.\n108: Both fine-grained personal access tokens and personal access tokens (classic) are tied to the user who generated them and will become inactive if the user loses access to the resource.\n109: Organization owners can set a policy to restrict the access of personal access tokens (classic) to their organization. For more information, see Setting a personal access token policy for your organization.\n110: Fine-grained personal access tokens\n111: Fine-grained personal access tokens have several security advantages over personal access tokens (classic), but also have limitations that may prevent you from using them in every scenario. These limits, and our plans to fix them, can be found in the section below.\n112: If you can use a fine-grained personal access token for your scenario, you'll benefit from these improvements:\n113: Each token is limited to access resources owned by a single user or organization.\n114: Each token can be further limited to only access specific repositories for that user or organization.\n115: Each token is granted specific, fine-grained permissions, which offer more control than the scopes granted to personal access tokens (classic).\n116: Organization owners can require approval for any fine-grained personal access tokens that can access resources in the organization.\n117: Fine-grained personal access tokens limitations\n118: Fine-grained personal access tokens do not support every feature of personal access tokens (classic). These feature gaps are not permanent - GitHub is working to close them. You can review our public roadmap for more details on when these scenarios will be supported.\n119: The major gaps in fine-grained personal access tokens are:\n120: Using fine-grained personal access token to contribute to public repos where the user is not a member.\n121: Using fine-grained personal access token to contribute to repositories where the user is an outside or repository collaborator.\n122: Using fine-grained personal access token to access multiple organizations at once.\n123: Using fine-grained personal access token to access Packages.\n124: Using fine-grained personal access token to call the Checks API.\n125: Using fine-grained personal access token to access Projects owned by a user account.\n126: All of these gaps will be solved over time, as GitHub continues to invest in more secure access patterns.\n127: Personal access tokens (classic)\n128: Personal access tokens (classic) are less secure. However, some features currently will only work with personal access tokens (classic):\n129: Only personal access tokens (classic) have write access for public repositories that are not owned by you or an organization that you are not a member of.\n130: Outside collaborators can only use personal access tokens (classic) to access organization repositories that they are a collaborator on.\n131: A few REST API endpoints are only available with a personal access tokens (classic). To check whether an endpoint also supports fine-grained personal access tokens, see the documentation for that endpoint, or see Endpoints available for fine-grained personal access tokens.\n132: If you choose to use a personal access token (classic), keep in mind that it will grant access to all repositories within the organizations that you have access to, as well as all personal repositories in your personal account.\n133: As a security precaution, GitHub automatically removes personal access tokens that haven't been used in a year. To provide additional security, we highly recommend adding an expiration to your personal access tokens.\n134: Keeping your personal access tokens secure\n135: Personal access tokens are like passwords, and they share the same inherent security risks. Before creating a new personal access token, consider if there is a more secure method of authentication available to you:\n136: To access GitHub from the command line, you can use GitHub CLI or Git Credential Manager instead of creating a personal access token.\n137: When using a personal access token in a GitHub Actions workflow, consider whether you can use the built-in GITHUB_TOKEN instead. For more information, see Use GITHUB_TOKEN for authentication in workflows.\n138: If these options are not possible, and you must create a personal access token, consider using another CLI service to store your token securely.\n139: When using a personal access token in a script, you can store your token as a secret and run your script through GitHub Actions. For more information, see Using secrets in GitHub Actions. You can also store your token as a Codespaces secret and run your script in Codespaces. For more information, see Managing your account-specific secrets for GitHub Codespaces.\n140: For more information about best practices, see Keeping your API credentials secure.\n141: Creating a fine-grained personal access token\n142: Note\n143: There is a limit of 50 fine-grained personal access tokens you can create. If you require more tokens or are building automations, consider using a GitHub App for better scalability and management. For more information, see Deciding when to build a GitHub App.\n144: Verify your email address, if it hasn't been verified yet.\n145: In the upper-right corner of any page on GitHub, click your profile picture, then click Settings.\n146: In the left sidebar, click Developer settings.\n147: In the left sidebar, under Personal access tokens, click Fine-grained tokens.\n148: Click Generate new token.\n149: Under Token name, enter a name for the token.\n150: Under Expiration, select an expiration for the token. Infinite lifetimes are allowed but may be blocked by a maximum lifetime policy set by your organization or enterprise owner. For more information, See Enforcing a maximum lifetime policy for personal access tokens.\n151: Optionally, under Description, add a note to describe the purpose of the token.\n152: Under Resource owner, select a resource owner. The token will only be able to access resources owned by the selected resource owner. Organizations that you are a member of will not appear if the organization has blocked the use of fine-grained personal access tokens. For more information, see Setting a personal access token policy for your organization.\n153: Optionally, if the resource owner is an organization that requires approval for fine-grained personal access tokens, below the resource owner, in the box, enter a justification for the request.\n154: Under Repository access, select which repositories you want the token to access. You should choose the minimal repository access that meets your needs. Tokens always include read-only access to all public repositories on GitHub.\n155: If you selected Only select repositories in the previous step, under the Selected repositories dropdown, select the repositories that you want the token to access.\n156: Under Permissions, select which permissions to grant the token. Depending on which resource owner and which repository access you specified, there are repository, organization, and account permissions. You should choose the minimal permissions necessary for your needs.\n157: The REST API reference document for each endpoint states whether the endpoint works with fine-grained personal access tokens and states what permissions are required in order for the token to use the endpoint. Some endpoints may require multiple permissions, and some endpoints may require one of multiple permissions. For an overview of which REST API endpoints a fine-grained personal access token can access with each permission, see Permissions required for fine-grained personal access tokens.\n158: Click Generate token.\n159: If you selected an organization as the resource owner and the organization requires approval for fine-grained personal access tokens, then your token will be marked as pending until it is reviewed by an organization administrator. Your token will only be able to read public resources until it is approved. If you are an owner of the organization, your request is automatically approved. For more information, see Reviewing and revoking personal access tokens in your organization.\n160: Pre-filling fine-grained personal access token details using URL parameters\n161: You can share templates for a fine-grained personal access token via links. By directing users to token creation with relevant fields already completed, you make it easier to automate workflows and improve their developer experience.\n162: Each supported field can be set using a specific query parameter. All parameters are optional and validated by the token generation form to ensure that the combinations of permissions and resource owner make sense.\n163: Here is an example URL template, with line breaks for legibility:\n164: HTTP\n165: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new\n166: ?name=Repo-reading+token\n167: &description=Just+contents:read\n168: &target_name=octodemo\n169: &expires_in=45\n170: &contents=read\n171: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new\n172: ?name=Repo-reading+token\n173: &description=Just+contents:read\n174: &target_name=octodemo\n175: &expires_in=45\n176: &contents=read\n177: Try the URL to create a token with contents:read and metadata:read, with the given name and description and an expiration date 45 days in the future. You'll see an error message indicating Cannot find the specified resource owner: octodemo because you're not a member of the octodemo organization.\n178: Below are some example URLs that generate the tokens we see most often:\n179: Read repository contents\n180: Push access to repositories\n181: Update code and open a pull request\n182: Manage Copilot licenses in an organization\n183: Make Copilot requests\n184: Supported query parameters\n185: To create your own token template, follow the query parameter details provided in this table:\n186: ParameterTypeExample ValueValid ValuesDescription\n187: namestringDeploy%20Bot≤ 40 characters, URL-encodedPre-fills the token's display name.\n188: descriptionstringUsed+for+deployments≤ 1024 chars, URL-encodedPre-fills the description for the token.\n189: target_namestringoctodemoUser or organization slugSets the token's resource target. This is the owner of the repositories that the token will be able to access. If not provided, defaults to the current user's account.\n190: expires_ininteger30 or noneInteger between 1 and 366, or noneDays until expiration or none for non-expiring. If not provided, the default is 30 days, or less if the target has a token lifetime policy set.\n191: <permission>stringcontents=readA series of permission and access levels.The permissions the token should have. Permissions can be set to read, write, or admin, but not every permission supports each of those levels.\n192: Permissions\n193: To set a permission, use its name as a query parameter, with the value specifying the desired access level. Valid access levels are read, write, and admin, but not every permission supports every level — some are read-only, some are write-only, and only a few accept admin.\n194: Combine multiple permissions in the form &contents=read&pull_requests=write&..., using as many as needed.\n195: Tip\n196: You do not need to include both read and write for a permission in your URL — write always includes read, and admin always includes write.\n197: Account permissions\n198: Important\n199: Account permissions can only be used when the current user is the resource owner.\n200: Parameter nameDisplay nameAccess levels\n201: blockingBlock another userread, write\n202: codespaces_user_secretsCodespaces user secretsread, write\n203: copilot_messagesCopilot Chatread\n204: copilot_editor_contextCopilot Editor Contextread\n205: copilot_requestsCopilot requestswrite\n206: emailsEmail addressesread, write\n207: user_eventsEventsread\n208: followersFollowersread, write\n209: gpg_keysGPG keysread, write\n210: gistsGistswrite\n211: keysGit SSH keysread, write\n212: interaction_limitsInteraction limitsread, write\n213: knowledge_basesKnowledge basesread, write\n214: user_modelsModelsread\n215: planPlanread\n216: private_repository_invitationsPrivate repository invitationsread\n217: profileProfilewrite\n218: git_signing_ssh_public_keysSSH signing keysread, write\n219: starringStarringread, write\n220: watchingWatchingread, write\n221: Note\n222: The copilot_requests permission enables making Copilot requests for the given user. These requests count towards the user's premium request allowance. Additional requests beyond the allowance incur overage billing. For more information about Copilot requests and billing, see Requests in GitHub Copilot (legacy).\n223: Repository permissions\n224: Repository permissions work for both user and organization resource owners.\n225: Parameter nameDisplay nameAccess levels\n226: actionsActionsread, write\n227: administrationAdministrationread, write\n228: artifact_metadataArtifact metadataread, write\n229: attestationsAttestationsread, write\n230: code_qualityCode qualityread, write\n231: security_eventsCode scanning alertsread, write\n232: codespacesCodespacesread, write\n233: codespaces_lifecycle_adminCodespaces lifecycle adminread, write\n234: codespaces_metadataCodespaces metadataread\n235: codespaces_secretsCodespaces secretswrite\n236: statusesCommit statusesread, write\n237: contentsContentsread, write\n238: repository_custom_propertiesCustom propertiesread, write\n239: vulnerability_alertsDependabot alertsread, write\n240: dependabot_secretsDependabot secretsread, write\n241: deploymentsDeploymentsread, write\n242: discussionsDiscussionsread, write\n243: environmentsEnvironmentsread, write\n244: issuesIssuesread, write\n245: merge_queuesMerge queuesread, write\n246: metadataMetadataread\n247: pagesPagesread, write\n248: pull_requestsPull requestsread, write\n249: repository_advisoriesRepository security advisoriesread, write\n250: secret_scanning_alertsSecret scanning alertsread, write\n251: secretsSecretsread, write\n252: actions_variablesVariablesread, write\n253: repository_hooksWebhooksread, write\n254: workflowsWorkflowswrite\n255: Organization permissions\n256: Important\n257: Organization permissions can only be used if the resource owner is an organization.\n258: Parameter nameDisplay nameAccess levels\n259: organization_api_insightsAPI Insightsread\n260: organization_administrationAdministrationread, write\n261: organization_user_blockingBlocking usersread, write\n262: organization_campaignsCampaignsread, write\n263: organization_custom_org_rolesCustom organization rolesread, write\n264: organization_custom_propertiesCustom repository propertiesread, write, admin\n265: organization_custom_rolesCustom repository rolesread, write\n266: organization_eventsEventsread\n267: organization_copilot_seat_managementGitHub Copilot Businessread, write\n268: issue_typesIssue Typesread, write\n269: organization_knowledge_basesKnowledge basesread, write\n270: membersMembersread, write\n271: organization_modelsModelsread\n272: organization_network_configurationsNetwork configurationsread, write\n273: organization_announcement_bannersOrganization announcement bannersread, write\n274: organization_codespacesOrganization Codespacesread, write\n275: organization_codespaces_secretsOrganization Codespaces secretsread, write\n276: organization_codespaces_settingsOrganization Codespaces settingsread, write\n277: organization_dependabot_secretsOrganization Dependabot secretsread, write\n278: organization_code_scanning_dismissal_requestsOrganization dismissal requests for code scanningread, write\n279: organization_private_registriesOrganization private registriesread, write\n280: organization_planPlanread\n281: organization_projectsProjectsread, write, admin\n282: organization_secretsSecretsread, write\n283: organization_self_hosted_runnersSelf-hosted runnersread, write\n284: team_discussionsTeam discussionsread, write\n285: organization_actions_variablesVariablesread, write\n286: organization_hooksWebhooksread, write\n287: Creating a personal access token (classic)\n288: Note\n289: Organization owners can restrict the access of personal access token (classic) to their organization. If you try to use a personal access token (classic) to access resources in an organization that has disabled personal access token (classic) access, your request will fail with a 403 response. Instead, you must use a GitHub App, OAuth app, or fine-grained personal access token.\n290: Warning\n291: Your personal access token (classic) can access every repository that you can access. GitHub recommends that you use fine-grained personal access tokens instead, which you can restrict to specific repositories. Fine-grained personal access tokens also enable you to specify fine-grained permissions instead of broad scopes.\n292: Verify your email address, if it hasn't been verified yet.\n293: In the upper-right corner of any page on GitHub, click your profile picture, then click Settings.\n294: In the left sidebar, click Developer settings.\n295: In the left sidebar, under Personal access tokens, click Tokens (classic).\n296: Select Generate new token, then click Generate new token (classic).\n297: In the \"Note\" field, give your token a descriptive name.\n298: To give your token an expiration, select Expiration, then choose a default option or click Custom to enter a date.\n299: Select the scopes you'd like to grant this token. To use your token to access repositories from the command line, select repo. A token with no assigned scopes can only access public information. For more information, see Scopes for OAuth apps.\n300: Click Generate token.\n301: Optionally, to copy the new token to your clipboard, click .\n302: To use your token to access resources owned by an organization that uses SAML single sign-on, authorize the token. For more information, see Authorizing a personal access token for use with single sign-on in the GitHub Enterprise Cloud documentation.\n303: Deleting a personal access token\n304: You should delete a personal access token if it is no longer needed. If you delete a personal access token that was used to create a deploy key, the deploy key will also be deleted.\n305: In the upper-right corner of any page on GitHub, click your profile picture, then click Settings.\n306: In the left sidebar, click Developer settings.\n307: In the left sidebar, under Personal access tokens, click either Fine-grained tokens or Tokens (classic), depending on which type of personal access token you'd like to delete.\n308: To the right of the personal access token you want to delete, click Delete.\n309: Note\n310: If you find a leaked personal access token belonging to someone else, you can submit a revocation request through the REST API. See Best practices for preventing data leaks in your organization.\n311: Using a personal access token on the command line\n312: Once you have a personal access token, you can enter it instead of your password when performing Git operations over HTTPS.\n313: For example, to clone a repository on the command line you would enter the following git clone command. You would then be prompted to enter your username and password. When prompted for your password, enter your personal access token instead of a password.\n314: $ git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPO.git\n315: Username: YOUR-USERNAME\n316: Password: YOUR-PERSONAL-ACCESS-TOKEN\n317: Although you are required to enter your username along with your personal access token, the username is not used to authenticate you. Instead, the personal access token is used to authenticate you. If you do not enter a username, you will receive an error message that your credentials are invalid.\n318: Personal access tokens can only be used for HTTPS Git operations. If your repository uses an SSH remote URL, you will need to switch the remote from SSH to HTTPS.\n319: If you are not prompted for your username and password, your credentials may be cached on your computer. You can update your credentials in the Keychain to replace your old password with the token.\n320: Instead of manually entering your personal access token for every HTTPS Git operation, you can cache your personal access token with a Git client. Git will temporarily store your credentials in memory until an expiry interval has passed. You can also store the token in a plain text file that Git can read before every request. For more information, see Caching your GitHub credentials in Git.\n321: Further reading\n322: About authentication to GitHub\n323: Token expiration and revocation\n324: Back to top\n325: Help and support\n326: Was this Doc helpful?\n327: YesNo\n328: Help us make GitHub Docs great!\n329: All Docs are open source. See something that's wrong or unclear? Submit a pull request.\n330: Make a contribution\n331: Still need help?\n332: Ask the GitHub communityContact supportExpert servicesBlog\n333: GitHub Inc. © 2026TermsPrivacyStatusPricing"
