      "numbered_text": "1: Get started with the Netlify API | Netlify Docs Skip to content\n2: Netlify Docs D o c s\n3: Start\n4: Build\n5: Fundamentals\n6: Build with AI\n7: Configure builds\n8: Git workflows\n9: Environment variables\n10: Frameworks\n11: Post-processing\n12: User-Agent categories\n13: Primitives\n14: AI Gateway\n15: Serverless Functions\n16: Edge Functions\n17: Server\n18: Image CDN\n19: Blobs\n20: Database\n21: Caching\n22: Async Workloads\n23: Deploy\n24: Manage\n25: Accounts & Billing\n26: Projects\n27: Domains\n28: Data & Storage\n29: Security & access\n30: Monitoring & Insights\n31: Preview Servers\n32: Forms\n33: Visual Editor\n34: Routing & Redirects\n35: Support Hub\n36: Extend\n37: Install & use\n38: Develop & share\n39: Netlify SDK for extensions\n40: Building code agents\n41: Framework adapter API\n42: Reference\n43: Error reference\n44: Netlify skills\n45: Request processing order\n46: CLI reference\n47: Netlify SDK for extensions\n48: Visual Editor reference\n49: APIs\n50: Netlify API\n51: Database API\n52: Server API\n53: Frameworks API\n54: Cache API\n55: Blobs API\n56: Serverless Functions API\n57: Edge Functions API\n58: Dev Tool Guides\n59: API and CLI guides\n60: Terraform provider\n61: Command Palette\n62: Resources\n63: Troubleshooting\n64: Changelog\n65: Migrate\n66: Support\n67: Checklists\n68: Release phases\n69: Enterprise credits\n70: AI\n71: API & CLI guides\n72: API & CLI guides\n73: Overview\n74: API guides\n75: Get started with API\n76: Deploy project from a zip file with API\n77: Deploy zip file to production\n78: More API guides\n79: API reference docs\n80: Go client\n81: JS client\n82: CLI guides\n83: Get started with CLI\n84: Local development\n85: Manage functions\n86: Debug with VSCode\n87: More CLI guides\n88: CLI reference docs\n89: On this page\n90: Overview\n91: Make a request\n92: Authentication\n93: Rate limiting\n94: Pagination\n95: Link header\n96: Deploy with the API\n97: File digest method\n98: ZIP file method\n99: Create and deploy at once\n100: Poll for deploy state\n101: Draft deploys\n102: Commonly used endpoints\n103: Sites\n104: Site metadata\n105: Environment variables\n106: Files\n107: Deploys\n108: Snippets\n109: Forms\n110: Hooks\n111: On this page\n112: Overview\n113: Make a request\n114: Authentication\n115: Rate limiting\n116: Pagination\n117: Link header\n118: Deploy with the API\n119: File digest method\n120: ZIP file method\n121: Create and deploy at once\n122: Poll for deploy state\n123: Draft deploys\n124: Commonly used endpoints\n125: Sites\n126: Site metadata\n127: Environment variables\n128: Files\n129: Deploys\n130: Snippets\n131: Forms\n132: Hooks\n133: For the complete Netlify documentation index, see llms.txt. Markdown versions of any documentation page are available by appending .md to its URL.\n134: The new Netlify experience is here. Learn more or join the waitlist.\n135: API And CLI Guides\n136: /\n137: API Guides\n138: Get started with the Netlify API\n139: Copy page View as Markdown\n140: Copy as Markdown\n141: View as Markdown\n142: Use the Netlify REST API to manage atomic deploys of your sites and apps, manage form submissions, inject JavaScript snippets, manage DNSDNSThe system that translates a domain name like yoursite.com into the address computers use to find your site.×, and so much more.\n143: This document covers the basics for interacting with the Netlify API, plus instructions for deploying sites and notes on some commonly used endpoints.\n144: You can browse the OpenAPI reference for the Netlify API to explore available endpoints. Check out our Support Guide on understanding and using Netlify’s API.\n145: Project ID, site_id, and NETLIFY_SITE_ID are the same value\n146: The Netlify UI labels this identifier Project ID, but the API, CLI, and environment variablesenvironment variablesNamed values stored outside your code that your site can read at build time or runtime.× still use site. Every project has exactly one identifier, and all of the following names refer to the same value:\n147: Netlify UI — Project ID (labeled Site ID before the rename)\n148: Netlify API — site_id, including in paths such as /api/v1/sites/{site_id}\n149: Netlify CLI, client libraries, and .netlify/state.json — siteId, siteID, --site, and NETLIFY_SITE_ID`\n150: To copy the value, go to Project configuration > General > Project details > Project information and copy Project ID.\n151: Project names work the same way: the UI’s Project name (formerly Site name) is the API’s site_slug. Wherever the API accepts a {site_id}, you can also pass the project’s domaindomainThe web address people use to visit your site, such as yoursite.com.×, such as mysite.netlify.app.\n152: Additionally, we have two API clients for your convenience:\n153: Go Client\n154: JS Client\n155: If you’d like to interact with the Netlify API using a no-code tool, you can use n8n.io’s Netlify node. The node currently supports the following operations:\n156: Create a new deployment\n157: Get a deployment\n158: Get all deployments\n159: Cancel a deployment\n160: Get a site\n161: Get all sites\n162: Delete a site\n163: Make a request\n164: Section titled “Make a request”\n165: All URLs start with https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/. SSLSSLThe technology that enables HTTPS by encrypting the connection to your site.× only. The path is prefixed with the API version. If we change the API in backward-incompatible ways, we’ll bump the version marker and maintain stable support for the old URLs.\n166: To make a request for all sites you have access to, for example, append the sites index path to the base URL to form something like https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites. Here’s an example in curl:\n167: Terminal window\n168: curl -H \"User-Agent: MyApp (YOUR_NAME@EXAMPLE.COM)\" \\\n169: -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\\n170: https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites\n171: Authentication\n172: Section titled “Authentication”\n173: Netlify uses OAuth2 for authenticationauthenticationThe process of verifying who someone is before letting them access your site.×. All requests must use HTTPSHTTPSThe secure protocol, enabled by SSL, that encrypts data transferred between a browser and your site.×.\n174: To generate a personal access tokenpersonal access tokenA credential tied to your personal account that lets tools and scripts act on your behalf without your password.× (PATPATShort for personal access token, a credential tied to your personal account.×):\n175: Go to Applications > Personal access tokens .\n176: Select New access token.\n177: Enter a descriptive name to help you remember what the token will be used for.\n178: Select Allow access to my SAML-based Netlify team to authorize access to your SAML-based team data through the API.\n179: Select an Expiration date for your token to help keep your information secure.\n180: Select Generate token.\n181: Copy the token to your clipboard and store it in a safe location. Once you navigate away from this page, you won’t be able to access the value again.\n182: Select Done.\n183: Use your PAT for manual authentication in shell scripts or commands that use the Netlify API.\n184: To authenticate API requests, include the token in the authorization header:\n185: Authorization: Bearer <YOUR_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN>\n186: To authenticate in shell scripts, refer to the code sample above for an example of how to use this token in a curl request.\n187: Password resets invalidate tokens\n188: If you reset your Netlify password, all personal access tokens and OAuth tokens created before the reset will be permanently invalidated. After a password reset, you must generate new tokens and update any services or scripts that rely on them.\n189: SAML SSO\n190: If your team requires you to log in with single sign-on (SSO), your personal access tokens will be denied access to the team by default. You can choose to grant access to the team when you generate a new token. You must be logged in to the team with SSO to grant access to it.\n191: If you’re making a public integration with Netlify for others to enjoy, you must use OAuth2. This allows users to authorize your application to use Netlify on their behalf without having to copy/paste API tokens or touch sensitive login info. You’ll need an application client key and a client secretsecretSensitive credentials, like passwords and API keys, that must be kept private.× to integrate with the Netlify API. You can register a new application in your Netlify user settings for OAuth applications. Visit our blog post on integrating with Netlify for more information including common grant types and an example project.\n192: The OAuth2 end-user authorization endpoint is https://app.netlify.com/authorize.\n193: Rate limiting\n194: Section titled “Rate limiting”\n195: To protect Netlify from getting flooded by automated deploys or misbehaving applications, the Netlify API is rate limited.\n196: You can make up to 500 requests per minute for most requests.\n197: Certain operations have their own stricter limits. For example, you can deploy through the Netlify API up to 3 times per minute and up to 100 times per day.\n198: You can check the returned HTTP headers of any API request to verify your current rate limit status:\n199: X-RateLimit-Limit: 500\n200: X-RateLimit-Remaining: 56\n201: X-RateLimit-Reset: 1372700873\n202: If you need higher limits, please contact us.\n203: Pagination\n204: Section titled “Pagination”\n205: All API requests that return over 100 items are paginated by default, with a limit of 100 items per page. You can specify further pages with the ?page parameter. You can also set a custom page size that’s less than 100 with the ?per_page parameter.\n206: Note that page numbering starts with 1 and that omitting the ?page parameter will return the first page.\n207: Link header\n208: Section titled “Link header”\n209: The pagination info is included in the Link header.\n210: Link: <https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites?page=3&per_page=20>; rel=\"next\",\n211: <https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites?page=5&per_page=20>; rel=\"last\"\n212: Linebreak is included for readability.\n213: The possible rel values are:\n214: next\n215: Shows the URL of the immediate next page of results.\n216: last\n217: Shows the URL of the last page of results.\n218: prev\n219: Shows the URL of the immediate previous page of results.\n220: Deploy with the API\n221: Section titled “Deploy with the API”\n222: The most common API action is doing deploys, either of a new site or an existing site. If builds are stopped for an existing site, you can still deploy with the API to update the site.\n223: Netlify supports two ways of doing deploys:\n224: Sending a digest of all files in your deploy, and then uploading any files Netlify doesn’t already have on its storage servers.\n225: Sending a ZIP file of the entire site and letting Netlify unzip and deploy.\n226: If you’re looking to deploy sites at scale, such as building an AI code agent and looking for a deploy target, check out the APIs for code agents docs.\n227: Whether you deploy a brand new site or create a deploy within an existing site, the process is similar.\n228: First create a new site, if needed:\n229: POST /api/v1/sites\n230: Now you have a site ID and you can create a new deploy, either with a file digest or a ZIP file.\n231: File digest method\n232: Section titled “File digest method”\n233: We recommend using a digest including a file path and SHA1 for each item. This method also allows you to upload serverless functions, however serverless functions should use SHA256 instead.\n234: POST /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys\n235: {\n236: \"files\": {\n237: \"/index.html\": \"907d14fb3af2b0d4f18c2d46abe8aedce17367bd\",\n238: \"/main.css\": \"f18c2d7367bd9046abe8aedce17d14fb3af2b0d4\"\n239: },\n240: \"functions\": {\n241: \"hello-world\": \"708b029d8aa9c8fa513d1a25b97ffb6efb12b423\"\n242: }\n243: }\n244: When using a file digest, the API will return an object which includes the following properties:\n245: {\n246: \"id\": \"1234\",\n247: \"required\": [\"907d14fb3af2b0d4f18c2d46abe8aedce17367bd\"],\n248: \"required_functions\": [\"708b029d8aa9c8fa513d1a25b97ffb6efb12b423\"]\n249: }\n250: The required property will give you a list of files by SHA1 that you need to upload. Similarly, required_functions will get you an array of required functions by SHA256 to upload, if you included a functions digest when creating the deploy.\n251: Tip\n252: If you have two files with the same SHA1, you don’t have to upload both of them.\n253: Now upload the files, using the deploy ID returned as id in the file digest response:\n254: PUT /api/v1/deploys/{deploy_id}/files/index.html\n255: Warning\n256: Be sure to escape the file_path parameter, and ensure file paths don’t include # or ? characters.\n257: Use Content-Type: application/octet-stream and use the file contents as the HTTP request body.\n258: If the required file is a function, upload it to the functions endpoint, again using the deploy ID returned as id in the file digest response:\n259: PUT /api/v1/deploys/{deploy_id}/functions/hello-world?runtime=js\n260: Possible runtime parameters are:\n261: js: zipped Node.js programs or bundled JavaScript files\n262: go: Go binaries\n263: When uploading functions, use the name of the function, not the file path or any file extensions. Clients must zip the function prior to uploading to the API.\n264: Once all files have been uploaded, Netlify will post process the deploy and invalidate the CDNCDNA network of servers around the world that delivers your site’s files from the location closest to each visitor.×.\n265: Async requests for large deploys\n266: Section titled “Async requests for large deploys”\n267: API requests that last longer than 30 seconds will be terminated automatically. When creating large deploys, pass the async property in your file digest:\n268: {\n269: \"async\": true,\n270: \"files\": {\n271: \"/index.html\": \"907d14fb3af2b0d4f18c2d46abe8aedce17367bd\"\n272: },\n273: \"functions\": {\n274: \"hello-world\": \"708b029d8aa9c8fa513d1a25b97ffb6efb12b423\"\n275: }\n276: }\n277: The request will then return the deploy ID (as id) which can be polled to determine when the deploy is ready for file uploads.\n278: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys/{deploy_id}\n279: You can check the state parameter in the response. It will be set to preparing as the upload manifest is generated, and either prepared, uploading, uploaded, or ready depending on the contents of the deploy. At this point, the deploy is either ready, or the API will give you a list of required files and required_functions.\n280: Additionally, when uploading large files, sometimes the request will time out. It is safe to retry these uploads a few times to verify whether additional attempts are successful.\n281: ZIP file method\n282: Section titled “ZIP file method”\n283: You can deploy using a ZIP file but note there’s a limit of 25,000 files per zip extraction for a site. For the same site, you always need to upload a single zip with all the files.\n284: To deploy using a ZIP file, create a new deploy with Content-Type: application/zip and the ZIP file as the HTTP request body:\n285: POST /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys\n286: A deploy from a ZIP file will enter post-processing mode straight after being created.\n287: While we generally recommend using file digests, you can use the ZIP file method straight from the command line with cURL:\n288: Terminal window\n289: curl -H \"Content-Type: application/zip\" \\\n290: -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\\n291: --data-binary \"@website.zip\" \\\n292: https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites/mysite.netlify.app/deploys\n293: Create and deploy at once\n294: Section titled “Create and deploy at once”\n295: When creating a new site, you can include a file digest or a ZIP file straight away, to save an HTTP request.\n296: The following will create a new site and deploy it from a ZIP file:\n297: Terminal window\n298: curl -H \"Content-Type: application/zip\" \\\n299: -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_OAUTH2_ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\\n300: --data-binary \"@website.zip\" \\\n301: https://api.netlify.com/api/v1/sites\n302: Poll for deploy state\n303: Section titled “Poll for deploy state”\n304: You can poll the deploy to check the state:\n305: GET /api/v1/deploys/{deploy_id}\n306: { \"id\": \"1234\", \"state\": \"ready\" }\n307: Once the state changes to ready, the deploy is live.\n308: Draft deploys\n309: Section titled “Draft deploys”\n310: When creating a new deploy, you can set \"draft\": true to mark the deploy as a draft deploy.\n311: A draft deploy works like a normal deploy, but it won’t change the current published deploy of the site when it’s done processing.\n312: Commonly used endpoints\n313: Section titled “Commonly used endpoints”\n314: This section describes usage for some popular endpoints.\n315: We also have an OpenAPI reference for the Netlify API that you can explore.\n316: Check out our Support Guide on understanding and using Netlify’s API.\n317: Sites\n318: Section titled “Sites”\n319: The /sites endpoint allows you to access sites deployed on Netlify.\n320: Trying to manage your site’s environment variables?\n321: To update or retrieve your site’s environment variablesenvironment variablesNamed values stored outside your code that your site can read at build time or runtime.×, leverage the environment variables endpoints. The /sites endpoint does not support environment variables.\n322: Get sites\n323: Section titled “Get sites”\n324: GET /api/v1/sites returns all sites you have access to.\n325: [\n326: {\n327: \"id\": \"3970e0fe-8564-4903-9a55-c5f8de49fb8b\",\n328: \"premium\": false,\n329: \"claimed\": true,\n330: \"name\": \"synergy\",\n331: \"custom_domain\": \"www.example.com\",\n332: \"url\": \"http://www.example.com\",\n333: \"admin_url\": \"https://api.netlify.com/sites/synergy\",\n334: \"screenshot_url\": null,\n335: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:08Z\",\n336: \"updated_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:19Z\",\n337: \"user_id\": \"51f60d2d5803545326000005\"\n338: }\n339: ]\n340: Get site\n341: Section titled “Get site”\n342: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id} returns the specified site.\n343: About site IDs\n344: You can find a value for {site_id} by visiting the Netlify UI at Project configuration > General > Project details > Project information and copying Project ID, which is the UI label for site_id. The same value is returned as id when you create a site or get a list of sites, and it’s the value the NETLIFY_SITE_ID environment variable expects.\n345: Whenever the API requires a {site_id}, you can either use the id of a site obtained through the API, or the domaindomainThe web address people use to visit your site, such as yoursite.com.× of the site (for example, mysite.netlify.app or www.example.com). These two are interchangeable whenever they’re used in API paths.\n346: GET /api/v1/sites/3970e0fe-8564-4903-9a55-c5f8de49fb8b returns the site with a matching id.\n347: GET /api/v1/sites/www.example.com returns the site matching the domain www.example.com.\n348: {\n349: \"id\": \"3970e0fe-8564-4903-9a55-c5f8de49fb8b\",\n350: \"premium\": false,\n351: \"claimed\": true,\n352: \"name\": \"synergy\",\n353: \"custom_domain\": \"www.example.com\",\n354: \"notification_email\": \"me@example.com\",\n355: \"url\": \"http://www.example.com\",\n356: \"admin_url\": \"https://api.netlify.com/sites/synergy\",\n357: \"screenshot_url\": null,\n358: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:08Z\",\n359: \"updated_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:19Z\",\n360: \"user_id\": \"51f60d2d5803545326000005\"\n361: }\n362: Create site\n363: Section titled “Create site”\n364: POST /api/v1/sites creates a new site. By default the site will be created in your personal team.\n365: When creating a site, you can set the following properties:\n366: name: the name of the site (mysite.netlify.app)\n367: custom_domain: the custom domain of the site (www.example.com)\n368: password: password protect the site\n369: force_ssl: will force SSL on the site if SSL is enabled\n370: processing_settings: sets the Pretty URLs post processing setting:\n371: {\n372: \"html\": { \"pretty_urls\": true }\n373: }\n374: repo: configures continuous deployment. It’s a bit complicated to create a repo object so please check out our Support Guide on linking a repository using the API.\n375: Create site in team\n376: Section titled “Create site in team”\n377: POST /api/v1/{account_slug}/sites/ creates a new site in a specific team. It takes the same parameters as when creating a site.\n378: Update site\n379: Section titled “Update site”\n380: PATCH /api/v1/sites/{site_id} updates some attributes on a site.\n381: PUT /api/v1/sites/{site_id} updates some attributes on a site.\n382: This lets you update a site. It takes all the same parameters as when creating a site.\n383: If you do a PUT request to a site with Content-Type: application/zip and a zipped website in the HTTP request body, it works exactly like creating a new deploy for the site based on a ZIP file.\n384: Provision SSL for a site\n385: Section titled “Provision SSL for a site”\n386: POST /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/ssl activates SSL for a site.\n387: The site must have a custom domain with DNS records configured to point to Netlify’s infrastructure.\n388: Any domain aliases with valid DNS records will also be included in the SSL certificate for the site.\n389: This endpoint manually triggers SSL provisioning for a site’s custom domains. Once SSL provisioning is successful, the domain will be served over HTTPS.\n390: Delete site\n391: Section titled “Delete site”\n392: DELETE /api/v1/sites/{site_id} permanently deletes a site.\n393: This will return 200 OK.\n394: Site metadata\n395: Section titled “Site metadata”\n396: Each site has a metadata object. The properties of the metadata object can be used within the snippets for a site by using the Liquid template syntax.\n397: Get metadata\n398: Section titled “Get metadata”\n399: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/metadata gets the metadata for a site.\n400: {\n401: \"my_meta_key\": \"my_meta_value\"\n402: }\n403: Update metadata\n404: Section titled “Update metadata”\n405: PUT /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/metadata replaces the metadata object with a new metadata object.\n406: Environment variables\n407: Section titled “Environment variables”\n408: The environment variables API endpoints allow you to access and set both site and shared environment variables. Environment variable changes require a build and deploy to take effect.\n409: Get environment variables\n410: Section titled “Get environment variables”\n411: GET /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env returns all environment variables for a team or site. The list will only include shared environment variables if the request is made by a Team Owner.\n412: GET /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env/{key} returns an individual environment variable for a team or site.\n413: About account IDs\n414: You can find a value for {account_id} by querying GET /api/v1/accounts/{account_slug}. The slug is available in the Netlify UI at Team settings > General > Team details > Team information . An account in the Netlify REST API is equivalent to a team in the UI.\n415: Whenever the API requires an {account_id}, you can substitute {account_slug}. These two are interchangeable whenever they’re used in API paths.\n416: Create and update environment variables\n417: Section titled “Create and update environment variables”\n418: POST /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env creates site or shared environment variables with the specified scopes and contextual values. An environment variable’s contextual values are the different values set for use in each deploy context.\n419: PUT /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env/{key} updates an existing environment variable by replacing all of its values with the values provided with this request.\n420: PATCH /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env/{key} updates or creates a new value for an existing environment variable.\n421: Delete environment variables\n422: Section titled “Delete environment variables”\n423: DELETE /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env/{key} deletes an environment variable and all of its values.\n424: DELETE /api/v1/accounts/{account_id}/env/{key}/value/{id} deletes a specific environment variable value.\n425: Files\n426: Section titled “Files”\n427: All files deployed by Netlify can be read through the API. Where the public URL of a file will serve the processed version for HTML pages, the files accessed through the API are the original uploaded files.\n428: Netlify is based on a concept of atomic deploys. This means you never work on individual files. If you want to change a file, you do a new deploy with a new version of the site. To delete a file, you create a new deploy without the file. The file digest based deployment method means that these operations are fast and low-cost. Atomic deploys guarantees that your site is never in an inconsistent state where some files are being uploaded and where users might get HTML files that are not in sync with the CSS, image files, etc.\n429: Get files\n430: Section titled “Get files”\n431: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/files returns a list of all the files in the current deploy.\n432: [\n433: {\n434: \"id\": \"/index.html\",\n435: \"path\": \"/index.html\",\n436: \"sha\": \"20828dcdf2cd07e5980fe52759101591bf5014ab\",\n437: \"mime_type\": \"text/html\",\n438: \"size\": 27232\n439: }\n440: ]\n441: Get file\n442: Section titled “Get file”\n443: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/files/{file_path} returns the file.\n444: {\n445: \"id\": \"/index.html\",\n446: \"path\": \"/index.html\",\n447: \"sha\": \"20828dcdf2cd07e5980fe52759101591bf5014ab\",\n448: \"mime_type\": \"text/html\",\n449: \"size\": 27232\n450: }\n451: You can get the raw contents of the file by using the custom media type application/vnd.bitballoon.v1.raw as the Content-Type of your HTTP request.\n452: Deploys\n453: Section titled “Deploys”\n454: You can access all deploys for a specific site.\n455: Get deploys\n456: Section titled “Get deploys”\n457: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys returns a list of all deploys for a site.\n458: [\n459: {\n460: \"id\": \"52465f435803544542000001\",\n461: \"premium\": false,\n462: \"claimed\": true,\n463: \"name\": \"synergy\",\n464: \"custom_domain\": \"www.example.com\",\n465: \"notification_email\": \"me@example.com\",\n466: \"url\": \"http://www.example.com\",\n467: \"deploy_url\": \"http://52465f435803544542000001.some-site.netlify.app\",\n468: \"admin_url\": \"https://api.netlify.com/sites/synergy\",\n469: \"screenshot_url\": null,\n470: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:08Z\",\n471: \"updated_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:19Z\",\n472: \"user_id\": \"51f60d2d5803545326000005\",\n473: \"state\": \"old\"\n474: }\n475: ]\n476: Get deploy\n477: Section titled “Get deploy”\n478: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys/{deploy_id} returns a specific deploy.\n479: {\n480: \"id\": \"52465f435803544542000001\",\n481: \"premium\": false,\n482: \"claimed\": true,\n483: \"name\": \"synergy\",\n484: \"custom_domain\": \"www.example.com\",\n485: \"notification_email\": \"me@example.com\",\n486: \"url\": \"http://www.example.com\",\n487: \"deploy_url\": \"http://52465f435803544542000001.some-site.netlify.app\",\n488: \"admin_url\": \"https://api.netlify.com/sites/synergy\",\n489: \"screenshot_url\": null,\n490: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:08Z\",\n491: \"updated_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:19Z\",\n492: \"user_id\": \"51f60d2d5803545326000005\",\n493: \"state\": \"old\"\n494: }\n495: Restore deploy (rollback)\n496: Section titled “Restore deploy (rollback)”\n497: POST /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/deploys/{deploy_id}/restore restores an old deploy and makes it the live version of the site.\n498: {\n499: \"id\": \"52465f435803544542000001\",\n500: \"premium\": false,\n501: \"claimed\": true,\n502: \"name\": \"synergy\",\n503: \"custom_domain\": \"www.example.com\",\n504: \"notification_email\": \"me@example.com\",\n505: \"url\": \"http://www.example.com\",\n506: \"deploy_url\": \"http://52465f435803544542000001.some-site.netlify.app\",\n507: \"admin_url\": \"https://api.netlify.com/sites/synergy\",\n508: \"screenshot_url\": null,\n509: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:08Z\",\n510: \"updated_at\": \"2013-09-17T05:13:19Z\",\n511: \"user_id\": \"51f60d2d5803545326000005\",\n512: \"state\": \"current\"\n513: }\n514: Snippets\n515: Section titled “Snippets”\n516: Snippets are code snippets that are injected into every HTML page of the website, either right before the closing head tag or right before the closing body tag.\n517: Each snippet can specify code for all pages and code that gets injected into “Thank you” pages shown after a successful form submission.\n518: Get snippets\n519: Section titled “Get snippets”\n520: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/snippets gets a list of snippets specific to a site.\n521: [\n522: {\n523: \"id\": 0,\n524: \"title\": \"Test\",\n525: \"general\": \"\\u003Cscript\\u003Ealert(\\\"Hello\\\")\\u003C/script\\u003E\",\n526: \"general_position\": \"head\",\n527: \"goal\": \"\",\n528: \"goal_position\": \"footer\"\n529: }\n530: ]\n531: The general property is the code that will be injected right before either the head or body end tag. The general_position can be head or footer and determines whether to inject the code in the head element or before the closing body tag.\n532: The goal property is the code that will be injected into the “Thank you” page after a form submission. goal_position determines where to inject this code.\n533: Get snippet\n534: Section titled “Get snippet”\n535: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/snippets/{snippet_id} gets a specific snippet.\n536: {\n537: \"id\": 0,\n538: \"title\": \"Test\",\n539: \"general\": \"\\u003Cscript\\u003Ealert(\\\"Hello\\\")\\u003C/script\\u003E\",\n540: \"general_position\": \"head\",\n541: \"goal\": \"\",\n542: \"goal_position\": \"footer\"\n543: }\n544: Add snippet\n545: Section titled “Add snippet”\n546: POST /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/snippets adds a new snippet to a site.\n547: Update snippet\n548: Section titled “Update snippet”\n549: PUT /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/snippets/{snippet_id} replaces a snippet.\n550: Delete snippet\n551: Section titled “Delete snippet”\n552: DELETE /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/snippets/{snippet_id} deletes a snippet.\n553: Forms\n554: Section titled “Forms”\n555: You can access all Netlify Forms metadata and submissions for a site.\n556: Get forms\n557: Section titled “Get forms”\n558: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/forms returns a list of all forms for a site, including metadata about each form, but not including form submissions.\n559: [\n560: {\n561: \"id\": \"ac0865cc46440b1e64666f520e8d88d670c8a2f6\",\n562: \"site_id\": \"0d3a9d2f-ef94-4380-93df-27ee400e2048\",\n563: \"name\": \"Landing Page\",\n564: \"paths\": [\"/index\"],\n565: \"submission_count\": 3,\n566: \"fields\": [\n567: { \"name\": \"name\", \"type\": \"text\" },\n568: { \"name\": \"email\", \"type\": \"email\" },\n569: { \"name\": \"phone\", \"type\": \"text\" },\n570: { \"name\": \"company\", \"type\": \"text\" },\n571: { \"name\": \"website\", \"type\": \"url\" },\n572: { \"name\": \"number_of_employees\", \"type\": \"select\" }\n573: ],\n574: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-18T20:26:19Z\"\n575: }\n576: ]\n577: Get verified submissions\n578: Section titled “Get verified submissions”\n579: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/submissions returns a list of verified form submissions across all forms for a specific site.\n580: GET /api/v1/forms/{form_id}/submissions returns a list of verified form submissions for a specific form.\n581: [\n582: {\n583: \"id\": \"5231110b5803540aeb000019\",\n584: \"number\": 13,\n585: \"title\": null,\n586: \"email\": \"test@example.com\",\n587: \"name\": \"Mathias Biilmann\",\n588: \"first_name\": \"Mathias\",\n589: \"last_name\": \"Biilmann\",\n590: \"company\": \"Netlify\",\n591: \"summary\": \"Hello, World\",\n592: \"body\": \"Hello, World\",\n593: \"data\": {\n594: \"email\": \"test@example.com\",\n595: \"name\": \"Mathias Biilmann\",\n596: \"ip\": \"127.0.0.1\"\n597: },\n598: \"created_at\": \"2013-09-12T00:55:39Z\",\n599: \"site_url\": \"http://synergy.netlify.app\"\n600: }\n601: ]\n602: Get spam submissions\n603: Section titled “Get spam submissions”\n604: To get spam submissions, add a state=spam query parameter to the URL:\n605: GET /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/submissions?state=spam returns a list of spam form submissions across all forms for a specific site.\n606: GET /api/v1/forms/{form_id}/submissions?state=spam returns a list of spam form submissions for a specific form.\n607: Change submission state\n608: Section titled “Change submission state”\n609: You can change the state of a submission from spam to verified or vice versa.\n610: PUT /api/v1/submissions/{submission_id}/spam marks the submission as spam.\n611: PUT /api/v1/submissions/{submission_id}/ham marks the submission as verified.\n612: Delete submissions\n613: Section titled “Delete submissions”\n614: DELETE /api/v1/submissions/{submission_id} removes a form submission.\n615: Delete form\n616: Section titled “Delete form”\n617: DELETE /api/v1/sites/{site_id}/forms/{form_id} removes a form and any existing submissions to it.\n618: Future submissions to the form will result in a 404 error, and previous submissions will no longer be available.\n619: Hooks\n620: Section titled “Hooks”\n621: Netlify can trigger webhooks, send email notifications, or send Slack messages on certain events.\n622: The /hooks endpoint lets you control the hooks for your site.\n623: Get hook types\n624: Section titled “Get hook types”\n625: GET /api/v1/hooks/types returns a list of types of hooks that you can configure on Netlify.\n626: [\n627: {\n628: \"name\": \"url\",\n629: \"fields\": [\n630: {\n631: \"name\": \"url\",\n632: \"options\": {\n633: \"type\": \"string\",\n634: \"title\": \"URL to notify\"\n635: }\n636: }\n637: ],\n638: \"events\": [\"submission_created\", \"deploy_created\", \"deploy_failed\"]\n639: }\n640: ]\n641: Each type has a series of fields that you need to set to create a new hook, and a list of events that can trigger them.\n642: Get hooks for a site\n643: Section titled “Get hooks for a site”\n644: GET /api/v1/hooks?site_id={site_id} returns a list of a hooks defined for a specific site.\n645: [\n646: {\n647: \"id\": \"5636b7a00d61eec2d6001004\",\n648: \"site_id\": \"0d3a9d2f-ef94-4380-93df-27ee400e2048\",\n649: \"type\": \"email\",\n650: \"event\": \"submission_created\",\n651: \"data\": { \"email\": \"test@example.com\" },\n652: \"created_at\": \"2015-10-20T21:51:51Z\",\n653: \"updated_at\": \"2015-10-20T21:51:51Z\"\n654: }\n655: ]\n656: Create hook\n657: Section titled “Create hook”\n658: POST /api/v1/hooks creates a new hook.\n659: An example request body for an email hook for a specific form in your site would be formatted like this:\n660: {\n661: \"site_id\": \"0d3a9d2f-ef94-4380-93df-27ee400e2048\",\n662: \"form_id\": \"5235a7a00d61eec2d6001302\",\n663: \"type\": \"email\",\n664: \"event\": \"submission_created\",\n665: \"data\": { \"email\": \"test@example.com\" }\n666: }\n667: form_id is optional and links the hook to a specific form within your site. You can also use form_name with the value of the name attribute of the form of your site as an alternative to form_id.\n668: Delete hook\n669: Section titled “Delete hook”\n670: DELETE /api/v1/hooks/{hook_id} removes a hook permanently.\n671: Note, for outgoing webhooks, returning a 410 Gone status code from the URL endpoint will trigger a deletion of the hook.\n672: Last updated: Oct 3, 2026\n673: Previous Overview Next Deploy project from a zip file with API\n674: Did you find this doc useful?\n675: Your feedback helps us improve our docs.\n676: Yes\n677: No\n678: Do not fill in this field\n679: Email (optional)\n680: What else would you like to tell us about this doc?\n681: I consent to being contacted regarding my feedback\n682: Send\n683: Netlify\n684: Careers\n685: Blog\n686: Terms\n687: Privacy\n688: Reading these docs with an AI agent? Append .md to any docs URL for its Markdown source, or start from the\n689: full llms.txt index.\n690: © 2026 Netlify\n691: Ask Netlify"
    },
