      "numbered_text": "1: Create API token · Cloudflare Fundamentals docsSkip to contentDocumentation Index\n2: Fetch the complete documentation index at: https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/llms.txt\n3: Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\n4: DocsDirectoryAPISDKsChangelog\n5: SearchCtrlK\n6: Log inDashboard\n7: Home\n8: /Cloudflare Fundamentals\n9: /…\n10: Cloudflare's API\n11: /Get started\n12: /Create API token\n13: Create API token\n14: Last updated Nov 6, 2026|Copy as Markdown|View as Markdown|Agent setup\n15: Prerequisite\n16: Before you begin, find your zone and account IDs.\n17: Determine if you want a user token or an Account API token. Use Account API tokens if you prefer service tokens that are not associated with users and your desired API endpoints are compatible.\n18: From the Cloudflare dashboard ↗︎, go to My Profile > API Tokens for user tokens. For Account Tokens, go to Manage Account > API Tokens.\n19: Select Create Token.\n20: Add a token name to describe why or how the token is used.\n21: Select a template from the available API token templates or start from scratch. Templates include policies with predefined permissions. The following example uses the Edit zone DNS template.\n22: Create a policy to organize the token's permissions. Select a policy type (Account, User, or Zone), then select resource rules within the policy to specify which resources the permissions apply to, such as specific accounts or zones.\n23: Choose the permissions to grant within the policy. Most permissions offer Edit or Read access. Edit is full CRUDL (create, read, update, delete, list) access, while Read is the read permission and list where appropriate. For example, Zone DNS Read for example.com allows the token to read DNS records only for that zone. Refer to the available token permissions for more information.\n24: (Optional) Restrict how a token is used in the Token expiration and Client IP Address Filtering fields.\n25: Select Continue to summary.\n26: Review the token summary. Select Back to make adjustments. You can also edit a token after creation.\n27: Select Create Token to generate the token's secret.\n28: Copy the secret to a secure place.\n29: Warning\n30: The token secret is only shown once. Do not store the secret in plaintext where others can access it. Anyone with this token can perform the authorized actions against the resources that the token has access to.\n31: The token secret page also includes an example command to test the token. Use the /user/tokens/verify endpoint to fetch the current status of the given token.\n32: curl \"https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/user/tokens/verify\" \\\n33: --header \"Authorization: Bearer <API_TOKEN>\"\n34: The result:\n35: {\n36: \"result\": {\n37: \"id\": \"100bf38cc8393103870917dd535e0628\",\n38: \"status\": \"active\"\n39: },\n40: \"success\": true,\n41: \"errors\": [],\n42: \"messages\": [\n43: {\n44: \"code\": 10000,\n45: \"message\": \"This API Token is valid and active\",\n46: \"type\": null\n47: }\n48: ]\n49: }\n50: New API tokens use the cfut_ prefixed scannable format, which allows credential scanning tools to detect leaked tokens.\n51: With this you have successfully created an API token and can start working with the Cloudflare API. After creating your first API token, you can create additional API tokens via the API.\n52: PreviousUnder a DDoS attack?NextGet Global API key (legacy)\n53: Was this helpful?\n54: YesNo\n55: Edit pageReport issue\n56: On this page\n57: Overview\n58: Edit pageReport issue\n59: Cloudflare Fundamentals\n60: /\n61: Overview\n62: Concepts\n63: How Cloudflare DNS works\n64: Traffic flow through Cloudflare\n65: Accounts, zones, and profiles\n66: Cloudflare IP addresses\n67: Get started\n68: Accounts\n69: Create account\n70: Account security\n71: Add abuse contact\n72: Allow Cloudflare access\n73: Leaked Password Notifications\n74: Manage active sessions\n75: Review audit logs - v1\n76: Aud