      "numbered_text": "1: API keys | Supabase Docs\n2: Skip to content\n3: DOCS\n4: Start\n5: Products\n6: Build\n7: Manage\n8: Reference\n9: Resources\n10: DOCS\n11: Search\n12: CtrlK Ask AI Main menu\n13: Open menu\n14: Main menu\n15: StartProductsBuildManageReferenceResources\n16: Start\n17: Overview\n18: Start with Supabase\n19: Build with AI tools\n20: The Supabase way to build\n21: API Keys\n22: Local Development\n23: Architecture\n24: Migrating to new API keys\n25: Framework QuickstartsFramework Quickstarts\n26: React\n27: Next.js\n28: TanStack Start\n29: Astro\n30: Vue\n31: Nuxt\n32: SvelteKit\n33: SolidJS\n34: RedwoodJS\n35: Refine\n36: Hono\n37: iOS SwiftUI\n38: Android Kotlin\n39: Expo React Native\n40: Flutter\n41: Unity\n42: Flask (Python)\n43: Reflex (Python)\n44: Laravel PHP\n45: Ruby on Rails\n46: Spring Boot\n47: Web app demosWeb app demos\n48: Next.js\n49: React\n50: Vue 3\n51: Nuxt 3\n52: Angular\n53: RedwoodJS\n54: SolidJS\n55: Svelte\n56: SvelteKit\n57: Refine\n58: Mobile tutorialsMobile tutorials\n59: Flutter\n60: Expo React Native\n61: Android Kotlin\n62: Ionic React\n63: Ionic Vue\n64: Ionic Angular\n65: Swift\n66: DOCS\n67: Start\n68: Products\n69: Build\n70: Manage\n71: Reference\n72: Resources\n73: DOCS\n74: Search\n75: CtrlK Ask AI Main menu\n76: Open menu\n77: Updated Nov 18, 2026\n78: API keys\n79: Every request to your Supabase project carries an API key. The key identifies what is calling your project: a web page, a mobile app, a server, or an Edge Function. Supabase Auth identifies who is calling, when someone is signed in.\n80: This guide covers:\n81: Which key do you use? answers that in one table.\n82: How API keys work explains what each key type is and which Postgres role it maps to.\n83: Find and use your keys covers the ways to retrieve a key, wiring it into your code, and rotating one that leaked.\n84: Security reference lists what publishable keys don't protect against, how to handle secret keys, and the known limitations.\n85: Which key do you use?\n86: Pick the key by asking where the code runs.\n87: Where the code runsUse this keyBecause\n88: Anything you ship: browser, mobile app, CLI, scriptPublishable keyAnyone can read it, so it only reaches what Row Level Security allows\n89: Anything you control: server, Edge Function, cron jobSecret keyIt bypasses Row Level Security, so it must never leave your control\n90: Think of your project's data as a building. The publishable key is taped to the front door, and it only opens the lobby. The secret key is the master key, and it stays in your pocket.\n91: After you know which key you need, see Find and use your keys to get its value and wire it in. To swap keys in an application that already ships legacy keys, follow Migrating to new API keys instead.\n92: Changes to API keys\n93: Supabase is deprecating the anon and service_role keys by the end of 2026. Use the publishable (sb_publishable_xxx) and secret (sb_secret_xxx) keys instead. For the reasoning behind the change, see the announcement on GitHub.\n94: In most cases you can get keys from your project's Connect dialog. For every way to retrieve a key, including the CLI and the Management API, refer to Find your keys.\n95: How API keys work\n96: What an API key identifies\n97: An API key authenticates an application component to give it access to Supabase services. An application component might be a web page, a mobile app, or a server. An API key doesn't distinguish between users but between applications.\n98: API keys provide the first layer of authentication for data access. Supabase Auth builds on top of that. This table covers the difference:\n99: ResponsibilityQuestionAnswer\n100: API keysWhat is accessing the project?A web page, a mobile app, a server, or an Edge Function\n101: Supabase AuthWho is accessing the project?An individual signed-in user\n102: Key types\n103: Supabase supports four types of API keys:\n104: TypeFormatPrivilegesAvailabilityUse\n105: Publishable keysb_publishable_...LowPlatform, CLISafe to expose online: web page, mobile or desktop app, GitHub actions, CLIs, source code.\n106: Secret keyssb_secret_...ElevatedPlatform, CLIOnly use in backend components of your app, such as servers, APIs with their own authorization checks, Edge Functions, and microservices. They provide full access to your project's data, bypassing Row Level Security.\n107: anonJWT (long-lived)LowPlatform, CLILegacy version of publishable keys.\n108: service_roleJWT (long-lived)ElevatedPlatform, CLILegacy version of secret keys.\n109: Publishable and secret keys are short strings, not JWTs. If a tool, tutorial, or AI assistant tells you to copy a long key that begins with eyJ, it was written for the legacy keys.\n110: Running supabase start prints a publishable key and a secret key for your local project. The local secret key takes the place of the local service_role key. See the CLI getting started guide for the full output.\n111: Legacy anon and service_role keys\n112: Creating publishable and secret keys doesn't revoke your legacy keys. Both key systems work at the same time.\n113: Creating a publishable or secret key adds it alongside your existing anon and service_role keys without affecting them, so your legacy keys keep working. They stay valid until you disable them in the Settings > API Keys section of the Dashboard, which is a separate step.\n114: See Migrating to new API keys for the full process.\n115: Publishable keys and public components\n116: Publishable keys identify the public components of your application. Public components run in environments where you can't keep a secret. These include:\n117: Web pages, where the key is bundled in source code.\n118: Mobile or desktop applications, where the key is bundled inside the compiled packages or executables.\n119: CLI, scripts, tools, or other pre-built executables.\n120: Public APIs that return the key without requiring authorization first.\n121: These environments are always considered public because anyone can retrieve the key from the source code or build artifacts.\n122: Postgres roles and Row Level Security\n123: Every key resolves to a built-in Postgres role, and that role is what your Row Level Security policies match on. Using a publishable key doesn't mean your user is anonymous: your application authenticates with the publishable key while your user authenticates separately through Supabase Auth with their own JWT.\n124: KeyUser signed in through Supabase AuthPostgres role\n125: Publishable keyNoanon\n126: Publishable keyYesauthenticated\n127: Secret keyNot applicableservice_role\n128: Write your Row Level Security policies for the anon and authenticated roles. Policies never apply to a secret key, because service_role has the BYPASSRLS attribute.\n129: Postgres evaluates table grants first, and only then applies Row Level Security. The two failures look different. A missing grant returns a permission error, including for service_role, whereas a policy that matches no rows returns an empty result. Check the grant before you debug the policy. See Securing your API.\n130: Secret keys and elevated access\n131: Unlike publishable keys, secret keys allow elevated access to your project's data. Use them only in secure, developer-controlled components of your application, such as:\n132: Servers that run their own authorization checks, such as Edge Functions, microservices, and web servers.\n133: Periodic jobs, queue processors, topic subscribers.\n134: Admin and back-office tools that run authorization checks first.\n135: Data processing pipelines, such as for analytics, reports, backups, or database synchronization.\n136: A leaked secret key exposes all of your project's data\n137: A secret key bypasses every Row Level Security policy you have. Never put one in a browser, a shipped application, or source control. For the full list of places a secret key must never go, see Handling secret keys safely.\n138: If a secret key leaks, see Rotate a leaked or compromised API key.\n139: Secret keys authorize access to your project's data through the built-in service_role Postgres role. By design, this role has full access to your project's data. It also has the BYPASSRLS attribute, so it skips every Row Level Security policy you attach.\n140: Secret keys improve on the old JWT-based service_role key, and we recommend them wherever possible. They add checks that prevent misuse:\n141: A secret key doesn't work in a browser. Supabase matches on the User-Agent header and returns HTTP 401 Unauthorized.\n142: A project doesn't need any secret keys if nothing uses them.\n143: Find and use your keys\n144: Getting a key into an app has two halves. You copy the value, which needs a signed-in Dashboard session, and your code reads it by name. The key itself never belongs in source code, so start by setting these names in .env:\n145: 1\n146: # Safe to expose to the browser. Prefix per your framework.\n147: 2\n148: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co\n149: 3\n150: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...\n151: 4\n152: 5\n153: # Server-only. Never prefix these, or your bundler will ship the key.\n154: 6\n155: SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co\n156: 7\n157: SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...\n158: Find your keys\n159: Pick the path that matches where you are working.\n160: Every path below reads keys that already exist. If your project has no publishable or secret key yet, create them first in the Settings > API Keys section of the Dashboard.\n161: DashboardSupabase CLIManagement APILocal stack\n162: Use the Dashboard when you are setting up a project by hand. It needs nothing but a signed-in session.\n163: Open your project's Connect dialog. It shows the URL and publishable key for the framework you select, ready to paste into .env.\n164: To pick a specific key, or to see every key your project has, open the Settings > API Keys section of the Dashboard instead. Every key lives there, legacy or not, and there is no separate Settings > API page.\n165: Copy each value into .env under the variable names above.\n166: Use the CLI to script setup, or to read the keys for a preview branch. This path needs the Supabase CLI, so install it first.\n167: Sign in, if you haven't already:\n168: 1\n169: supabase login\n170: Find the project ref, if you don't know it:\n171: 1\n172: supabase projects list\n173: List the keys for that project:\n174: 1\n175: supabase projects api-keys --remote=your-project-ref\n176: Pass the branch's own project ref to read the keys for a preview branch. A branch has its own keys, and the command returns the linked project's keys when you omit --remote.\n177: Use the Management API to fetch keys from your own tooling, such as a deploy script or an internal provisioning service.\n178: Authenticate with a personal access token. An OAuth application needs the secrets:read scope. A personal access token you've scoped needs the API Keys permission with Read access. To reveal key values with reveal=true, as the example below does, the token also needs API Key Secrets with Read access. Without the required scope or permissions, the request returns 403 Forbidden.\n179: 1\n180: export PROJECT_REF=\"your-project-ref\"\n181: 2\n182: export SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=\"your-personal-access-token\"\n183: 3\n184: 4\n185: curl -X GET \"https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/$PROJECT_REF/api-keys?reveal=true\" \\\n186: 5\n187: -H \"Authorization: Bearer $SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\\n188: 6\n189: | jq '.[] | {name, type}'\n190: reveal=true includes the key values in the response, and this jq filter drops them again so they stay out of your terminal. Write the values straight into your secret store rather than printing them, because anything on standard output lands in your CI logs. See Get project API keys for the full response.\n191: A local stack mints its own keys. They are unrelated to your hosted project's keys, so a local key never grants access to your hosted data.\n192: This path needs the Supabase CLI and a container runtime. See Running a local Supabase project for both.\n193: Start the stack:\n194: 1\n195: supabase start\n196: The publishable and secret keys are in the output, under Authentication Keys.\n197: Print them again at any time while the stack is running:\n198: 1\n199: supabase status\n200: Use a publishable key in client code\n201: Pass the publishable key to createClient in any code that reaches a user's device.\n202: 1\n203: import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js'\n204: 2\n205: 3\n206: export const supabase = createClient(\n207: 4\n208: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,\n209: 5\n210: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY!\n211: 6\n212: )\n213: Row Level Security decides what this client can reach, so enable it on every table before you deploy.\n214: Use a secret key in backend code\n215: Pass the secret key to createClient only in code that never reaches a user's device, such as a server route or a worker.\n216: 1\n217: import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js'\n218: 2\n219: 3\n220: export const supabaseAdmin = createClient(\n221: 4\n222: process.env.SUPABASE_URL!,\n223: 5\n224: process.env.SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY!\n225: 6\n226: )\n227: Inside an Edge Function\n228: Don't read the key from the environment inside an Edge Function. Use the @supabase/server SDK instead. It verifies the caller's secret key for you and hands back a privileged client on ctx, so the key never appears in your code.\n229: 1\n230: import { withSupabase } from 'npm:@supabase/server@1'\n231: 2\n232: 3\n233: export default {\n234: 4\n235: fetch: withSupabase({ auth: 'secret' }, async (_req, ctx) => {\n236: 5\n237: // ctx.supabaseAdmin bypasses Row Level Security\n238: 6\n239: const { data } = await ctx.supabaseAdmin.from('profiles').select('email')\n240: 7\n241: return Response.json({ data })\n242: 8\n243: }),\n244: 9\n245: }\n246: Set verify_jwt = false for a function called with a secret key rather than a user's token.\n247: supabase/config.tomlsupabase/config.jsonc\n248: 1\n249: [functions.roster]\n250: 2\n251: verify_jwt = false\n252: 1\n253: {\n254: 2\n255: \"functions\": {\n256: 3\n257: \"roster\": {\n258: 4\n259: \"verify_jwt\": false\n260: 5\n261: }\n262: 6\n263: }\n264: 7\n265: }\n266: If you build the client yourself instead of using the SDK, read the key from SUPABASE_SECRET_KEYS. The runtime injects it as a JSON dictionary keyed by key name, so pick the one you want.\n267: 1\n268: import { createClient } from 'npm:@supabase/supabase-js@2'\n269: 2\n270: 3\n271: const secretKeys = JSON.parse(Deno.env.get('SUPABASE_SECRET_KEYS')!)\n272: 4\n273: 5\n274: const supabaseAdmin = createClient(Deno.env.get('SUPABASE_URL')!, secretKeys['default'])\n275: SUPABASE_ANON_KEY and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY still exist in the runtime, but they carry the legacy keys, so reading them keeps you on the deprecated path. See Environment variables for the full list of injected variables.\n276: If you are replacing legacy keys in an existing application rather than starting fresh, follow Migrating to new API keys.\n277: Rotate a leaked or compromised key\n278: To replace a leaked secret key or service_role key and retire the compromised one, see Rotate a leaked or compromised API key.\n279: Security reference\n280: What publishable keys don't protect against\n281: Publishable keys aren't intended to protect against the following, because anyone can retrieve a key from a public component:\n282: Static or dynamic code analysis and reverse engineering attempts.\n283: Use of the Network inspector in the browser.\n284: Cross-site request forgery, cross-site scripting, phishing attacks.\n285: Man-in-the-middle attacks.\n286: When you use a publishable key, Postgres guards access to your project's data through the built-in anon and authenticated roles. For full protection, confirm that:\n287: You have enabled Row Level Security on all tables.\n288: You regularly review your Row Level Security policies for permissions granted to the anon and authenticated roles.\n289: You don't change the roles' attributes without understanding what the change does.\n290: Your project's Security Advisor checks for common security problems with the built-in Postgres roles. Review each finding carefully before you dismiss it.\n291: Handling secret keys safely\n292: Exposing a secret key puts all of your project's data at risk. The rules below are grouped by the kind of mistake they prevent.\n293: Where a secret key must never appear\n294: A web page, a public document, source code, or a bundled mobile, desktop, or CLI package.\n295: A browser, even on localhost.\n296: How a secret key must never travel\n297: Over chat, email, or SMS.\n298: In a URL or a query parameter, because those are often logged.\n299: In a request header, until you have log sanitization in place.\n300: Over plain HTTP, or to any host other than your project's Supabase endpoint.\n301: What must never be written down\n302: Don't log a key, even one that looks invalid. A typo today can reveal the real key later.\n303: If you must record a key, log no more than 6 characters from the random part after its prefix.\n304: To record which key was used, store a SHA256 hash of it instead.\n305: Storing and sharing a secret key\n306: Work with secret keys only on computers you fully own or control.\n307: Encrypt keys stored in files or environment variables.\n308: Keep keys out of source control, including CI scripts. Use the tool's own secrets storage instead.\n309: Share keys through an encrypted transfer tool, such as the one in your password manager. Better still, have the other person read the key from the Settings > API Keys section of the Dashboard.\n310: Limiting the damage of a leak\n311: Use a separate secret key for each backend component. If one component leaks its key, you only rotate that one.\n312: Rotate a leaked key as soon as you fix the cause of the leak. The browser block returns HTTP 401 Unauthorized, but an attacker can still use the key from other tools. For the steps, see Rotate a leaked or compromised API key.\n313: Handle secret keys using secure coding practices.\n314: Known limitations\n315: Publishable and secret keys aren't JWTs, which creates a few compatibility differences to plan for:\n316: Send publishable and secret keys on the apikey header, not on Authorization: Bearer. Because the keys aren't JWTs, anything that tries to verify one as a JWT fails. For migration compatibility the verify_jwt platform check accepts them on either header, but passing that check doesn't authenticate the caller. See Authorization headers.\n317: Edge Functions need to authorize API keys in code. The verify_jwt check alone doesn't authenticate a caller that sends only an API key. Use the @supabase/server SDK, as shown in Securing Edge Functions, rather than relying on the platform check.\n318: The Data API's OpenAPI root, GET /rest/v1/, accepts only secret keys. A publishable key gets a 401 there, even though it works on table routes such as /rest/v1/<table>. To check that a publishable key works, call a table route or /auth/v1/health instead.\n319: Public Realtime connections last a maximum of 24 hours, unless the connection is upgraded to user-level authentication through Supabase Auth or a supported third-party auth provider.\n320: Edit this page\n321: Is this helpful?\n322: No Yes\n323: AI tools\n324: Connect your AI agentAsk ChatGPTAsk ClaudeCopy as MarkdownPrefer on Google\n325: Need some help?\n326: Contact support\n327: Latest product updates?\n328: See Changelog\n329: Something's not right?\n330: Check system status\n331: © Supabase Inc—Do Not Sell or Share My Personal InformationContributingAuthor StyleguideOpen SourceSupaSquad\n332: Twitter\n333: GitHub\n334: Discord\n335: Youtube\n336: Start with Supabase\n337: API Keys"
