isable readonly mode temporarily\n82: Enable database webhook\n83: Generate typescript types\n84: Get a migration\n85: Get a snippet\n86: Get backup schedule\n87: Get database metadata\n88: Get database openapi\n89: Get jit access\n90: Get jit access config\n91: Get pooler config\n92: Get postgres config\n93: Get project pgbouncer config\n94: Get readonly mode status\n95: Get ssl enforcement config\n96: Invite external jit access\n97: List all backups\n98: List all snippets\n99: List jit access\n100: List migration history\n101: Patch a migration\n102: Patch postgres config\n103: Read only query\n104: Remove a read replica\n105: Restore pitr backup\n106: Rollback migrations\n107: Run a query\n108: Setup a read replica\n109: Update backup schedule\n110: Update database password\n111: Update jit access\n112: Update jit access config\n113: Update pooler config\n114: Update postgres config\n115: Update ssl enforcement config\n116: Upsert a migration\n117: Domains\n118: Activate custom hostname\n119: Activate vanity subdomain config\n120: Check vanity subdomain availability\n121: Deactivate vanity subdomain config\n122: Get hostname config\n123: Get vanity subdomain config\n124: Update hostname config\n125: Verify dns config\n126: Edge Functions\n127: Bulk update functions\n128: Create a function\n129: Delete a function\n130: Deploy a function\n131: Get a function\n132: Get a function body\n133: List all functions\n134: Update a function\n135: Environments\n136: Count action runs\n137: Create a branch\n138: Create a branch\n139: Delete a branch\n140: Diff a branch\n141: Disable preview branching\n142: Get a branch\n143: Get a branch config\n144: Get action run\n145: Get action run logs\n146: List action runs\n147: List all branches\n148: Merge a branch\n149: Push a branch\n150: Reset a branch\n151: Restore a branch\n152: Update a branch config\n153: Update action run status\n154: OAuth\n155: Authorize user\n156: Exchange oauth token\n157: Oauth authorize project claim\n158: Revoke token\n159: Organization webhooks\n160: Create endpoint\n161: Delete all endpoints\n162: Delete endpoint\n163: Get delivery\n164: Get endpoint\n165: List deliveries\n166: List endpoints\n167: Retry delivery\n168: Send test event\n169: Update endpoint\n170: Organizations\n171: Assign organization member role\n172: Create an organization\n173: Get an organization\n174: Get organization entitlements\n175: List all organizations\n176: List organization github connections\n177: List organization members\n178: List organization members\n179: List organization projects\n180: List organization roles\n181: Remove organization members\n182: Organizations Members Invitations\n183: Create organization invitations\n184: Delete organization invitations\n185: Profile\n186: Get profile\n187: Project webhooks\n188: Create endpoint\n189: Delete all endpoints\n190: Delete endpoint\n191: Get delivery\n192: Get endpoint\n193: List deliveries\n194: List endpoints\n195: Retry delivery\n196: Send test event\n197: Update endpoint\n198: Projects\n199: Cancel a project restoration\n200: Create a project\n201: Create notebook\n202: Create private link association\n203: Delete a project\n204: Delete network bans\n205: Delete notebook\n206: Delete private link association\n207: Delete private link association for database\n208: Download project backup\n209: Get all projects for organization\n210: Get available regions\n211: Get database disk\n212: Get disk utilization\n213: Get network restrictions\n214: Get notebook\n215: Get postgres upgrade eligibility\n216: Get postgres upgrade status\n217: Get project\n218: Get project config\n219: Get project disk autoscale config\n220: Get services health\n221: List all network bans\n222: List all network bans enriched\n223: List all projects\n224: List available restore versions\n225: List notebooks\n226: List private link associations\n227: Modify database disk\n228: Patch network restrictions\n229: Pause a project\n230: Preview a project transfer\n231: Restart a project\n232: Restore a project\n233: Review network restrictions\n234: Transfer a project\n235: Update a project\n236: Update network restrictions\n237: Update notebook\n238: Upgrade postgres version\n239: Realtime\n240: Get realtime config\n241: Shutdown realtime\n242: Update realtime config\n243: Rest\n244: Get postgrest service config\n245: Update postgrest service config\n246: Secrets\n247: Bulk create secrets\n248: Bulk delete secrets\n249: Create project api key\n250: Delete project api key\n251: Get pgsodium config\n252: Get project api key\n253: Get project api keys\n254: Get project legacy api keys\n255: List all secrets\n256: Update pgsodium config\n257: Update project api key\n258: Update project legacy api keys\n259: Storage\n260: Get storage config\n261: List all buckets\n262: Update storage config\n263: DOCS\n264: Start\n265: Products\n266: Build\n267: Manage\n268: Reference\n269: Resources\n270: DOCS\n271: Search\n272: CtrlK Ask AI Main menu\n273: Open menu\n274: Management API\n275: Manage your Supabase organizations and projects programmatically.\n276: Authentication\n277: All API requests require an access token to be included in the Authorization header: Authorization Bearer <access_token>.\n278: There are two ways to generate an access token:\n279: Personal access token (PAT):\n280: PATs are tokens with a custom expiry that you manually generate to access the Management API. They are useful for automating workflows or developing against the Management API. A scoped PAT can only reach the organizations, projects, and permissions you choose when creating it. A classic PAT, shown as Legacy in the dashboard, carries the same privileges as your user account. Prefer scoped PATs, and keep every token secret.\n281: To generate or manage your personal access tokens, visit your account page. See Personal access tokens for the permission each endpoint requires.\n282: OAuth2:\n283: OAuth2 allows your application to generate tokens on behalf of a Supabase user, providing secure and limited access to their account without requiring their credentials. Use this if you're building a third-party app that needs to create or manage Supabase projects on behalf of your users. Tokens generated via OAuth2 are short-lived and tied to specific scopes to ensure your app can only perform actions that are explicitly approved by the user.\n284: See Build a Supabase Integration to set up OAuth2 for your application.\n285: 1\n286: curl https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects \\\n287: 2\n288: -H \"Authorization: Bearer sbp_bdd0••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••4f23\"\n289: All API requests must be authenticated and made over HTTPS.\n290: Rate limits\n291: Rate limits are applied to prevent abuse and ensure fair usage of the Management API. Rate limits are based on a per-user, per-scope model, meaning each user gets independent rate limits for each project and organization they interact with.\n292: Standard rate limit\n293: LimitDurationScope\n294: 120 requests1 minutePer user, per project or organization, per endpoint\n295: When you exceed this rate limit, all further API calls return a 429 Too Many Requests response for the remainder of the minute. Once the time window expires, your request quota resets and you can make requests again.\n296: Rate limit scope\n297: Rate limits are applied with per-user, per-scope, per-endpoint isolation:\n298: Project scope: rate limits apply independently to each project.\n299: Organization scope: rate limits apply independently to each organization.\n300: User scope: requests that don't include a project or organization, such as GET /v1/projects and GET /v1/organizations, are counted against the user.\n301: Endpoint: within each scope, every endpoint has its own limit.\n302: For example, you can make 120 requests to Project A and 120 requests to Project B within the same minute, as they are tracked separately. Within one organization, calling GET /v1/organizations/{slug}/members 120 times doesn't affect calls to GET /v1/organizations/{slug} or POST /v1/projects for that organization.\n303: POST /v1/projects is organization-scoped, using the organization_slug in the request body.\n304: How rate limits are tracked\n305: Your requests are identified and tracked using one of the following identifiers, in this order of priority:\n306: OAuth App ID: If your request is authenticated via an OAuth application\n307: User ID: If your request is authenticated with a personal access token\n308: IP Address: If your request is unauthenticated (extracted from request headers)\n309: Each identifier is combined with the scope (project or organization) and the endpoint to create a unique tracking key. This ensures that rate limits are isolated per user and per scope, preventing one project or organization from affecting another.\n310: Who the limit applies to\n311: Personal access tokens: the limit applies to the user, so all tokens created by the same user share it.\n312: OAuth apps: the limit applies to the app, shared by everyone calling through it.\n313: Rate limit response headers\n314: Every API response includes rate limit information following official HTTP specification headers:\n315: X-RateLimit-Limit - The maximum number of requests allowed in the current time window\n316: X-RateLimit-Remaining - The number of requests remaining before you hit the rate limit\n317: X-RateLimit-Reset - The number of seconds remaining until your rate limit resets\n318: You can use these headers to monitor your usage and implement proactive rate limit handling before receiving a 429 response.\n319: Endpoint exceptions\n320: Some endpoints have stricter rate limits than the standard 120 requests per minute to prevent abuse of resource-intensive operations:\n321: EndpointLimitDurationReason\n322: GET /v1/projects/:ref/endpoints/logs.all30 requests1 minuteAnalytics log queries are computationally expensive\n323: GET /v1/projects/:ref/endpoints/usage.api-counts30 requests1 minuteAnalytics aggregation is computationally expensive\n324: GET /v1/projects/:ref/endpoints/usage.api-requests-count30 requests1 minuteAnalytics aggregation is computationally expensive\n325: GET /v1/projects/:ref/database/context10 requests1 minuteDatabase context operations are resource-intensive\n326: GET /v1/projects/:ref/database/context1 request1 secondBurst limit to prevent rapid successive requests\n327: POST /v1/projects/:ref/config/custom-hostname/initialize10 requests1 minuteThese operations are expensive\n328: POST /v1/projects/:ref/config/custom-hostname/reverify10 requests1 minuteThese operations are expensive\n329: DELETE /v1/projects/:ref/config/custom-hostname10 requests1 minuteThese operations are expensive\n330: GET /v1/projects/:ref/config/vanity-subdomain10 requests1 minuteThese operations are expensive\n331: Some endpoints have the standard 120 requests per minute but with different timeout durations:\n332: EndpointLimitDurationReason\n333: POST /v1/projects/:ref/database/migrations120 requests3 minutesDatabase migrations may require more processing time\n334: Best practices\n335: Monitor rate limit headers: Check the X-RateLimit-Remaining header to see how many requests you have left. When it approaches 0, slow down your requests to avoid hitting the limit.\n336: Implement exponential backoff: When you receive a 429 response, wait before retrying. You can use the X-RateLimit-Reset header (seconds) to determine exactly how long to wait.\n337: Batch operations: Where possible, combine multiple operations into fewer API calls to reduce your request count.\n338: Be mindful of expensive endpoints: Analytics, database context, and domain endpoints have stricter limits, so use them sparingly.\n339: The Management API is subject to our fair-use policy. All resources created via the API are subject to the pricing detailed on our Pricing pages.\n340: Additional links\n341: OpenAPI Docs\n342: OpenAPI Spec\n343: Report bugs and issues\n344: Is this helpful?\n345: No Yes\n346: Additional links\n347: OpenAPI Docs\n348: OpenAPI Spec\n349: Report bugs and issues\n350: Need some help?\n351: Contact support\n352: Latest product updates?\n353: See Changelog\n354: Something's not right?\n355: Check system status\n356: © Supabase Inc—Do Not Sell or Share My Personal InformationContributingAuthor StyleguideOpen SourceSupaSquad\n357: Twitter\n358: GitHub\n359: Discord\n360: Youtube"
