ata Fundamentals\n90: Importing CSV Data\n91: Graph Data Modeling\n92: Data Scientist Courses\n93: Get started with Graph Data Science\n94: Path Finding\n95: Generative AI Courses\n96: Neo4j & Generative AI Fundamentals\n97: Vector Indexes & Unstructured Data\n98: Constructing Knowledge Graphs\n99: Building Agents in Neo4j Aura\n100: Neo4j Certification\n101: Neo4j Certified Professional\n102: Neo4j Graph Data Science Certification\n103: Get Started Free\n104: Theme\n105: Light\n106: Dark\n107: Search\n108: Skip to content\n109: Query API\n110: Introduction\n111: Authorize requests\n112: Query the database\n113: Run transactions\n114: Route queries to read cluster members\n115: Retrieve query counters\n116: Profile queries\n117: Coordinate transactions and enforce causal consistency\n118: Run queries as a different user\n119: Stream results\n120: Reference\n121: Endpoints\n122: Headers\n123: Result formats & Data types\n124: Plain JSON\n125: Typed JSON\n126: Changelog\n127: Is this page helpful?\n128: Query API\n129: Introduction\n130: Raise an issue\n131: Introduction\n132: Introduced in 5.19\n133: The Query API is enabled by default.\n134: It was however disabled by default on self-managed instances versions < 5.25.\n135: To enable it on those deployments, add QUERY_API_ENDPOINTS to the configuration setting server.http_enabled_modules.\n136: The Query API allows to execute Cypher statements against a Neo4j server through HTTP requests.\n137: This API supersedes the deprecated HTTP API.\n138: The main use case of the API is for developing client applications in languages for which there is no supported library.\n139: If there exists an official library (driver) for the language you are using, consider using that instead — see Create applications.\n140: On self-managed instances, the API uses port 7474 for HTTP and port 7473 for HTTPS by default. Ports can be altered via the configuration settings server.http.listen_address and server.https.listen_address.\n141: Aura instances support only HTTPS through port 443.\n142: This guide assumes that you have:\n143: A running instance of Neo4j — If you don’t have one, install Neo4j locally or sign up for an Aura cloud instance.\n144: Some familiarity with Cypher — If you are new to it, check out Getting started → Cypher.\n145: The Query API supports HTTP/2 as well as HTTP/1.1, unless either is explicitly disabled in the server configuration setting server.http_enabled_transports.\n146: It’s up to the client to initiate a connection with the preferred protocol.\n147: Glossary\n148: Aura\n149: Aura is Neo4j’s fully managed cloud service.\n150: It comes with both free and paid plans.\n151: Cypher\n152: Cypher is Neo4j’s graph query language that lets you retrieve data from the database.\n153: It is like SQL, but for graphs.\n154: ACID\n155: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability (ACID) are properties guaranteeing that database transactions are processed reliably.\n156: An ACID-compliant DBMS ensures that the data in the database remains accurate and consistent despite failures.\n157: causal consistency\n158: A database is causally consistent if read and write queries are seen by every member of the cluster in the same order.\n159: This is stronger than eventual consistency.\n160: transaction\n161: A transaction is a unit of work that is either committed in its entirety or rolled back on failure.\n162: An example is a bank transfer: it involves multiple steps, but they must all succeed or be reverted, to avoid money being subtracted from one account but not added to the other.\n163: Authorize requests\n164: Products\n165: Neo4j Graph Database\n166: Neo4j AuraDB\n167: Neo4j Graph Data Science\n168: Deployment Center\n169: Professional Services\n170: Pricing\n171: Developers\n172: Developer Home\n173: Documentation\n174: Deployment Center\n175: Developer Blog\n176: Community\n177: Virtual Events\n178: GraphAcademy\n179: Learn\n180: Resource Library\n181: Neo4j Blog\n182: GraphAcademy\n183: Research Center\n184: Case Studies\n185: Events Calendar\n186: NODES 2026\n187: Webinars\n188: Company\n189: About Us\n190: Newsroom\n191: Awards and Honors\n192: Careers\n193: Culture\n194: Leadership\n195: Support\n196: Social Networks\n197: © 2026 Neo4j, Inc.\n198: Terms | Privacy Policy | Sitemap\n199: Neo4j®, Neo Technology®, Cypher®, Neo4j® Bloom™, Neo4j® AuraDB℠ and\n200: Neo4j® AuraDS℠ are registered trademarks\n201: of Neo4j, Inc. All other marks are owned by their respective companies."
