      "numbered_text": "1: HTTP Authentication - Neo4j MCP\n2: Docs\n3: Docs\n4: Neo4j DBMS\n5: Getting Started\n6: Operations\n7: Migration and Upgrade\n8: Data Ingestion Guide\n9: Status Codes\n10: Java Reference\n11: Kerberos Add-on\n12: Neo4j Aura\n13: Neo4j Tools\n14: Neo4j Bloom\n15: Neo4j Browser\n16: Neo4j Data Importer\n17: Neo4j Desktop\n18: Neo4j Enterprise Studio\n19: Neo4j Ops Manager\n20: Neodash commercial\n21: Neo4j Graph Data Science\n22: Neo4j Graph Data Science Library\n23: Neo4j Graph Data Science Client\n24: Neo4j Graph Analytics for Snowflake\n25: Cypher Query Language\n26: Cypher\n27: Cypher Cheat Sheet\n28: APOC Library\n29: Generative AI\n30: Neo4j GraphRAG for Python\n31: Embeddings and vector indexes tutorial\n32: GenAI plugin\n33: Vector search indexes\n34: Vector search functions\n35: GraphQL vector index search documentation\n36: Create applications\n37: Python Driver\n38: Go Driver\n39: Java Driver\n40: JDBC Driver\n41: JavaScript Driver\n42: .Net Driver\n43: MCP\n44: Neo4j GraphQL Library\n45: Neo4j Visualization Library\n46: OGM Library\n47: Spring Data Neo4j\n48: HTTP API\n49: Neo4j Query API\n50: Bolt\n51: Connect data sources\n52: Neo4j Connector for Apache Spark\n53: Neo4j Connector for Apache Kafka\n54: Change Data Capture (CDC)\n55: BigQuery to Neo4j\n56: Google Cloud to Neo4j\n57: Labs\n58: GenAI Ecosystem\n59: LLM Knowledge Graph Builder\n60: Vector Index & Search\n61: LangChain\n62: LangChain.js\n63: LlamaIndex\n64: Haystack\n65: DSPy\n66: Agent Memory\n67: Getting Started\n68: Tutorials\n69: Framework Integrations\n70: MCP Server\n71: Developer Tools\n72: APOC Extended\n73: Aura CLI\n74: arrows.app\n75: Cypher Workbench\n76: ETL Tool\n77: NeoDash\n78: Frameworks & Integrations\n79: Needle Starter Kit\n80: Neo4j Plugin for Liquibase\n81: Neo4j Migrations\n82: neomodel\n83: RDF & Linked Data\n84: Neosemantics (Java)\n85: RDFLib-Neo4j (Python)\n86: Get Help\n87: Community Forum\n88: Discord Chat\n89: Product Support\n90: Neo4j Developer Blog\n91: Neo4j Videos\n92: GraphAcademy\n93: Beginners Courses\n94: Neo4j Fundamentals\n95: Cypher Fundamentals\n96: Importing Data Fundamentals\n97: Importing CSV Data\n98: Graph Data Modeling\n99: Data Scientist Courses\n100: Get started with Graph Data Science\n101: Path Finding\n102: Generative AI Courses\n103: Neo4j & Generative AI Fundamentals\n104: Vector Indexes & Unstructured Data\n105: Constructing Knowledge Graphs\n106: Building Agents in Neo4j Aura\n107: Neo4j Certification\n108: Neo4j Certified Professional\n109: Neo4j Graph Data Science Certification\n110: Get Started Free\n111: Theme\n112: Light\n113: Dark\n114: Search\n115: Skip to content\n116: Neo4j MCP\n117: Product Version\n118: Version 2 (Current)\n119: Version 1\n120: Neo4j MCP\n121: Introduction\n122: Quickstart\n123: Tools\n124: Security\n125: Setup\n126: MCP for Aura\n127: Installation\n128: Client configuration\n129: HTTP Authentication\n130: HTTP mode\n131: Configuration reference\n132: STDIO configuration\n133: HTTP configuration\n134: Requirement checks\n135: Monitoring\n136: Logging\n137: Telemetry\n138: Versions and support\n139: Migrate from v1 to v2\n140: Known issues\n141: Is this page helpful?\n142: Neo4j MCP\n143: HTTP Authentication\n144: Raise an issue\n145: HTTP Authentication\n146: When using HTTP transport mode, the Neo4j MCP server supports two authentication methods to accommodate different deployment scenarios:\n147: Bearer token authentication\n148: Bearer token authentication enables seamless integration with Neo4j Enterprise Edition and Neo4j Aura environments that use Single Sign-On (SSO)/OAuth/OIDC for identity management.\n149: This method is ideal for:\n150: Enterprise deployments with centralized identity providers (Okta, Azure AD, etc.)\n151: Neo4j Aura databases configured with SSO\n152: Organizations requiring OAuth 2.0 compliance\n153: Multi-factor authentication scenarios\n154: The bearer token is obtained from your identity provider and passed to Neo4j for authentication.\n155: The MCP server acts as a pass-through, forwarding the token to Neo4j’s authentication system.\n156: In HTTP mode, also send the X-Neo4j-MCP-URI header with every request.\n157: See HTTP mode for the complete HTTP request requirements.\n158: Start your Neo4j MCP server in HTTP mode:\n159: export NEO4J_MCP_TRANSPORT_MODE=\"http\"\n160: neo4j-mcp\n161: The server starts on http://127.0.0.1:80 by default.\n162: Create or edit your mcp.json file, for example for Neo4j Enterprise or Aura with SSO/OAuth configured:\n163: {\n164: \"servers\": {\n165: \"neo4j-http-bearer\": {\n166: \"type\": \"http\",\n167: \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:80/db/neo4j/mcp\",\n168: \"headers\": {\n169: \"X-Neo4j-MCP-URI\": \"bolt://localhost:7687\",\n170: \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\"\n171: }\n172: }\n173: }\n174: }\n175: Replace the bearer token with your actual OAuth/SSO token from your identity provider.\n176: Basic authentication\n177: Basic authentication with username and password is suitable for:\n178: Neo4j Community Edition\n179: Development and testing environments\n180: Direct database credentials without SSO\n181: Start your Neo4j MCP server in HTTP mode:\n182: export NEO4J_MCP_TRANSPORT_MODE=\"http\"\n183: neo4j-mcp\n184: The server starts on http://127.0.0.1:80 by default.\n185: Create or edit your mcp.json file:\n186: {\n187: \"servers\": {\n188: \"neo4j-http\": {\n189: \"type\": \"http\",\n190: \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:80/db/neo4j/mcp\",\n191: \"headers\": {\n192: \"X-Neo4j-MCP-URI\": \"bolt://localhost:7687\",\n193: \"Authorization\": \"Basic BASE64_ENCODED_CREDENTIALS\"\n194: }\n195: }\n196: }\n197: }\n198: Generate the base64-encoded credentials:\n199: echo -n \"neo4j:password\" | base64\n200: Select an authentication method\n201: Use Bearer Token when:\n202: You’re using Neo4j Enterprise Edition or Aura with SSO/OIDC/OAuth configured\n203: You want to integrate with your organization’s identity provider\n204: You need to support OAuth 2.0 flows\n205: Use Basic Auth when:\n206: You’re using traditional username/password authentication\n207: You’re using Neo4j Community Edition\n208: You have direct database credentials\n209: Custom auth header name\n210: By default, the server reads credentials from the Authorization header.\n211: Some platforms reserve Authorization for their own infrastructure-level signing and require credentials to be sent under a different header name.\n212: You can override the header the server reads from by setting NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_AUTH_HEADER_NAME or the --http-auth-header-name CLI flag.\n213: Bearer vs Basic detection works the same way regardless of the header name — the server reads the credential value from whatever header you configure.\n214: The configured header name cannot be empty or whitespace.\n215: AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime reserves Authorization for request signing.\n216: Use NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_AUTH_HEADER_NAME=X-Amzn-Bedrock-AgentCore-Runtime-Custom-Authorization to pass Neo4j credentials through AgentCore without conflicting with AWS signing.\n217: export NEO4J_MCP_TRANSPORT_MODE=\"http\"\n218: export NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_AUTH_HEADER_NAME=\"X-Amzn-Bedrock-AgentCore-Runtime-Custom-Authorization\"\n219: neo4j-mcp\n220: Then configure your MCP client to send the custom header, for example in mcp.json:\n221: {\n222: \"servers\": {\n223: \"neo4j-http\": {\n224: \"type\": \"http\",\n225: \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:80/db/neo4j/mcp\",\n226: \"headers\": {\n227: \"X-Neo4j-MCP-URI\": \"bolt://localhost:7687\",\n228: \"X-Amzn-Bedrock-AgentCore-Runtime-Custom-Authorization\": \"Bearer <your-sso-token-here>\"\n229: }\n230: }\n231: }\n232: }\n233: Unauthenticated endpoints\n234: By default, all HTTP requests require valid credentials.\n235: One option lets you bypass authentication for the ping request that some platforms issue before the MCP session is established.\n236: NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED_PING (--http-allow-unauthenticated-ping, default=false):\n237: Allows JSON-RPC ping requests through without credentials.\n238: The MCP spec permits pings before initialization; platforms such as AWS AgentCore Runtime use them as liveness/health checks.\n239: export NEO4J_MCP_TRANSPORT_MODE=\"http\"\n240: export NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED_PING=\"true\"\n241: neo4j-mcp\n242: Enable this only when the deployment platform requires it.\n243: tools/list always requires authentication.\n244: Troubleshooting authentication\n245: Bearer Token Issues\n246: 401 Unauthorized - Token not accepted\n247: Verify your Neo4j instance is Enterprise Edition or Aura with OAuth/SSO configured\n248: Community Edition does not support bearer token authentication - use Basic Auth instead\n249: Confirm the token hasn’t expired - bearer tokens typically have short lifespans (15-60 minutes)\n250: Check with your identity provider to ensure the token is valid\n251: Invalid token format\n252: Ensure the header format is exactly: Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN\n253: No extra spaces before or after \"Bearer\"\n254: Token should not be base64-encoded (unlike Basic Auth)\n255: Neo4j rejects valid token\n256: Verify your Neo4j instance is configured to accept tokens from your identity provider\n257: Check Neo4j server logs for specific authentication errors\n258: Confirm the token issuer matches Neo4j’s OAuth configuration\n259: Basic auth issues\n260: 401 Unauthorized - Credentials not accepted\n261: Verify username and password are correct\n262: Check that credentials are properly base64 encoded: echo -n \"user:pass\" | base64\n263: Ensure the authorization header format is: Authorization: Basic BASE64_STRING\n264: Empty credentials error\n265: Both username and password must be non-empty\n266: The base64 string must decode to username:password format with both parts present\n267: General issues\n268: No authentication header provided\n269: HTTP mode requires authentication on every request except ping when NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED_PING is enabled\n270: Ensure your MCP client configuration includes the correct authentication header (default: Authorization; or the custom header if NEO4J_MCP_HTTP_AUTH_HEADER_NAME is set)\n271: Connection refused / Cannot reach server\n272: Verify the Neo4j MCP server is running in HTTP mode\n273: Check the server is listening on the correct host:port\n274: Confirm firewall rules allow connections to the MCP server port\n275: Client configuration\n276: HTTP mode\n277: Products\n278: Neo4j Graph Database\n279: Neo4j AuraDB\n280: Neo4j Graph Data Science\n281: Deployment Center\n282: Professional Services\n283: Pricing\n284: Developers\n285: Developer Home\n286: Documentation\n287: Deployment Center\n288: Developer Blog\n289: Community\n290: Virtual Events\n291: GraphAcademy\n292: Learn\n293: Resource Library\n294: Neo4j Blog\n295: GraphAcademy\n296: Research Center\n297: Case Studies\n298: Events Calendar\n299: NODES 2026\n300: Webinars\n301: Company\n302: About Us\n303: Newsroom\n304: Awards and Honors\n305: Careers\n306: Culture\n307: Leadership\n308: Support\n309: Social Networks\n310: © 2026 Neo4j, Inc.\n311: Terms | Privacy Policy | Sitemap\n312: Neo4j®, Neo Technology®, Cypher®, Neo4j® Bloom™, Neo4j® AuraDB℠ and\n313: Neo4j® AuraDS℠ are registered trademarks\n314: of Neo4j, Inc. All other marks are owned by their respective companies."
