mers\n275: Plans\n276: Partners\n277: Resources\n278: Uncover hidden patterns\n279: Reveal insights with Cypher queries and graph algorithms for your connected data.\n280: Build applications with ease\n281: Simplify development using a native graph model, flexible schema, and expressive Cypher.\n282: Always-on, zero admin\n283: 99.95% uptime SLA, automated upgrades, patches, and maintenance.\n284: Enterprise-grade security\n285: Protect your data and ensure compliance with robust security features.\n286: Neo4j AuraDB: A smarter way to store and connect data\n287: Traditional databases strip away valuable context and relationships. Neo4j AuraDB preserves these\n288: connections, storing data in nodes with relationships intact. This makes your data smarter, more\n289: contextual, and easier to work with. AuraDB evolves with your changing business needs, eliminating rigid\n290: schemas and painful JOINs.\n291: Learn More\n292: Capabilities\n293: Build your critical applications with confidence\n294: Uncover hidden patterns\n295: Build apps with ease\n296: Always-on, zero admin\n297: Enterprise-grade security\n298: Match Patterns in Data and Relationships\n299: Find complex or recursive patterns without JOINs using expressive Cypher query language and graph algorithms.\n300: Learn More\n301: Explore Data Insights\n302: Find hidden connections and relationships using the Visualization tool and built-in Dashboards in the Aura console. Minimize manual query work and generate visual insights faster with AI-powered dashboard creation and GenAI Copilot.\n303: Learn More\n304: Index-Free Adjacency\n305: Navigate the graph without index lookups or JOINS, keeping processing time proportional to data size, making traversals lightning fast with index-free adjacency.\n306: Learn More\n307: Analytical and Transactional Workloads\n308: Support diverse workloads to simplify data architecture. Automatically split analytical queries to run concurrently across multiple threads on multi-core CPUs for stronger results.\n309: Learn More\n310: Enrich Predictions and Inferences\n311: Discover more insights using graph data science and machine-learning algorithms, such as pathfinding, centrality algorithms, community detection algorithms, and network embeddings.\n312: Learn More\n313: Native Graph Data Model\n314: Eliminate the transition from data design to implementation. Use nodes, relationships, and properties to represent structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data naturally.\n315: Learn More\n316: Flexible Schema\n317: Add new entities, properties, and relationships without rebuilding the database or modifying application code.\n318: Learn More\n319: Write Less Code With Cypher\n320: Write less code using Cypher, a simple and expressive query language that often requires fewer lines of code than SQL (for RDBMS) or SPARQL.\n321: Learn More\n322: Faster, Easier Development\n323: Use 65+ pre-tuned graph data science algorithms for deeper data insights.\n324: Learn More\n325: 99.95% Uptime SLA\n326: High availability, reliability, and minimal downtime. Scale instances for data and compute needs, efficiently managing datasets and memory-intensive workloads.\n327: Learn More\n328: Zero Administration\n329: Build apps without having to manage infrastructure. Get automatic service upgrades, security updates, and new features without interruption.\n330: Learn More\n331: Minimize Downtime and Data Loss\n332: Restore databases with full or incremental backups across multiple zones, point-in-time recovery, and fully encrypted snapshots verified for data consistency.\n333: Learn More\n334: Multi-Cloud Freedom\n335: Available on Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud in over 60 global regions.\n336: Learn More\n337: End-to-End Encryption\n338: Data is encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys. Network traffic, even within service infrastructure, is encrypted via Transport Layer Security (TLS).\n339: Learn More\n340: Fine-Grained Access Control\n341: Protect data from unauthorized access with role-based and property-based access control (PBAC), enabling selective graph traversals and precise read/write access for different parts of the graph.\n342: Learn More\n343: Network Security\n344: Segregate network traffic and resources to prevent unauthorized access. You also reduce the risk of security breaches with network isolation and private endpoints.\n345: Learn More\n346: Comply With Industry Regulations\n347: Adhere to industry compliance standards for data security and privacy regulations, such as ISO 27001, ISO 20243, GDPR, CCPA, SOC2 Type II, SOC3, and HIPAA.\n348: Learn More\n349: Free Guide\n350: Build and work with your first knowledge graph\n351: Read Now\n352: Powerful use cases\n353: Optimize your projects with the Graph Database as a foundation.\n354: AI systems\n355: Build the knowledge layer with the models, data, and memory your AI systems need to reason with\n356: real context, explain their answers, and keep learning as your data changes.\n357: Learn More\n358: Knowledge graphs\n359: Access deep, dynamic context by connecting your data in knowledge graphs. You can quickly design,\n360: implement, and evolve your knowledge graph with Neo4j.\n361: Learn More\n362: Fraud detection & analytics\n363: Real-time analysis of data relationships is essential to uncovering fraud rings and other\n364: sophisticated scams before fraudsters and criminals cause lasting.\n365: Learn More\n366: Healthcare & life sciences\n367: Pharmaceutical companies, chemical manufacturers and biotech companies are using Neo4j to analyze\n368: data in ways not previously possible without graphs.\n369: Learn More\n370: Real-time recommendations\n371: Graph-powered recommendation engines help companies personalize products, content and services by\n372: leveraging a multitude of connections in real time.\n373: Learn More\n374: Supply chain management\n375: Graph technology is essential to optimize the flow of goods, uncover vulnerabilities and boost\n376: overall supply chain resilience. Discover how Transparency-One, Caterpillar and others use graph\n377: technology to ensure business...\n378: Learn More\n379: Explore All Use Cases\n380: Loved by devs. Deployed worldwide.\n381: 1,700+ organizations build on Neo4j for data breakthroughs.\n382: Data insights & results\n383: First slide details.\n384: Current Slide\n385: Second slide details.\n386: Third slide details.\n387: Fourth slide details.\n388: Fifth slide details.\n389: Previous Slide\n390: Next Slide\n391: “Neo4j AuraDB aligns well with Dun & Bradstreet’s cloud strategy and helps us to deliver the deep\n392: knowledge our clients need to take intelligent actions based on timely compliance decisions.”\n393: Matthew Voss, Identity Insights Leader\n394: Learn More\n395: “Neo4j AuraDB helps us visualize our data and the opportunities in one place,\n396: so we can focus our efforts on streamlining our service delivery and operations.”\n397: Luke Slowen, Enterprise Architect\n398: Learn\n399: More\n400: “The schema flexibility and analytical capabilities of Neo4j allow us to see\n401: relationships\n402: between manufacturing batches that simply would not be possible with other tools.”\n403: Eric Wespi, Data Scientist\n404: Learn More\n405: “Our developer team is able to calculate the risk score of every asset, defined as any piece of software, server, services, website, source code, and more, in just four minutes. These are complex traversals ... we’re able to do them extremely quickly.”\n406: Zach Probst, Staff Software Engineer\n407: Learn More\n408: \"We’re excited by Neo4j’s latest enhancements to their graph database solutions, all of which have the potential to take our data and processing and analytics to the next level and achieve unprecedented levels of insight and decision-making.”\n409: Get started today!\n410: AuraDB Free\n411: $0\n412: Learn and Explore Graphs\n413: Start Learning\n414: AuraDB Professional\n415: $65\n416: /GB/month\n417: (minimum 1GB cluster)\n418: Build Production-Ready Apps\n419: Try for Free\n420: AuraDB Business Critical\n421: $146\n422: /GB/month\n423: (minimum 2GB cluster)\n424: Scale Apps for Enterprise Use\n425: Sign Up\n426: AuraDB Virtual Dedicated Cloud\n427: Deploy applications, using all capabilities of AuraDB Business Critical, but in a dedicated environment\n428: Contact Us\n429: Learn More About Pricing\n430: Works seamlessly with your tech stack\n431: Neo4j integrates with leading technology providers\n432: Tools and tips to build intelligent apps\n433: Get started graphing with AuraDB.\n434: Featured Resources\n435: GraphAcademy\n436: Blog\n437: Videos\n438: Events\n439: & Webinars\n440: AuraDB Product Brief\n441: Overview of AuraDB’s graph database features, benefits, and practical use\n442: cases.\n443: Learn More\n444: Neo4j Trust Center\n445: Learn about our security posture and request compliance documentation.\n446: Learn More\n447: AuraDB Datasheet\n448: Snapshot of AuraDB's graph database features, benefits, and key use cases.\n449: Learn More\n450: Aura Documentation\n451: Guides for data modeling, data management, querying, security, drivers,\n452: visualization, and integration.\n453: Learn More\n454: Fundamentals of Property Graphs\n455: Learn the basics and how to evolve your graphs.\n456: 1 hour\n457: Fundamentals of Writing Cypher\n458: Learn to write our query graph language.\n459: 1 hour\n460: Graph Data Modeling Fundamentals\n461: Learn how to design a Neo4j graph using best practices.\n462: 2 hours\n463: Intro to Neo4j Graph Data Science\n464: Gain a high-level, technical understanding.\n465: 30 minutes\n466: What’s New in AuraDB\n467: Easily build a knowledge graph from your existing documents with Document Intelligence\n468: The startup’s guide to getting started building on Neo4j Aura\n469: From first graph algorithm experiment to production with Aura Graph Analytics\n470: Build better recommendations with Aura Graph Analytics\n471: Multiple databases now generally available in AuraDB\n472: Managing risk in a manufacturing plant with Aura Graph Analytics\n473: Use Knowledge Graphs to Implement RAG\n474: Training Series:\n475: Graph Database Intro\n476: Learn about core concepts, such as nodes, relationships, and properties.\n477: Training Series:\n478: Enhance Geospatial Analytics With Graphs\n479: Work with Apache Sedona, Spatial SQL, and GeoPandas for various data types.\n480: Going Meta:\n481: Migrate From Triple Store to Property Graph\n482: See step by step how to make the migration easy.\n483: Road to NODES:\n484: How to Graph Relational Database Models\n485: Learn how to get your app up and running.\n486: Neo4j Live:\n487: Querying and Data Importing\n488: Learn about upgraded graph visualization, quick search capabilities, and other new features.\n489: Neo4j:AuraDB Overview and Best Practices\n490: Build smarter apps using connected data and graphs without JOINs.\n491: Neo4j: Build Powerful Fraud Detection Apps\n492: Uncover difficult-to-detect hidden patterns in your data to fight fraud.\n493: Neo4j: Optimize Supply Chain With Knowledge Graphs\n494: Improve visibility, increase agility, and build a more resilient network.\n495: Neo4j: Trends in Graph and Analytics\n496: Learn to handle the rise of GenAI, unstructured, and semi-structured data with knowledge graphs.\n497: AuraDB frequently asked questions\n498: How does AuraDB differ from traditional relational databases?\n499: AuraDB is a purpose-built, high-performance native graph database engine that stores and navigates\n500: relationships between data elements directly. Relational databases, use JOINs to access related data. In\n501: contrast, AuraDB handles relationships natively using pointers, eliminating the associated performance costs.\n502: Learn more\n503: AuraDB also supports ACID transactional properties, ensuring that all data changes are consistent and\n504: reliable. This guarantees data integrity even in multi-user, highly concurrent environments.\n505: This robust architecture enables Neo4j customers to build reliable transactional and analytical apps with\n506: confidence in their data’s consistency and integrity. Learn more about Neo4j AuraDB's database internals.\n507: What programming languages and drivers does AuraDB support?\n508: AuraDB empowers developers with high query performance, a flexible data model, and broad language support,\n509: making migration easy. AuraDB supports a wide range of programming languages, with official drivers for .NET,\n510: Java, JavaScript, Go, and Python.\n511: Additionally, community contributors provide drivers for many other languages, including PHP, Ruby, R,\n512: Erlang, Clojure, C/C++, and more. Learn more about available drivers.\n513: What security and regulatory certification standards does\n514: Neo4j Aura meet?\n515: Aura complies with key security and regulatory standards, including CCPA, ISO 27001, ISO 20243, SOC2 Type II,\n516: SOC3, HIPAA and GDPR.\n517: Visit Neo4j's Trust Center at https://trust.neo4j.com/ for comprehensive security and compliance\n518: information.\n519: What migration and interoperability tools does Neo4j provide?\n520: Neo4j supports various approaches for migration and interoperability. It offers standard APIs like GraphQL,\n521: which work across different database models. Neo4j also supports the ISO standard graph query language GQL,\n522: with mature implementations like Cypher and OpenCypher, and provides a plugin for Apache Gremlin.\n523: Neo4j includes Sql2Cypher, a tool that converts SQL to Cypher, often simplifying and improving query\n524: readability for those migrating from SQL. Additionally, Neo4j provides SQL access through BI connectors.\n525: How does Neo4j help developers optimize query performance?\n526: Neo4j’s “EXPLAIN and PROFILE” options in the Cypher query language help architects and developers identify\n527: where indexes can improve query performance. Neo4j doesn’t need JOINs to access related/connected data. This\n528: means Neo4j doesn’t need JOIN indexes, materialized views, or other components that relational databases use\n529: to work around the fact that they don’t store relationships directly.\n530: In short, Neo4j uses indexes as a way to find the Node(s) (entities or discrete objects of a domain) that are\n531: the starting point for a graph query. It doesn’t need additional indexes, materialized views, or other\n532: components to speed up queries of related data.\n533: What data integration and quality tools are available in\n534: Neo4j AuraDB?\n535: AuraDB has multiple data integration and data quality tools built into the database, including:\n536: Cypher query language and Graph Data Science algorithm for data transformation,\n537: aggregation, preparation, and feature generation.\n538: Python client for direct integration with Pandas data frames, enabling data scientists to\n539: leverage Python libraries for data preparation and transformation.\n540: APOC library with stored procedures for data aggregation, formatting, cleaning, and\n541: engineering.\n542: Connectors (Spark, Kafka, Data Warehouse) for data movement and transformation.\n543: Data Importer for no-code graph data modeling and loading.\n544: Knowledge is at your fingertips\n545: Make your AI accurate, explainable, and trustworthy.\n546: Start for free\n547: Talk to us\n548: Products\n549: AuraDB\n550: Aura Graph Analytics\n551: Aura Agent\n552: Enterprise Studio\n553: Community Edition\n554: Cypher Query Language\n555: Fleet Manager\n556: Graph Analytics\n557: Graph Database\n558: Graph Data Science\n559: GraphQL\n560: Knowledge layer\n561: Pricing\n562: Virtual Graph\n563: Solutions\n564: AI systems\n565: AI integrations\n566: Case studies\n567: Financial crime intelligence\n568: GraphRAG\n569: Industries & use cases\n570: Intelligence analysis\n571: Knowledge graphs\n572: Model context protocol (MCP)\n573: Pattern matching\n574: Develope