      "numbered_text": "1: Snowflake SQL API | Snowflake Documentation\n2: DOCUMENTATION\n3: /\n4: EN\n5: Language\n6: English\n7: Français\n8: Deutsch\n9: 日本語\n10: 한국어\n11: Português\n12: Get started\n13: Guides\n14: Developer\n15: Reference\n16: Release notes\n17: Tutorials\n18: Status\n19: For AI agents: documentation index at /llms.txt — fetch to discover all Snowflake documentation pages. Markdown version of this page: /en/developer-guide/sql-api/index.md\n20: Overview\n21: Snowflake Horizon Catalog\n22: Applications and tools for connecting to Snowflake\n23: Virtual warehouses\n24: Databases, Tables, & Views\n25: Data types\n26: Data Integration\n27: Snowflake Openflow\n28: Apache Iceberg™\n29: Apache Iceberg™ Tables\n30: Snowflake Open Catalog\n31: Zero-Copy Connectors\n32: About SAP® and Snowflake\n33: About Salesforce Data Cloud and Snowflake\n34: About Workday Live Data Query for Snowflake\n35: About Workday Data Cloud and Snowflake\n36: Data engineering\n37: Data loading\n38: Dynamic tables\n39: Streams and tasks\n40: Row timestamps\n41: DCM Projects\n42: Pipeline Builder\n43: dbt Projects on Snowflake\n44: Data Unloading\n45: Storage lifecycle policies\n46: Migrations\n47: Queries\n48: Collaboration\n49: Snowflake AI & ML\n50: Snowflake Postgres\n51: Alerts & Notifications\n52: Security\n53: Organizations & Accounts\n54: Business continuity & data recovery\n55: Performance optimization\n56: Cost & Billing\n57: Snowflake SQL API¶\n58: The Snowflake SQL API is a REST API that you can use to access and update data in a Snowflake database. You can use\n59: this API to develop custom applications and integrations that:\n60: Perform queries\n61: Manage your deployment (e.g. provision users and roles, create tables, etc.)\n62: The Snowflake SQL API provides operations that you can use to:\n63: Submit SQL statements for execution.\n64: Check the status of the execution of a statement.\n65: Cancel the execution of a statement.\n66: You can use this API to execute standard queries and most\n67: DDL and DML statements.\n68: See Limitations of the SQL API for the types of statements that are not supported.\n69: Introduction to the SQL API\n70: Get an overview of the API.\n71: About the SQL API endpoints\n72: Learn about the endpoints that make up the API.\n73: Authenticating to the server\n74: Use OAuth or Key Pair to authenticate with the Snowflake server.\n75: Submitting a request to execute SQL statements\n76: Set up and submit requests using an API endpoint.\n77: Handling responses\n78: Check request status and get results and other data after a request.\n79: Submitting multiple SQL statements in a single request\n80: Send multiple SQL statements in a single API request.\n81: Creating and calling stored procedures\n82: Create a stored procedure by specifying it in the body of a request.\n83: Using explicit transactions\n84: Execute SQL in a transaction by specifying the start, end, and statements in the transaction.\n85: Getting details about an error\n86: Retrieve error information.\n87: Canceling the execution of a SQL statement\n88: Cancel SQL statement execution.\n89: Snowflake SQL API reference\n90: Read details about the operations, objects, HTTP headers, and response codes for this API.\n91: Was this page helpful?\n92: YesNo\n93: Visit Snowflake\n94: Join the conversation\n95: Develop with Snowflake\n96: Share your feedback\n97: Read the latest on our blog\n98: Get your own certification\n99: Privacy NoticeSite TermsCookies Settings© 2026 Snowflake, Inc. All Rights Reserved."
