      "numbered_text": "1: Authenticating to the server | Snowflake Documentation\n2: DOCUMENTATION\n3: /\n4: EN\n5: Language\n6: English\n7: Français\n8: Deutsch\n9: 日本語\n10: 한국어\n11: Português\n12: Get started\n13: Guides\n14: Developer\n15: Reference\n16: Release notes\n17: Tutorials\n18: Status\n19: For AI agents: documentation index at /llms.txt — fetch to discover all Snowflake documentation pages. Markdown version of this page: /en/developer-guide/sql-api/authenticating.md\n20: Overview\n21: Builders\n22: DevOps with Snowflake\n23: Observability\n24: Cortex AI Cookbook\n25: Cortex AI Cookbook\n26: Snowpark Library\n27: Snowpark API\n28: Snowpark Connect for Apache Spark\n29: Machine Learning\n30: Snowflake ML\n31: Snowpark Code Execution Environments\n32: Snowpark Container Services\n33: Snowflake Container Runtime\n34: Code Bundles\n35: Functions and procedures\n36: Logging, Tracing, and Metrics\n37: Snowflake APIs\n38: Snowflake Python APIs\n39: Snowflake REST APIs\n40: SQL API\n41: Introduction\n42: Endpoints\n43: Authentication\n44: Submitting requests\n45: Handling responses\n46: Multiple statements\n47: Stored procedures\n48: Transactions\n49: Handling errors\n50: Query canceling\n51: SQL API reference\n52: Apps\n53: Streamlit in Snowflake\n54: About Streamlit in Snowflake\n55: Getting started\n56: Getting started with Streamlit in Snowflake\n57: Example: Build a personalized data dashboard\n58: Example: Build a form that writes to Snowflake\n59: Streamlit object management\n60: Billing considerations\n61: Security considerations\n62: Privilege requirements\n63: Understanding owner's rights\n64: PrivateLink\n65: App development\n66: Create your app\n67: Edit your app\n68: Manage your app\n69: Delete your app\n70: Runtime environments\n71: Dependency management\n72: File organization\n73: Secrets and configuration\n74: Personalization with user information\n75: Migrations and upgrades\n76: Identify your app type\n77: Migrate to a container runtime\n78: Migrate from ROOT_LOCATION\n79: Convert Tableau workbooks with CoCo\n80: Features\n81: Embedding Streamlit apps in external pages\n82: External access\n83: Git integration\n84: Restricted caller's rights\n85: Logging and tracing\n86: Row access policies\n87: Sharing Streamlit in Snowflake apps\n88: Sleep timer\n89: Streamlit in Snowflake in Workspaces\n90: Limitations and library changes\n91: Troubleshooting Streamlit in Snowflake\n92: Streamlit open-source library documentation\n93: Snowflake App Runtime\n94: Snowflake Native App Framework\n95: Snowflake Declarative Sharing\n96: Snowflake Native SDK for Connectors\n97: External Integration\n98: External Functions\n99: Kafka and Spark Connectors\n100: Snowflake Scripting\n101: Snowflake Scripting Developer Guide\n102: Tools\n103: Snowflake CLI\n104: CI/CD\n105: Git in Snowflake\n106: Python ecosystem\n107: Snowflake SQLAlchemy\n108: Drivers\n109: Overview\n110: Considerations when drivers reuse sessions\n111: Scala versions\n112: Reference\n113: API Reference\n114: DeveloperSQL APIAuthentication\n115: Authenticating to the server¶\n116: This topic describes how to authenticate to the server when using the Snowflake SQL API.\n117: When you send a request, the request must include authentication information. The next sections explain how to add\n118: this information to the request:\n119: Using OAuth\n120: Using key-pair authentication\n121: Using workload identity federation\n122: Using OAuth¶\n123: To use OAuth, follow these steps:\n124: Set up OAuth for authentication.\n125: See Introduction to OAuth for details on how to set up OAuth and get an OAuth token.\n126: Use Snowflake CLI to verify that you can use a generated OAuth token to connect to Snowflake:\n127: For Linux and MacOS systems\n128: Copy code\n129: $ snow connection test --account <account_identifier> --user <user> --authenticator=oauth --token=<oauth_token>\n130: For Windows systems\n131: Copy code\n132: $ snow connection test --account <account_identifier> --user <user> --authenticator=oauth --token=<oauth_token>\n133: In each API request you send, set the following headers:\n134: Authorization: Bearer oauth_token\n135: where oauth_token is the generated OAuth token.\n136: (Optional) X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type: OAUTH\n137: If you omit the X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type header, Snowflake determines the token type by examining the token.\n138: Even though this header is optional, you can choose to specify this header. You can set the header to one of the following values:\n139: KEYPAIR_JWT (for key-pair authentication)\n140: OAUTH (for OAuth)\n141: PROGRAMMATIC_ACCESS_TOKEN (for programmatic access tokens)\n142: Using key-pair authentication¶\n143: To use key pair authentication, follow these steps:\n144: Set up key-pair authentication.\n145: As part of this process, you must:\n146: Generate a public-private key pair. The generated private key should be in a file (e.g. named rsa_key.p8).\n147: Assign the public key to your Snowflake user. After you assign the key to the user, run the\n148: DESCRIBE USER command. In the output, the RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_FP property should be set to the fingerprint of the public key assigned to the user.\n149: For instructions on how to generate the key pair and assign a key to a user,\n150: see Key-pair authentication and key-pair rotation. For language-specific examples of creating a fingerprint and generating a\n151: JWT token, see the following:\n152: Python\n153: Java\n154: Node.js\n155: Use Snowflake CLI to verify that you can use the generated private key to\n156: connect to Snowflake:\n157: Copy code\n158: $ snow connection generate-jwt --account <account_identifier> --user <user> --private-key-path <path>/rsa_key.p8\n159: The command prompts you for a private key passphrase to complete the connection. You can avoid the prompt by providing the passphrase in the PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE environment variable.\n160: In your application code:\n161: Generate the fingerprint (a SHA-256 hash) of the public key for the user. Prefix the fingerprint with SHA256:.\n162: For example:\n163: SHA256:hash\n164: You can also execute the SQL DESCRIBE USER command to get the value from\n165: the RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_FP property.\n166: Generate a JSON Web Token (JWT) with the following fields in the payload:\n167: FieldDescriptionExample\n168: iss\n169: Issuer of the JWT. Set it to the following value:\n170: account_identifier.user.SHA256:public_key_fingerprint\n171: where:\n172: account_identifier is your Snowflake account identifier.\n173: If you are using the account locator, exclude any region information from\n174: the account locator.\n175: user is your Snowflake user name.\n176: SHA256:public_key_fingerprint is the fingerprint that you generated in the previous step.\n177: Note\n178: The account_identifier and user values must use all uppercase characters. If your account ID contains periods (.), you must replace them with hyphens (-), as periods in an account identifier cause the JWT to be invalid.\n179: MYORGANIZATION-MYACCOUNT.MYUSER.SHA256:public_key_fingerprint\n180: sub\n181: Subject for the JWT. Set it to the following value:\n182: account_identifier.userMYORGANIZATION-MYACCOUNT.MYUSER\n183: iat\n184: Issue time for the JWT in UTC. Set the value to the current time value as either seconds or milliseconds.\n185: 1615370644 (seconds)\n186: 1615370644000 (milliseconds)\n187: exp\n188: Expiration time for the JWT in UTC. You can specify the value as either seconds or milliseconds.\n189: Note\n190: The JWT is valid for at most one hour after the token is issued, even if you specify a longer expiration time.\n191: 1615374184 (seconds)\n192: 1615374184000 (milliseconds)\n193: Expand\n194: Show lessSee more\n195: In each API request that you send, set the following headers:\n196: Authorization: Bearer JWT\n197: where JWT is the token that you generated.\n198: (Optional) X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type: KEYPAIR_JWT\n199: If you omit the X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type header, Snowflake determines the token type by examining the token.\n200: Even though this header is optional, you can choose to specify this header. You can set the header to one of the following values:\n201: KEYPAIR_JWT (for key-pair authentication)\n202: OAUTH (for OAuth)\n203: PROGRAMMATIC_ACCESS_TOKEN (for programmatic access tokens)\n204: Python example¶\n205: The following sections describe how to generate a JWT and fingerprint using Python.\n206: For an example of generating a JWT in Python, see sql-api-generate-jwt.py. The\n207: sql-api-generate-jwt.py example uses the PyJWT module, which you can install by running:\n208: Copy code\n209: pip install pyjwt\n210: Generating a JWT in Python¶\n211: The following sections of code demonstrate how to generate a JWT. For a full example,\n212: see sql-api-generate-jwt.py.\n213: Note\n214: This example is intended for use as a reference only. Do not use this code in production applications or environments.\n215: Copy code\n216: from datetime import timedelta, timezone, datetime\n217: # This example relies on the PyJWT module (https://pypi.org/project/PyJWT/).\n218: import jwt\n219: # Construct the fully qualified name of the user in uppercase.\n220: # - Replace <account_identifier> with your account identifier.\n221: # (See https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/admin-account-identifier.html .)\n222: # - Replace <user_name> with your Snowflake user name.\n223: account = \"<account_identifier>\"\n224: # Get the account identifier without the region, cloud provider, or subdomain.\n225: if not '.global' in account:\n226: idx = account.find('.')\n227: if idx > 0:\n228: account = account[0:idx]\n229: else:\n230: # Handle the replication case.\n231: idx = account.find('-')\n232: if idx > 0:\n233: account = account[0:idx]\n234: # Use uppercase for the account identifier and user name.\n235: account = account.upper()\n236: user = \"<user_name>\".upper()\n237: qualified_username = account + \".\" + user\n238: # Get the current time in order to specify the time when the JWT was issued and the expiration time of the JWT.\n239: now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n240: # Specify the length of time during which the JWT will be valid. You can specify at most 1 hour.\n241: lifetime = timedelta(minutes=59)\n242: # Create the payload for the token.\n243: payload = {\n244: # Set the issuer to the fully qualified username concatenated with the public key fingerprint (calculated in the previous step).\n245: \"iss\": qualified_username + '.' + public_key_fp,\n246: # Set the subject to the fully qualified username.\n247: \"sub\": qualified_username,\n248: # Set the issue time to now.\n249: \"iat\": now,\n250: # Set the expiration time, based on the lifetime specified for this object.\n251: \"exp\": now + lifetime\n252: }\n253: # Generate the JWT. private_key is the private key that you read from the private key file in the previous step when you generated the public key fingerprint.\n254: encoding_algorithm=\"RS256\"\n255: token = jwt.encode(payload, key=private_key, algorithms=encoding_algorithm)\n256: # If you are using a version of PyJWT prior to 2.0, jwt.encode returns a byte string, rather than a string.\n257: # If the token is a byte string, convert it to a string.\n258: if isinstance(token, bytes):\n259: token = token.decode('utf-8')\n260: print(token)\n261: decoded_token = jwt.decode(token, key=private_key.public_key(), algorithms=[encoding_algorithm])\n262: print(\"Generated a JWT with the following payload:\\n{}\".format(decoded_token))\n263: Generating a fingerprint in Python¶\n264: The following sections of code demonstrate how to generate the fingerprint. For a full example, see\n265: sql-api-generate-jwt.py.\n266: Copy code\n267: from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import load_pem_private_key\n268: from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import Encoding\n269: from cryptography.hazmat.primitives.serialization import PublicFormat\n270: from cryptography.hazmat.backends import default_backend\n271: ..\n272: import base64\n273: from getpass import getpass\n274: import hashlib\n275: ..\n276: # If you generated an encrypted private key, implement this method to return\n277: # the passphrase for decrypting your private key. As an example, this function\n278: # prompts the user for the passphrase.\n279: def get_private_key_passphrase():\n280: return getpass('Passphrase for private key: ')\n281: # Private key that you will load from the private key file.\n282: private_key = None\n283: # Open the private key file.\n284: # Replace <private_key_file_path> with the path to your private key file (e.g. /x/y/z/rsa_key.p8).\n285: with open('<private_key_file_path>', 'rb') as pem_in:\n286: pemlines = pem_in.read()\n287: try:\n288: # Try to access the private key without a passphrase.\n289: private_key = load_pem_private_key(pemlines, None, default_backend())\n290: except TypeError:\n291: # If that fails, provide the passphrase returned from get_private_key_passphrase().\n292: private_key = load_pem_private_key(pemlines, get_private_key_passphrase().encode(), default_backend())\n293: # Get the raw bytes of the public key.\n294: public_key_raw = private_key.public_key().public_bytes(Encoding.DER, PublicFormat.SubjectPublicKeyInfo)\n295: # Get the sha256 hash of the raw bytes.\n296: sha256hash = hashlib.sha256()\n297: sha256hash.update(public_key_raw)\n298: # Base64-encode the value and prepend the prefix 'SHA256:'.\n299: public_key_fp = 'SHA256:' + base64.b64encode(sha256hash.digest()).decode('utf-8')\n300: Snowflake CLI example¶\n301: You can use the Snowflake CLI snow connection generate-jwt command to generate a JWT for key-pair authentication. For more information, see snow connection generate-jwt.\n302: This example generates a token for account TEST and user JDOE, using the private key from rsa_key.p8:\n303: Copy code\n304: snow connection generate-jwt --user JDOE --account TEST --private-key-file=rsa_key.p8\n305: The command prompts you for a private key passphrase to complete the connection. You can avoid the prompt by providing the passphrase in the PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE environment variable.\n306: Java example¶\n307: For an example of generating a JWT in Java, see\n308: SimpleStatementsApi.java.\n309: Note\n310: This example is intended for use as a reference only. Do not use this code in production applications or environments.\n311: This example uses the following third-party libraries:\n312: Swagger Codegen: an open source library useful in developing REST\n313: APIs and applications.\n314: Auth0: provides Java APIs for authentication and generating JWT tokens.\n315: Node.js example¶\n316: For an example of generating a JWT in Node.js, see\n317: sql-api-generate-jwt.js.\n318: Note\n319: This example is intended for use as a reference only. Do not use this code in production applications or environments.\n320: Using workload identity federation¶\n321: To use workload identity federation, follow these steps:\n322: As a workload administrator, configure your cloud provider or OpenID Connect (OIDC) provider to issue an attestation:\n323: AWS\n324: Microsoft Azure (see Configure Microsoft Entra ID and Configure Azure)\n325: Google Cloud\n326: Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS)\n327: Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)\n328: Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)\n329: Custom OIDC provider\n330: As a Snowflake administrator, create a service user for the workload that is authenticating:\n331: AWS\n332: Microsoft Azure\n333: Google Cloud\n334: Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS)\n335: Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)\n336: Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)\n337: Custom OIDC provider\n338: Obtain a token that can be included in the authorization header of your request:\n339: AWS: For a workload with an attached IAM role, follow the AWS steps to get a JWT token. Be sure to set the audience to snowflakecomputing.com.\n340: Microsoft Azure: For a workload with an attached managed identity, follow the Azure steps to get an access token. Be sure to set the resource to api://fd3f753b-eed3-462c-b6a7-a4b5bb650aad.\n341: Google Cloud: For a workload with an attached service account, follow the Google Cloud steps to get an ID token. Be sure to set the audience to snowflakecomputing.com.\n342: Kubernetes / Custom OIDC: Use the token issued by your OIDC provider.\n343: Put the token in the authorization header of your request using the following format:\n344: Copy code\n345: Authorization: Bearer WIF.{provider}.{token}\n346: X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type: WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_FEDERATION\n347: Where:\n348: {provider} corresponds to your cloud provider. Specify one of the following values: AWS, AZURE, GCP, or OIDC.\n349: {token} is the token obtained in the previous step.\n350: Was this page helpful?\n351: YesNo\n352: Visit Snowflake\n353: Join the conversation\n354: Develop with Snowflake\n355: Share your feedback\n356: Read the latest on our blog\n357: Get your own certification\n358: Privacy NoticeSite TermsCookies Settings© 2026 Snowflake, Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n359: On this page\n360: Using OAuth\n361: Using key-pair authentication\n362: Using workload identity federation\n363: Related content\n364: Snowflake SQL API reference"
