      "numbered_text": "1: Snowflake-managed MCP server | Snowflake Documentation\n2: DOCUMENTATION\n3: /\n4: EN\n5: Language\n6: English\n7: Français\n8: Deutsch\n9: 日本語\n10: 한국어\n11: Português\n12: Get started\n13: Guides\n14: Developer\n15: Reference\n16: Release notes\n17: Tutorials\n18: Status\n19: For AI agents: documentation index at /llms.txt — fetch to discover all Snowflake documentation pages. Markdown version of this page: /en/user-guide/snowflake-cortex/cortex-agents-mcp.md\n20: Overview\n21: Snowflake Horizon Catalog\n22: Applications and tools for connecting to Snowflake\n23: Virtual warehouses\n24: Databases, Tables, & Views\n25: Data types\n26: Data Integration\n27: Snowflake Openflow\n28: Apache Iceberg™\n29: Apache Iceberg™ Tables\n30: Snowflake Open Catalog\n31: Zero-Copy Connectors\n32: About SAP® and Snowflake\n33: About Salesforce Data Cloud and Snowflake\n34: About Workday Live Data Query for Snowflake\n35: About Workday Data Cloud and Snowflake\n36: Data engineering\n37: Data loading\n38: Dynamic tables\n39: Streams and tasks\n40: Row timestamps\n41: DCM Projects\n42: Pipeline Builder\n43: dbt Projects on Snowflake\n44: Data Unloading\n45: Storage lifecycle policies\n46: Migrations\n47: Queries\n48: Collaboration\n49: Snowflake AI & ML\n50: Governance and availability\n51: Snowflake CoWork\n52: Snowflake Dashboards\n53: Snowflake Artifacts\n54: Snowflake CoCo\n55: Cortex AI Functions\n56: Cortex Agents\n57: Cortex Agent SDK\n58: Snowflake-managed MCP server\n59: Cortex AI Gateway\n60: Cortex Analyst\n61: Cortex Search\n62: Cortex Knowledge Extensions\n63: Cortex Inference\n64: Cortex Training\n65: Cortex AI Guardrails\n66: AI Observability\n67: ML Functions\n68: Provisioned Throughput\n69: ML Development and ML Ops\n70: Pricing\n71: Snowflake Postgres\n72: Alerts & Notifications\n73: Security\n74: Organizations & Accounts\n75: Business continuity & data recovery\n76: Performance optimization\n77: Cost & Billing\n78: GuidesSnowflake AI & MLSnowflake-managed MCP server\n79: Snowflake-managed MCP server¶\n80: Overview¶\n81: Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open-source standard that lets AI agents securely interact with business applications and external data systems, such as databases and content repositories. MCP lets enterprise businesses reduce integration challenges and quickly deliver outcomes from models. Since its launch, MCP has become foundational for agentic applications, providing a consistent and secure mechanism for invoking tools and retrieving data.\n82: The Snowflake-managed MCP server lets AI agents securely retrieve data from Snowflake accounts without needing to deploy separate infrastructure. You can configure the MCP server to serve Cortex Analyst, Cortex Search, and Cortex Agents as tools, along with custom tools and SQL executions on the standards-based interface. MCP clients discover and invoke these tools, and retrieve data required for the application. With managed MCP servers on Snowflake, you can build scalable enterprise-grade applications while maintaining access and privacy controls. The MCP server on Snowflake provides:\n83: Standardized integration: Unified interface for tool discovery and invocation, in compliance with the rapidly evolving standards.\n84: Comprehensive authentication: Snowflake OAuth by default, with optional External OAuth so MCP clients can authenticate against your organization’s identity provider.\n85: Robust governance: role-based access control (RBAC) for the MCP server and tools to manage tool discovery and invocation.\n86: For information about the MCP lifecycle, see Lifecycle. For an example of an MCP implementation, see the Getting Started with Managed Snowflake MCP Server Quickstart.\n87: The Cortex AI Cookbook has recipes that build on the managed MCP server:\n88: Snowflake-managed MCP or the Cortex Agents REST API: choose between MCP and the REST API, with identity, grant, topology, and rollout practices for MCP servers. Start here.\n89: Long-running Cortex Agent runs over managed MCP: keep agent runs inside an MCP client’s per-call time limit.\n90: LangChain agents on Cortex AI Gateway with MCP tools and traces: connect an external agent framework to an MCP server with a programmatic access token.\n91: For information about sharing MCP servers with other accounts, see Share Snowflake-managed MCP servers.\n92: MCP server security recommendations¶\n93: Important\n94: When you configure hostnames for MCP server connections, use hyphens (-) instead of underscores (_). MCP servers have connection issues with hostnames containing underscores. For an example, see MCP server URL.\n95: Using multiple MCP servers without verifying tools and descriptions could lead to vulnerabilities such as tool poisoning or tool shadowing. Snowflake recommends verifying third-party MCP servers before using them. This includes any MCP server from another Snowflake user or account. Verify all tools offered by third-party MCP servers.\n96: We recommend using OAuth as the authentication method. Using hardcoded tokens can lead to token leakage.\n97: When using a Programmatic Access Token (PAT), set it to use the least-privileged role allowed to work with MCP. This will help prevent leaking a secret with access to a highly-privileged role.\n98: Configure proper permissions for the MCP server and tools following the least-privilege principle. Access to the MCP Server does not give access to the tools. Permission needs to be granted for each tool.\n99: Tool results leave Snowflake. The answer, any data it contains, generated SQL, and citations go to the MCP client and, through the client, to its model provider, under that party’s terms. A tool returns only what the calling role can read, so decide what each tool may return before you expose it.\n100: Avoid configurations that can create recursive loops. For example, an external client calling a Cortex Agent tool through MCP, which in turn invokes another MCP server that calls back into a Cortex Agent, can produce expensive, unbounded loops. Snowflake enforces a maximum recursion depth of 10 invocations. Ensure your agent and tool configurations don’t create circular invocation paths.\n101: Create an MCP server object¶\n102: Create an object, specifying the tools and other metadata. MCP clients that connect with the server, after requisite authentication, are able to discover and invoke these tools.\n103: Navigate to the database and schema where you want to create the MCP server.\n104: Create the MCP server by using the following syntax:\n105: Copy code\n106: CREATE [ OR REPLACE ] MCP SERVER [ IF NOT EXISTS ] <server_name>\n107: FROM SPECIFICATION $$ <specification_yaml> $$;\n108: For business data applications that require governed orchestration, Snowflake recommends exposing a Cortex Agent as the client-facing MCP tool. Configure the agent with the Cortex Analyst, Cortex Search, and custom tools that it needs, and then expose the agent through the MCP server. This configuration gives the external MCP client one governed interface and lets the agent select the appropriate resources for each request.\n109: The following example exposes only a Cortex Agent:\n110: Copy code\n111: CREATE OR REPLACE MCP SERVER <database_name>.<schema_name>.<server_name>\n112: FROM SPECIFICATION $$\n113: tools:\n114: - title: \"Governed business data agent\"\n115: name: \"business_data_agent\"\n116: type: \"CORTEX_AGENT_RUN\"\n117: identifier: \"<database_name>.<schema_name>.<agent_name>\"\n118: description: \"Use this agent for governed business data questions.\"\n119: $$;\n120: When an MCP client sends a question, the client selects the agent based on its name and description. The MCP server passes the question to the agent. The agent then selects and orchestrates its configured Cortex Analyst, Cortex Search, or custom tools and returns the response to the client.\n121: Use precise, domain-specific names and descriptions when you expose multiple agents. This information helps the MCP client select the correct agent.\n122: Expose Cortex Analyst or Cortex Search directly when you want the external MCP client to select those resources independently. A directly exposed Cortex Analyst tool generates SQL and returns the statement to the client. A SQL execution tool runs queries without Cortex Agent orchestration.\n123: Configure tool types¶\n124: Snowflake currently supports the following tool types:\n125: CORTEX_AGENT_RUN: Cortex Agent tool\n126: CODING_AGENT: Coding Agent tool, with no stored agent object. Snowflake adds code_toolset_all on every run\n127: CORTEX_SEARCH_SERVICE_QUERY: Cortex Search Service tool\n128: CORTEX_ANALYST_MESSAGE: Cortex Analyst tool\n129: SYSTEM_EXECUTE_SQL: SQL execution\n130: GENERIC: tool for UDFs and stored procedures\n131: Configure tool annotations¶\n132: For GENERIC and CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tools, you can override the default MCP tool annotations that Snowflake returns to clients. These hints let clients distinguish tools that are read-only, destructive, idempotent, or able to interact with external entities.\n133: Add an annotations object to the tool definition and specify any of the following Boolean fields:\n134: readOnlyHint: The tool doesn’t modify its environment.\n135: destructiveHint: The tool might perform destructive updates. This hint applies only when readOnlyHint is false.\n136: idempotentHint: Repeated calls with the same arguments have the same effect as one call.\n137: openWorldHint: The tool might interact with external entities.\n138: You can omit fields that you don’t want to override. By default, GENERIC and CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tools use readOnlyHint: false, destructiveHint: true, idempotentHint: false, and openWorldHint: true.\n139: Important\n140: Tool annotations are client-facing hints, not security controls. Snowflake doesn’t validate that a tool behaves as its annotations claim. Use RBAC and least-privilege access to control the operations that the underlying UDF, stored procedure, or Cortex Agent can perform.\n141: The following examples show how to configure different tool types:\n142: Agent toolCoding agent toolAnalyst toolSearch toolSQL execution toolUDF / Stored Procedure\n143: For the Agent tool, your client passes a message to the agent. The agent processes the request and returns a response. Use the following code to specify the tool configuration.\n144: Copy code\n145: tools:\n146: - title: \"Governed business data agent\"\n147: name: \"business_data_agent\"\n148: type: \"CORTEX_AGENT_RUN\"\n149: identifier: \"<database_name>.<schema_name>.<agent_name>\"\n150: description: \"Answers governed business data questions by using configured Cortex Analyst and Cortex Search resources.\"\n151: annotations:\n152: openWorldHint: false\n153: The full agent tool response is available to MCP clients. See content_cel_filter to learn how clients can control which parts they retrieve.\n154: To route questions across several agents from one tool, set identifier to a router agent or a Snowflake CoWork profile. A profile is a router agent, so both work the same way. See Route questions between agents with the agent router and Call the profile from an MCP client.\n155: A coding agent tool runs a Cortex coding agent without a stored agent object. Add a tool with type CODING_AGENT to the MCP server specification. Snowflake adds the code_toolset_all tool on every run. That tool gives the agent a managed sandbox where it can write and run code. The sandbox stays attached to the conversation thread. See Coding Agent.\n156: Omit identifier. There is no stored agent to point at.\n157: config is optional. It can include any field of the Cortex Agents run request, such as model, instructions, tools, tool_resources, and skills.\n158: You can put other tools in config. Snowflake appends code_toolset_all after those tools. Do not also add code_execution. A run can include code_toolset_all or code_execution, but not both.\n159: The appended tool uses the default permission policy, always_ask. A state-modifying tool such as write, edit, or bash then waits for approval, and the MCP call ends before that tool runs. Set tool_resources.code_toolset_all.permission_policy.type to always_allow when the call should run those tools in one request. always_allow removes the approval gate: the agent runs any command and modifies any resource its role can reach. Use it only in a trusted automation. See Sandbox permission policy. To let the caller answer the prompt instead, see Answer a permission prompt.\n160: Copy code\n161: tools:\n162: - title: \"Coding agent\"\n163: name: \"ask_coding_agent\"\n164: type: \"CODING_AGENT\"\n165: description: \"Ask the coding agent. code_toolset_all gives the agent a managed sandbox where it can write and run code. That sandbox stays attached to the conversation thread.\"\n166: config:\n167: model: \"claude-sonnet-4-6\"\n168: instructions:\n169: response: \"Write and run code when that answers the question.\"\n170: tool_resources:\n171: code_toolset_all:\n172: permission_policy:\n173: type: always_allow\n174: Using the Analyst tool, your client can generate SQL from natural language text. Use the following code to specify the tool configuration.\n175: Note\n176: The Snowflake-managed MCP server only supports using semantic views with Cortex Analyst. It does not support semantic models.\n177: Copy code\n178: tools:\n179: - name: \"revenue-semantic-view\"\n180: type: \"CORTEX_ANALYST_MESSAGE\"\n181: identifier: \"database1.schema1.Semantic_View_1\"\n182: description: \"Semantic view for all revenue tables\"\n183: title: \"Semantic view for revenue\"\n184: Using the Search tool, your client can perform unstructured search on its data.\n185: Copy code\n186: tools:\n187: - name: \"product-search\"\n188: type: \"CORTEX_SEARCH_SERVICE_QUERY\"\n189: identifier: \"database1.schema1.Cortex_Search_Service1\"\n190: description: \"cortex search service for all products\"\n191: title: \"Product Search\"\n192: For the SQL execution tool, your client can execute SQL queries on Snowflake. You can optionally configure the following options:\n193: read_only: When set to true, only read operations (SELECT queries) are allowed. Defaults to true. If the CORTEX_MCP_SERVER_FORCE_SQL_EXEC_READ_ONLY account parameter is set to TRUE, the SQL execution tool is always read-only, regardless of this setting.\n194: query_timeout: Maximum time in seconds for query execution.\n195: warehouse: The warehouse to use for query execution. If not specified, the default warehouse is used.\n196: Important\n197: Snowflake generally recommends exposing a Cortex Agent as the only client-facing tool on a given MCP server used for governed business questions. Exposing SYSTEM_EXECUTE_SQL on the same server allows the MCP client to bypass the agent’s semantic views, verified queries, and orchestration; if direct SQL is required, expose it through a separate MCP server with a dedicated least-privileged role.\n198: Use the following code to specify the tool configuration:\n199: Copy code\n200: tools:\n201: - title: \"SQL Execution Tool\"\n202: name: \"sql_exec_tool\"\n203: type: \"SYSTEM_EXECUTE_SQL\"\n204: description: \"A tool to execute SQL queries against the connected Snowflake database.\"\n205: config:\n206: read_only: true\n207: query_timeout: 600\n208: warehouse: \"<warehouse_name>\"\n209: For your custom tools, you must provide the user-defined function (UDF) or stored procedure signature in the tool configuration. The custom tool enables you to invoke UDFs and stored procedures as tools through the MCP server.\n210: You can specify the following in the tool configuration:\n211: type: function for UDF, procedure for stored procedure\n212: warehouse: The warehouse to use. If you don’t specify a warehouse, the default warehouse is used.\n213: query_timeout: Maximum time in seconds for tool execution.\n214: input_schema: Corresponds to the function signature.\n215: Copy code\n216: tools:\n217: - name: \"my_custom_tool\"\n218: identifier: \"db.schema.my_function\"\n219: type: \"GENERIC\"\n220: description: \"Custom tool description\"\n221: annotations:\n222: readOnlyHint: true\n223: destructiveHint: false\n224: idempotentHint: true\n225: openWorldHint: false\n226: config:\n227: type: \"function\"\n228: query_timeout: 120\n229: warehouse: \"WAREHOUSE\"\n230: input_schema:\n231: type: \"object\"\n232: properties:\n233: query:\n234: type: \"string\"\n235: Use the following examples to create and configure custom tools using UDFs and stored procedures:\n236: UDF examplesStored procedure examplesTool configuration examples\n237: The following examples demonstrate creating UDFs that can be used as custom tools:\n238: Copy code\n239: -- create a simple udf\n240: CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION MULTIPLY_BY_TEN(x FLOAT)\n241: RETURNS FLOAT\n242: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n243: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n244: HANDLER = 'multiply_by_ten'\n245: AS\n246: $$\n247: def multiply_by_ten(x: float) -> float:\n248: return x * 10\n249: $$;\n250: SHOW FUNCTIONS LIKE 'MULTIPLY_BY_TEN';\n251: -- test return json/variant\n252: CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION CALCULATE_PRODUCT_AND_SUM(x FLOAT, y FLOAT)\n253: RETURNS VARIANT\n254: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n255: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n256: HANDLER = 'calculate_values'\n257: AS\n258: $$\n259: import json\n260: def calculate_values(x: float, y: float) -> dict:\n261: \"\"\"\n262: Calculates the product and sum of two numbers and returns them in a dictionary.\n263: The dictionary is converted to a VARIANT (JSON) in the SQL return.\n264: \"\"\"\n265: product = x * y\n266: sum_val = x + y\n267: return {\n268: \"product\": product,\n269: \"sum\": sum_val\n270: }\n271: $$;\n272: -- test return list/array\n273: CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION GET_NUMBERS_IN_RANGE(x FLOAT, y FLOAT)\n274: RETURNS ARRAY -- Use ARRAY to explicitly state a list is being returned\n275: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n276: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n277: HANDLER = 'get_numbers'\n278: AS\n279: $$\n280: def get_numbers(x: float, y: float) -> list:\n281: \"\"\"\n282: Returns a list of integers between x (exclusive) and y (inclusive).\n283: Assumes x < y.\n284: \"\"\"\n285: # Ensure x and y are treated as integers for range generation\n286: start = int(x) + 1\n287: end = int(y) + 1 # range() is exclusive on the stop value\n288: # Use a list comprehension to generate the numbers\n289: # The Python list will be converted to a Snowflake ARRAY.\n290: return list(range(start, end))\n291: $$;\n292: The following examples demonstrate creating stored procedures that can be used as custom tools:\n293: Copy code\n294: -- create a simple stored procedure\n295: CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE MULTIPLY_BY_TEN_SP(x FLOAT)\n296: RETURNS FLOAT\n297: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n298: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n299: PACKAGES = ('snowflake-snowpark-python')\n300: HANDLER = 'multiply_by_ten'\n301: AS\n302: $$\n303: # The handler logic is identical to the UDF for a scalar return\n304: def multiply_by_ten(x: float) -> float:\n305: return x * 10\n306: $$;\n307: -- test return json/variant\n308: CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE CALCULATE_VALUES_SP(x FLOAT, y FLOAT)\n309: RETURNS VARIANT\n310: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n311: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n312: PACKAGES = ('snowflake-snowpark-python')\n313: HANDLER = 'calculate_values'\n314: AS\n315: $$\n316: # The handler logic is identical to the UDF for a VARIANT return\n317: def calculate_values(x: float, y: float) -> dict:\n318: \"\"\"\n319: Calculates the product and sum of two numbers and returns them in a dictionary.\n320: The dictionary is converted to a VARIANT (JSON) in the SQL return.\n321: \"\"\"\n322: product = x * y\n323: sum_val = x + y\n324: return {\n325: \"product\": product,\n326: \"sum\": sum_val\n327: }\n328: $$;\n329: -- test return list/array\n330: CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE GET_NUMBERS_SP(x FLOAT, y FLOAT)\n331: RETURNS ARRAY\n332: LANGUAGE PYTHON\n333: RUNTIME_VERSION = '3.13'\n334: PACKAGES = ('snowflake-snowpark-python')\n335: HANDLER = 'get_numbers'\n336: AS\n337: $$\n338: def get_numbers(x: float, y: float) -> list:\n339: \"\"\"\n340: Returns a list of integers between x (exclusive) and y (inclusive).\n341: The Python list will be converted to a Snowflake ARRAY.\n342: \"\"\"\n343: # Ensure x and y are treated as integers for range generation\n344: start = int(x) + 1\n345: end = int(y) + 1 # range() is exclusive on the stop value\n346: # Use a list comprehension to generate the numbers\n347: return list(range(start, end))\n348: $$;\n349: The following examples demonstrate configuring custom tools for UDFs and stored procedures:\n350: Copy code\n351: CREATE MCP SERVER my_mcp_server\n352: FROM SPECIFICATION $$\n353: tools:\n354: - title: \"Custom Tool 1\"\n355: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.MULTIPLY_BY_TEN\"\n356: name: \"multiply_by_ten\"\n357: type: \"GENERIC\"\n358: description: \"Multiplies input value by ten and returns the result.\"\n359: config:\n360: type: \"function\"\n361: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n362: input_schema:\n363: type: \"object\"\n364: properties:\n365: x:\n366: description: \"A number to be multiplied by ten\"\n367: type: \"number\"\n368: - title: \"Custom Tool 2\"\n369: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.CALCULATE_PRODUCT_AND_SUM\"\n370: name: \"calculate_product_and_sum\"\n371: type: \"GENERIC\"\n372: description: \"Calculates the product and sum of two numbers and returns them in a JSON object.\"\n373: config:\n374: type: \"function\"\n375: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n376: input_schema:\n377: type: \"object\"\n378: properties:\n379: x:\n380: description: \"First number\"\n381: type: \"number\"\n382: y:\n383: description: \"Second number\"\n384: type: \"number\"\n385: - title: \"Custom Tool 3\"\n386: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.GET_NUMBERS_IN_RANGE\"\n387: name: \"get_numbers_in_range\"\n388: type: \"GENERIC\"\n389: description: \"Returns a list of integers between two numbers.\"\n390: config:\n391: type: \"function\"\n392: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n393: input_schema:\n394: type: \"object\"\n395: properties:\n396: x:\n397: description: \"Start number (exclusive)\"\n398: type: \"number\"\n399: y:\n400: description: \"End number (inclusive)\"\n401: type: \"number\"\n402: - title: \"Custom Tool 4\"\n403: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.MULTIPLY_BY_TEN_SP\"\n404: name: \"multiply_by_ten_sp\"\n405: type: \"GENERIC\"\n406: description: \"Multiplies input value by ten and returns the result.\"\n407: config:\n408: type: \"procedure\"\n409: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n410: input_schema:\n411: type: \"object\"\n412: properties:\n413: x:\n414: description: \"A number to be multiplied by ten\"\n415: type: \"number\"\n416: - title: \"Custom Tool 5\"\n417: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.CALCULATE_PRODUCT_AND_SUM_SP\"\n418: name: \"calculate_product_and_sum_sp\"\n419: type: \"GENERIC\"\n420: description: \"Calculates the product and sum of two numbers and returns them in a JSON object.\"\n421: config:\n422: type: \"procedure\"\n423: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n424: input_schema:\n425: type: \"object\"\n426: properties:\n427: x:\n428: description: \"First number\"\n429: type: \"number\"\n430: y:\n431: description: \"Second number\"\n432: type: \"number\"\n433: - title: \"Custom Tool 6\"\n434: identifier: \"EXAMPLE_DATABASE.AGENTS.GET_NUMBERS_IN_RANGE_SP\"\n435: name: \"get_numbers_in_range_sp\"\n436: type: \"GENERIC\"\n437: description: \"Returns a list of integers between two numbers.\"\n438: config:\n439: type: \"procedure\"\n440: warehouse: \"COMPUTE_SERVICE_WAREHOUSE\"\n441: input_schema:\n442: type: \"object\"\n443: properties:\n444: x:\n445: description: \"Start number (exclusive)\"\n446: type: \"number\"\n447: y:\n448: description: \"End number (inclusive)\"\n449: type: \"number\"\n450: $$;\n451: Serve every agent the caller can use¶\n452: Use selection: \"all\" to let the MCP client choose from every agent the user can access, up to 50 per user. New agents appear as soon as you grant USAGE on them, with no change to the server. Because grants alone decide which agents each user sees, grant USAGE on an agent only to the roles that should use it from MCP clients. To choose which agents are offered, or to write their tool names and descriptions, list them in tools instead.\n453: To serve every Cortex Agent that the caller’s role can use, without listing each agent in tools, add a tool_config entry to the specification:\n454: Copy code\n455: CREATE OR REPLACE MCP SERVER <database_name>.<schema_name>.<server_name>\n456: FROM SPECIFICATION $$\n457: tool_config:\n458: - type: \"CORTEX_AGENT_RUN\"\n459: selection: \"all\"\n460: $$;\n461: On each tools/list request, Snowflake generates one CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tool for each active Cortex Agent in the account that the caller’s role has the USAGE privilege on. Temporary agents aren’t included. Snowflake checks privileges again on every request, so a granted or revoked agent shows up on the client’s next tools/list request.\n462: Each generated tool is built from the agent:\n463: Name: sf_ followed by the lowercase database, schema, and agent names, separated by hyphens. For example, the agent PROD_DB.AGENTS.SALES_AGENT becomes sf_prod_db-agents-sales_agent.\n464: Title: The display name from the agent’s profile, followed by the database and schema, for example Sales Bot (PROD_DB.AGENTS). If the agent has no display name, the title is the agent name.\n465: Description: The agent’s comment, followed by a line with the agent’s fully qualified name.\n466: Arguments: The same arguments as an explicit agent tool. See Call an agent tool.\n467: Snowflake skips an agent in these cases:\n468: The database, schema, or agent name must be enclosed in double quotes, or contains $.\n469: The generated name would be longer than 64 characters.\n470: An explicit tool in tools already runs the agent, or already uses the generated name.\n471: To serve a skipped agent, add it to tools as an explicit CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tool. Explicit tools are listed before generated tools. If the specification has a tool_config entry, explicit tool names can’t start with sf_.\n472: A server lists at most 50 generated tools for a caller, and explicit tools don’t count toward this limit. To find agents, Snowflake examines at most 1,000 agents per request. A client can still call a generated tool by name when the agent didn’t fit in the list. The instructions that the server returns from initialize and server/discover tell the client about the limit and the skipped agents.\n473: If the caller’s role can’t use the agent, or the agent doesn’t exist, a call to a generated tool name returns Unknown tool: <name>.\n474: Help the client pick the right agent. The client chooses an agent based on each tool’s name and description. If a user can access several agents that cover similar topics, the client might not always pick the best one. To help it choose, give each agent a clear comment, or list a smaller set as explicit CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tools with your own names and descriptions.\n475: Set a comment on each agent. The generated description is the agent’s comment, followed by the line Snowflake Cortex Agent: <fully qualified name>. An agent without a comment has only that line, which doesn’t tell the client what the agent is for. In the comment, say what the agent answers, what data it covers, and when not to use it. Snowflake truncates a comment that is too long, so put the most important information first. The display name in the agent’s profile sets the title, not the description, so it doesn’t replace the comment.\n476: For example, the agent SALES_DB.AGENTS.REVENUE_AGENT has this comment:\n477: Answers questions about quarterly revenue, bookings, and pipeline by region. Don't use it for support tickets or product usage.\n478: The client receives this description:\n479: Answers questions about quarterly revenue, bookings, and pipeline by region. Don't use it for support tickets or product usage.\n480: Snowflake Cortex Agent: SALES_DB.AGENTS.REVENUE_AGENT\n481: For an MCP server in a Snowflake Native App, Snowflake generates tools only for agents that have the same owner as the MCP server. See Use Cortex Agents and MCP servers in an app.\n482: Manage MCP server objects¶\n483: After you create an MCP server, use the following commands to inspect or remove it:\n484: To show MCP servers, use the following commands:\n485: Copy code\n486: SHOW MCP SERVERS IN DATABASE <database_name>;\n487: SHOW MCP SERVERS IN SCHEMA <schema_name>;\n488: SHOW MCP SERVERS IN ACCOUNT;\n489: The following shows the output of the command:\n490: | created_on | name | database_name | schema_name | owner | comment |\n491: ------------------------------------------+-------------------+---------------+-------------+--------------+------------------------------\n492: | Fri, 23 Jun 1967 07:00:00.123000 +0000 | TEST_MCP_SERVER | TEST_DATABASE | TEST_SCHEMA | ACCOUNTADMIN | [NULL] |\n493: | Fri, 23 Jun 1967 07:00:00.123000 +0000 | TEST_MCP_SERVER_2 | TEST_DATABASE | TEST_SCHEMA | ACCOUNTADMIN | Test MCP server with comment |\n494: To describe an MCP server, use the following command:\n495: Copy code\n496: DESCRIBE MCP SERVER <server_name>;\n497: The following shows the output of the command:\n498: | name | database_name | schema_name | owner | comment | server_spec | created_on |\n499: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------\n500: | TEST_MCP_SERVER | TEST_DATABASE | TEST_SCHEMA | ACCOUNTADMIN | [NULL] | {\"version\":1,\"tools\":[{\"name\":\"product-search\",\"identifier\":\"db.schema.search_service\",\"type\":\"CORTEX_SEARCH_SERVICE_QUERY\"}]} | Fri, 23 Jun 1967 07:00:00.123000 +0000 |\n501: To drop an MCP server, use the following command:\n502: Copy code\n503: DROP MCP SERVER <server_name>;\n504: There’s no ALTER command for a Snowflake-managed MCP server. To change its specification, run CREATE OR REPLACE MCP SERVER with the full new specification. Replacing a server drops every grant on it, so put the GRANT USAGE ON MCP SERVER statements in the same script as the CREATE OR REPLACE statement and run them together.\n505: MCP server URL¶\n506: To connect to the MCP server, use the URL endpoint with the following format:\n507: Copy code\n508: https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/{database}/schemas/{schema}/mcp-servers/{name}\n509: For information about formatting your account URL, see Account identifiers.\n510: Use the account URL that combines your organization name and account name. If the account name contains underscores, replace each underscore with a hyphen; Snowflake accepts this form automatically, and MCP clients have connection issues with hostnames that contain underscores. For example, for the organization acme and the account marketing_test_account:\n511: Use: https://acme-marketing-test-account.snowflakecomputing.com/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n512: Don’t use: https://acme-marketing_test_account.snowflakecomputing.com/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n513: Access control¶\n514: Required privileges¶\n515: You can use the following privileges to manage access to the MCP server and the underlying tools.\n516: PrivilegeObjectDescription\n517: CREATEMCP SERVERRequired to create the MCP server\n518: OWNERSHIPMCP SERVERRequired to update the object configuration\n519: MODIFYMCP SERVERProvides drop and describe on the MCP server. Doesn’t allow tools/list or tools/call; those require USAGE\n520: USAGEMCP SERVERRequired to connect with the MCP server, discover tools (tools/list), and call tools (tools/call)\n521: USAGECortex Search ServiceRequired to invoke the Cortex Search tool in the MCP server\n522: SELECTSemantic ViewRequired to invoke the Cortex Analyst tool in the MCP server\n523: USAGECortex AgentRequired to invoke the Cortex Agent as a tool in the MCP server\n524: USAGEUser-defined function (UDF) or stored procedureRequired to invoke the UDF or stored procedure as a tool in the MCP server\n525: Expand\n526: Show lessSee more\n527: Access requirements for the SQL execution tool¶\n528: To invoke the SQL execution tool (SYSTEM_EXECUTE_SQL), the role used to connect to the MCP server needs the following, in addition to USAGE on the MCP server:\n529: The SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX_USER or SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX_AGENT_USER database role. The SQL execution tool runs through the Cortex Agents API, so calls from a role that has neither database role fail with an authorization error.\n530: USAGE on the warehouse that runs the queries. The tool uses the warehouse set in the tool configuration, or, if no warehouse is set, the default warehouse of the user who connects.\n531: Privileges on the objects that the SQL statements reference. Queries run with the caller’s role, so, for example, the role needs SELECT to read a table, and INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE to modify it when read_only is false and the CORTEX_MCP_SERVER_FORCE_SQL_EXEC_READ_ONLY account parameter isn’t set to TRUE.\n532: Grant access to a Cortex Agent-based MCP server¶\n533: The following example creates a dedicated access role and grants it access to an MCP server that exposes a Cortex Agent:\n534: Copy code\n535: CREATE ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n536: GRANT DATABASE ROLE SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX_AGENT_USER TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n537: GRANT USAGE ON WAREHOUSE <warehouse_name> TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n538: GRANT USAGE ON DATABASE <database_name> TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n539: GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA <database_name>.<schema_name> TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n540: GRANT USAGE ON MCP SERVER <database_name>.<schema_name>.<server_name> TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n541: GRANT USAGE ON AGENT <database_name>.<schema_name>.<agent_name> TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n542: GRANT ROLE <mcp_access_role> TO USER <username>;\n543: tools/list omits CORTEX_AGENT_RUN tools when the active role does not have USAGE on the referenced agent.\n544: The role also needs privileges on the resources configured for the agent. Grant only the privileges for the resources that the agent uses. For example:\n545: Copy code\n546: GRANT USAGE ON CORTEX SEARCH SERVICE <database_name>.<schema_name>.<search_service_name>\n547: TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n548: GRANT SELECT ON SEMANTIC VIEW <database_name>.<schema_name>.<semantic_view_name>\n549: TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n550: GRANT SELECT ON TABLE <database_name>.<schema_name>.<table_name>\n551: TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n552: GRANT USAGE ON FUNCTION <database_name>.<schema_name>.<function_name>(<argument_type>)\n553: TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n554: GRANT USAGE ON PROCEDURE <database_name>.<schema_name>.<procedure_name>(<argument_type>)\n555: TO ROLE <mcp_access_role>;\n556: For more information about agent privileges and the privileges required by agent tools, see Access control and authentication.\n557: Grant the MCP access role only to users who need it. Don’t grant the role to PUBLIC or grant broad access to all current and future tables solely to support an MCP client.\n558: Set up OAuth authentication¶\n559: Configure authentication on the MCP client. The Snowflake-managed MCP server supports OAuth 2.0 aligned with the authorization recommendation in the MCP protocol. The Snowflake-managed MCP server doesn’t support dynamic client registration.\n560: By default, MCP servers use Snowflake OAuth. To bind MCP servers to an External OAuth identity provider such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, see Configure External OAuth authentication.\n561: A single OAuth security integration (client ID and secret) can be shared across all users in an account. Each user still authenticates individually with their own credentials to obtain an access token, but the client ID and secret from the integration are the same for everyone. A single integration can also issue tokens that work across multiple MCP servers within the same account.\n562: Note\n563: If your Snowflake account uses PrivateLink and you are connecting from a SaaS MCP client\n564: (such as Claude.ai or ChatGPT), configure your MCP client with the public MCP server\n565: URL, not the PrivateLink URL. Additionally, enable\n566: USE_PRIVATELINK_FOR_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT = TRUE on your OAuth security integration.\n567: This causes Snowflake to redirect the user’s browser to the PrivateLink authorization\n568: endpoint while the token endpoint remains on the public URL so the SaaS vendor’s server\n569: can reach it. For details, see\n570: Using Snowflake OAuth with PrivateLink and SaaS clients.\n571: First, create the security integration. For information about this command, see CREATE SECURITY INTEGRATION (Snowflake OAuth).\n572: Use OAUTH_CLIENT_TYPE = 'CONFIDENTIAL' when the client can store and send a client secret, such as VS Code or Cursor. Use OAUTH_CLIENT_TYPE = 'PUBLIC' when the client can’t send a client secret and uses PKCE instead, such as Claude Desktop. For Claude Desktop, set the redirect URI to https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback.\n573: Confidential client:\n574: Copy code\n575: CREATE [ OR REPLACE ] SECURITY INTEGRATION [IF NOT EXISTS] <integration_name>\n576: TYPE = OAUTH\n577: OAUTH_CLIENT = CUSTOM\n578: ENABLED = TRUE\n579: OAUTH_CLIENT_TYPE = 'CONFIDENTIAL'\n580: OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI = '<redirect_URI>'\n581: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE\n582: ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST = ('<mcp_access_role>')\n583: Public client (PKCE):\n584: Copy code\n585: CREATE [ OR REPLACE ] SECURITY INTEGRATION [IF NOT EXISTS] <integration_name>\n586: TYPE = OAUTH\n587: OAUTH_CLIENT = CUSTOM\n588: ENABLED = TRUE\n589: OAUTH_CLIENT_TYPE = 'PUBLIC'\n590: OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI = 'https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback'\n591: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE\n592: ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST = ('<mcp_access_role>')\n593: ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST restricts the integration to the roles intended for MCP access. If the integration serves MCP servers with different access roles, include each role in the list.\n594: If your MCP client requires multiple redirect URIs (for example, VS Code registers more than one callback URL),\n595: use OAUTH_ALTERNATE_REDIRECT_URIS to specify additional URIs:\n596: Copy code\n597: CREATE [ OR REPLACE ] SECURITY INTEGRATION [IF NOT EXISTS] <integration_name>\n598: TYPE = OAUTH\n599: OAUTH_CLIENT = CUSTOM\n600: ENABLED = TRUE\n601: OAUTH_CLIENT_TYPE = 'CONFIDENTIAL'\n602: OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI = '<primary_redirect_URI>'\n603: OAUTH_ALTERNATE_REDIRECT_URIS = ('<alternate_URI_1>', '<alternate_URI_2>')\n604: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE\n605: ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST = ('<mcp_access_role>')\n606: Then, call the system function to retrieve your client ID and keys for client configuration. The integration name is case sensitive and must be in uppercase.\n607: Copy code\n608: SELECT SYSTEM$SHOW_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRETS('<integration_name>');\n609: Role behavior in OAuth sessions¶\n610: OAuth scopes control only the primary role for the MCP session. Secondary roles are separate\n611: and are controlled by the OAuth security integration, not by OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED.\n612: By default, MCP servers advertise session:role:all in Protected Resource Metadata, and the OAuth session uses the connecting user’s DEFAULT_ROLE as the primary role.\n613: Special primary-role scopes:\n614: session:role:all: Use the user’s DEFAULT_ROLE as the primary role. Despite the name, this\n615: doesn’t activate every role or secondary roles.\n616: session:role-any: Allow any primary role granted to the user, when any-role mode is enabled on\n617: the authorization server. For External OAuth, that requires\n618: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_ANY_ROLE_MODE = ENABLE or ENABLE_FOR_PRIVILEGE on the security integration.\n619: session:role:<role_name>: Use that named role as the primary role.\n620: To control which primary-role scopes MCP clients discover, set the OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED parameter at the account, database, or schema level. This parameter is independent of External OAuth: set it alone to customize scopes for Snowflake OAuth, or set it together with OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER when you bind MCP servers to an external identity provider (IdP). For accepted scope values and SET-time validation rules, see OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED.\n621: Copy code\n622: ALTER SCHEMA analytics_db.mcp_schema\n623: SET OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED = 'session:role:ANALYST,session:role:DATA_ENGINEER';\n624: Whether the advertised primary role is used in the session depends on the MCP client:\n625: Clients that request one of the advertised session:role:<role_name> scopes use that role as the primary role for the session.\n626: Some MCP clients (such as Claude) don’t support specifying a role in the OAuth scope and request session:role:all instead, so the session uses the user’s DEFAULT_ROLE even when other scopes are advertised.\n627: Secondary roles aren’t controlled by OAuth scopes. For Snowflake OAuth, they follow\n628: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES on the security integration:\n629: Recommended for MCP: leave OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE (the default) and restrict\n630: ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST to the MCP access role, so the session uses only the user’s\n631: DEFAULT_ROLE as the primary role. See Prefer a least-privileged MCP OAuth configuration.\n632: To activate the user’s default secondary roles when the session opens, set\n633: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = IMPLICIT. Snowflake OAuth doesn’t support in-session secondary\n634: role switching with USE SECONDARY ROLES. For details,\n635: see CREATE SECURITY INTEGRATION (Snowflake OAuth).\n636: For External OAuth, see Using secondary roles with External OAuth.\n637: To ensure the correct primary role is used when clients rely on session:role:all or the user’s DEFAULT_ROLE:\n638: Set each user’s DEFAULT_ROLE to the role that has the required privileges on the MCP server and its tools.\n639: Ensure each user has a DEFAULT_WAREHOUSE set (sessions fail to initialize if this is null).\n640: Copy code\n641: ALTER USER <username> SET DEFAULT_ROLE = '<mcp_access_role>' DEFAULT_WAREHOUSE = '<warehouse_name>';\n642: If you need different data access levels per user and clients don’t honor role scopes, use separate MCP servers with dedicated roles.\n643: Note\n644: If an MCP client (such as Claude) requests the session:role:all OAuth scope, the consent screen\n645: might display “secondary roles = ALL” even when your security integration has\n646: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE. That label is cosmetic and doesn’t mean the scope activates\n647: secondary roles. Snowflake enforces the security integration setting regardless of what the client\n648: requests, so default secondary roles aren’t activated unless you set\n649: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = IMPLICIT.\n650: Configure External OAuth authentication¶\n651: By default, MCP servers use Snowflake OAuth for authentication. To let MCP clients authenticate against your organization’s existing identity provider (IdP) such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID, set OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER to bind MCP servers to an External OAuth security integration. Optionally set OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED to advertise specific scopes in Protected Resource Metadata. You can also set OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED on its own for Snowflake OAuth; see Role behavior in OAuth sessions.\n652: When an External OAuth integration is bound to a schema, database, or account, Snowflake advertises the external issuer’s metadata in Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728). MCP clients that support OAuth resource metadata discovery fetch this information automatically to find the correct authorization server and scopes.\n653: Default behavior¶\n654: When neither parameter is set, MCP servers use Snowflake OAuth and advertise session:role:all as the supported scope.\n655: Steps to configure External OAuth for MCP servers¶\n656: Create an External OAuth security integration for your IdP. For details, see CREATE SECURITY INTEGRATION (External OAuth).\n657: Copy code\n658: CREATE OR REPLACE SECURITY INTEGRATION external_oauth_okta\n659: TYPE = EXTERNAL_OAUTH\n660: ENABLED = TRUE\n661: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_TYPE = OKTA\n662: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_ISSUER = '<OKTA_ISSUER>'\n663: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_JWS_KEYS_URL = '<OKTA_JWS_KEY_ENDPOINT>'\n664: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_AUDIENCE_LIST = ('https://<orgname>-<account_name>.snowflakecomputing.com')\n665: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_TOKEN_USER_MAPPING_CLAIM = 'sub'\n666: EXTERNAL_OAUTH_SNOWFLAKE_USER_MAPPING_ATTRIBUTE = 'login_name';\n667: Set OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER at the account, database, or schema level to bind MCP servers in that scope to the integration. For parameter details, see OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER.\n668: Copy code\n669: -- Schema level (applies to all MCP servers in the schema)\n670: ALTER SCHEMA analytics_db.mcp_schema SET OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER = external_oauth_okta;\n671: -- Database level (applies to all MCP servers in the database)\n672: ALTER DATABASE analytics_db SET OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER = external_oauth_okta;\n673: -- Account level (applies to all MCP servers in the account)\n674: ALTER ACCOUNT SET OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER = external_oauth_okta;\n675: Optionally, set OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED to advertise specific scopes in the Protected Resource Metadata response. This parameter is independent of OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER: you can set it alone for Snowflake OAuth, or together with an External OAuth binding as shown here. For accepted scope values, see OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED. For how scopes interact with primary and secondary roles, see Role behavior in OAuth sessions.\n676: Copy code\n677: ALTER SCHEMA analytics_db.mcp_schema SET OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED = 'session:role:ANALYST,session:role:DATA_ENGINEER';\n678: Create the MCP server. The server inherits the OAuth binding from the enclosing schema, database, or account.\n679: Copy code\n680: CREATE MCP SERVER analytics_db.mcp_schema.docs_mcp_server\n681: FROM SPECIFICATION $$\n682: tools:\n683: - name: \"docs-search\"\n684: type: \"CORTEX_SEARCH_SERVICE_QUERY\"\n685: identifier: \"analytics_db.mcp_schema.docs_search_service\"\n686: description: \"Search service for internal documentation\"\n687: title: \"Documentation Search\"\n688: $$;\n689: When a client connects to docs_mcp_server, Snowflake returns a 401 response with a WWW-Authenticate header pointing to the Protected Resource Metadata endpoint. The client fetches the metadata document, which advertises the Okta issuer URL from external_oauth_okta in authorization_servers, and then initiates the Okta OAuth flow. After obtaining a token, the client retries the request. Snowflake accepts the OAuth token only when it was issued by the bound External OAuth integration; a Snowflake OAuth token, or a token from a different External OAuth integration, is rejected. This issuer check applies only to OAuth calls against MCP server endpoints; other authenticators and non-MCP endpoints aren’t affected.\n690: Parameter inheritance¶\n691: OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER and OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED follow Snowflake’s standard parameter inheritance. Each parameter resolves independently by checking the following levels in order:\n692: Schema\n693: Database\n694: Account\n695: This means you can set a default authorization server at the account level and override it for specific schemas, or have some schemas use External OAuth while others use Snowflake OAuth (no parameter set).\n696: When OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER is set and OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED isn’t, Snowflake doesn’t advertise session:role:all. Instead:\n697: If the bound integration has EXTERNAL_OAUTH_ANY_ROLE_MODE = ENABLE or ENABLE_FOR_PRIVILEGE, the Protected Resource Metadata response includes session:role-any.\n698: Otherwise, the Protected Resource Metadata response includes an empty scopes_supported list, and the MCP client uses its own configured scopes or prompts the user.\n699: Failure modes¶\n700: The following conditions change what Snowflake advertises in the Protected Resource Metadata response, and how token enforcement behaves:\n701: ConditionBehavior\n702: The integration named in OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER is dropped\n703: The Protected Resource Metadata response includes an empty authorization_servers list.\n704: Snowflake doesn’t fall back to Snowflake OAuth. Inbound OAuth requests to MCP servers in that\n705: scope are rejected.\n706: The integration is disabledSame as when the integration is dropped.\n707: A role specified in OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED is dropped or renamed\n708: Snowflake continues to publish the configured scope string in Protected Resource Metadata.\n709: Authentication using that scope fails during the OAuth flow.\n710: OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED is set but OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER isn’tThe scopes apply to Snowflake OAuth discovery.\n711: Expand\n712: Show lessSee more\n713: For information about External OAuth, see External OAuth overview.\n714: For information about the parameters, see OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER and OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED.\n715: Prefer a least-privileged MCP OAuth configuration¶\n716: Snowflake recommends configuring MCP OAuth so the session uses the connecting user’s\n717: DEFAULT_ROLE without activating default secondary roles. That pattern matches the\n718: OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE and ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST settings in the earlier CREATE examples.\n719: Secondary roles still work when you set OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = IMPLICIT; the\n720: recommendation is to avoid that for MCP unless you intentionally need broader privileges.\n721: If an existing MCP OAuth integration uses OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = IMPLICIT, update it as follows:\n722: Configure or select a Cortex Agent with the governed resources that the client needs.\n723: Update the primary MCP server to expose the agent. Remove direct SQL execution from that server unless the client requires it.\n724: Create or select a least-privileged MCP access role and grant access to the MCP server, agent, and agent resources.\n725: Set the access role and warehouse as the user’s defaults.\n726: Disable secondary roles and restrict the integration to the MCP access role:\n727: Copy code\n728: ALTER SECURITY INTEGRATION <integration_name>\n729: SET OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE;\n730: ALTER SECURITY INTEGRATION <integration_name>\n731: SET ALLOWED_ROLES_LIST = ('<mcp_access_role>');\n732: Reconnect the MCP client and verify the effective role, visible tools, agent routing, and resource access.\n733: Connect from common MCP clients¶\n734: Once you have created the MCP server and the OAuth security integration, you can connect from any MCP-compatible client by pointing it at your MCP server URL:\n735: Copy code\n736: https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n737: Important\n738: When you configure the account URL in an MCP client, use hyphens (-) instead of underscores (_) in the hostname. MCP servers have connection issues with hostnames containing underscores. For an example, see MCP server URL.\n739: The following examples show how to register the Snowflake-hosted MCP server in commonly used clients. Replace the URL with your own MCP server URL.\n740: Claude.ai / Claude DesktopChatGPTCursorOther clients\n741: Claude (both claude.ai and Claude Desktop) supports Snowflake MCP servers in the Claude Directory or as a Custom Connector. Claude handles the OAuth flow against the security integration you created above. For Anthropic’s general guidance, see Get started with custom connectors using remote MCP.\n742: In Claude, open Settings → Connectors.\n743: Click Add custom connector or search for Snowflake in Browse connectors.\n744: Provide a Name (for example, Snowflake) and the MCP Server URL:\n745: Copy code\n746: https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n747: Add the client ID and secret from the security integration you created.\n748: Click Add. Claude opens a browser window and prompts you to sign in to Snowflake and approve the OAuth consent screen.\n749: After approving, the Snowflake tools appear in the connector list and can be used in any Claude conversation.\n750: Note\n751: When configuring the OAuth security integration for Claude, set OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI to the redirect URI shown by Claude during connector setup (typically https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback for claude.ai and a localhost URI for Claude Desktop). Claude requests the session:role:all scope; the session still uses the user’s DEFAULT_ROLE.\n752: ChatGPT supports remote MCP servers as Connectors in Developer Mode. For OpenAI’s general guidance on adding remote MCP servers, see Remote MCP servers in the OpenAI documentation.\n753: In ChatGPT, open Settings → Connectors → Advanced, and enable Developer mode.\n754: Return to Settings → Connectors and click Create.\n755: Fill in the connector form:\n756: Name: Snowflake (or any label).\n757: MCP Server URL:\n758: Copy code\n759: https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n760: Authentication: select OAuth.\n761: Fill in the client ID and secret from the security integration you created.\n762: Click Create. ChatGPT opens a browser window for you to sign in to Snowflake and approve the OAuth consent screen.\n763: The Snowflake tools become available in chats that have the connector enabled.\n764: Note\n765: Set OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI on your security integration to the redirect URI ChatGPT displays during connector creation.\n766: Cursor supports remote MCP servers via mcp.json. For the full Cursor configuration reference, see Model Context Protocol in the Cursor documentation. You can also browse for the Snowflake plugin in the Cursor marketplace.\n767: Edit ~/.cursor/mcp.json (global) or <project>/.cursor/mcp.json (project) and add an entry under mcpServers:\n768: Copy code\n769: {\n770: \"mcpServers\": {\n771: \"snowflake\": {\n772: \"url\": \"https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\",\n773: \"auth\": {\n774: \"CLIENT_ID\": \"${env:MCP_CLIENT_ID}\",\n775: \"CLIENT_SECRET\": \"${env:MCP_CLIENT_SECRET}\"\n776: }\n777: }\n778: }\n779: }\n780: After saving, open Cursor Settings → MCP, locate the snowflake server, and click Sign in. Cursor opens a browser window to complete the Snowflake OAuth flow. Once connected, the Snowflake tools appear in Cursor’s tool list.\n781: For any other MCP client that supports remote (HTTP) MCP servers, register the Snowflake-hosted MCP server using your URL:\n782: Copy code\n783: https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n784: If the client accepts custom HTTP headers instead of OAuth, see Connect with a programmatic access token.\n785: Connect with a programmatic access token¶\n786: Snowflake recommends OAuth for MCP clients that people sign in to. For a client that can’t use OAuth but can send custom HTTP headers, such as a script, an agent framework, or a service, you can authenticate with a programmatic access token (PAT) instead. Send these headers on every request:\n787: Authorization: Bearer <token_secret>\n788: X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type: PROGRAMMATIC_ACCESS_TOKEN (optional)\n789: Content-Type: application/json\n790: Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\n791: The following example lists the tools on a server:\n792: Copy code\n793: curl -X POST \"https://<account_url>/api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\" \\\n794: -H \"Authorization: Bearer $SNOWFLAKE_PAT\" \\\n795: -H \"X-Snowflake-Authorization-Token-Type: PROGRAMMATIC_ACCESS_TOKEN\" \\\n796: -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n797: -H \"Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\" \\\n798: -d '{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"method\": \"tools/list\", \"params\": {}}'\n799: Before you use a PAT with an MCP server:\n800: Make sure the user is subject to a network policy that allows the client’s IP addresses. A service user can’t generate or use a PAT without one, and a person user can generate a PAT without one but can’t authenticate with it. An authentication policy with a programmatic access token policy can change this requirement. For details, see Network policy requirements.\n801: Restrict the token to the least-privileged role that can use the MCP server and its tools. See Configuring role restrictions for programmatic access tokens.\n802: Keep the token out of client configuration files that might be shared or committed to source control. Read it from an environment variable or a secret store where the client supports it.\n803: Network policies for MCP clients¶\n804: If your Snowflake account has network policies enabled, you must allow inbound connections from your MCP client’s outbound IP addresses. When a remote MCP client (such as Claude, ChatGPT, or Cursor) connects to your Snowflake-managed MCP server, the request originates from the client provider’s infrastructure, not from the end user’s browser. If those IP addresses are not permitted by your network policy, the connection will be blocked.\n805: When a network policy blocks the token request, Snowflake can return error: invalid_client from the /oauth/token-request endpoint. That\n806: error is the same response clients see for incorrect credentials or an unsupported authentication method, so check your network policy if\n807: client credentials and authentication method look correct.\n808: To allow an MCP client to reach your Snowflake account, create a network rule that includes the client provider’s outbound IP addresses, then add that rule to your account’s network policy:\n809: Copy code\n810: CREATE NETWORK RULE mcp_client_ingress_rule\n811: MODE = INGRESS\n812: TYPE = IPV4\n813: VALUE_LIST = ('<client_provider_ip_1>', '<client_provider_ip_2>', ...);\n814: ALTER NETWORK POLICY <your_policy_name> ADD ALLOWED_NETWORK_RULE_LIST = ('mcp_client_ingress_rule');\n815: Replace the IP addresses with the outbound IPs published by your MCP client provider. For example, Anthropic publishes the outbound IP addresses used by Claude at https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/ip-addresses.\n816: Note\n817: This applies to all MCP client providers, not just Claude. Check your provider’s documentation for their outbound IP addresses used for remote MCP connections.\n818: For Snowflake OAuth client authentication at the token endpoint, see\n819: Configure Snowflake OAuth for custom clients.\n820: Troubleshoot MCP client connections¶\n821: If your MCP client can’t connect or a tool doesn’t behave as expected, use this table to identify the likely cause:\n822: SymptomLikely causeRecommended check\n823: OAuth consent or connection failsThe redirect URI doesn’t match the client configurationSet OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI to the exact callback URI shown by the MCP client.\n824: Authentication succeeds, but the MCP server doesn’t connectThe MCP server URL is incompleteUse the fully qualified database, schema, and MCP server path.\n825: The client reports a hostname-related connection failureThe account hostname contains underscoresReplace underscores (_) with hyphens (-) in the account hostname.\n826: The session fails to initializeThe user doesn’t have a default warehouseSet DEFAULT_WAREHOUSE on the user and grant the default role USAGE on that warehouse.\n827: The session uses the wrong data access roleThe user’s default role isn’t the MCP access roleSet and verify the user’s DEFAULT_ROLE.\n828: Tools aren’t visibleThe default role doesn’t have access to the MCP serverGrant USAGE on the MCP server to the user’s default role.\n829: A visible tool can’t be invokedThe default role is missing an underlying object privilegeGrant the privilege required by the agent, search service, semantic view, function, or procedure.\n830: A SaaS client can’t reach a PrivateLink accountThe client is using a private endpointUse the public MCP server URL and set USE_PRIVATELINK_FOR_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT = TRUE on the OAuth integration.\n831: A remote MCP client is blocked by a network policyThe client’s outbound IP addresses aren’t allowedAdd the MCP client provider’s outbound IP addresses to the account network policy.\n832: Expand\n833: Show lessSee more\n834: Interact with the MCP server using a custom MCP client¶\n835: For information about building a custom MCP client, see Build an MCP client.\n836: Note\n837: The Snowflake MCP server currently only supports tool capabilities.\n838: Discover and invoke tools¶\n839: The MCP clients can discover and invoke tools with tools/list and tools/call requests.\n840: A tools/list entry has name, description, and inputSchema. title is present when the specification sets it. annotations is present by default. The server does not return outputSchema.\n841: To discover or invoke tools, send a JSON-RPC request in a POST call to the MCP server URL. For the request format, see Tools in the MCP specification.\n842: The following example lists the tools on the server:\n843: Copy code\n844: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n845: {\n846: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n847: \"id\": 1,\n848: \"method\": \"tools/list\",\n849: \"params\": {}\n850: }\n851: The following example shows the shape of the response:\n852: Copy code\n853: {\n854: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n855: \"id\": 1,\n856: \"result\": {\n857: \"tools\": [\n858: {\n859: \"name\": \"<tool_name>\",\n860: \"title\": \"<tool_title>\",\n861: \"description\": \"<tool_description>\",\n862: \"inputSchema\": {\n863: \"type\": \"object\",\n864: \"properties\": {}\n865: },\n866: \"annotations\": {}\n867: }\n868: ]\n869: }\n870: }\n871: As of August 20, 2026, the server returns tools/call responses as a\n872: Server-Sent Events (SSE)\n873: stream rather than a single JSON body. Your client must list both content types in the Accept header:\n874: Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\n875: The response examples in this section show the JSON payload that the server sends in each data: event.\n876: While the tool runs, the server sends an SSE comment line (: keep-alive) about every 5 seconds so that proxies don’t close the idle connection.\n877: If the request sets _meta.progressToken, the server also sends a notifications/progress event with each keep-alive.\n878: The last event in the stream is the complete JSON-RPC response for the request; the server doesn’t send a separate end-of-stream event.\n879: Clients that follow the MCP specification (2025-11-25)\n880: handle this automatically. For details, see\n881: MCP: Streaming responses for tool calls (August 2026).\n882: For the Analyst tool, your client passes messages in the request. The SQL statement is listed in the output. You must pass the name of the tool that you’re invoking in the request in the name parameter.\n883: Copy code\n884: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n885: {\n886: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n887: \"id\": 1,\n888: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n889: \"params\": {\n890: \"name\": \"test-analyst\",\n891: \"arguments\": {\n892: \"message\": \"text\"\n893: }\n894: }\n895: }\n896: The following example shows the response:\n897: Copy code\n898: {\n899: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n900: \"id\": 1,\n901: \"result\": {\n902: \"content\": [\n903: {\n904: \"type\": \"text\",\n905: \"text\": \"string\"\n906: }\n907: ]\n908: }\n909: }\n910: For Search tool requests, your client can pass the query and the following optional arguments:\n911: columns\n912: limit\n913: The search results and request ID are returned in the output. You must pass the name of the tool that you’re invoking in the request as the name parameter.\n914: Copy code\n915: POST /api/v2/databases/{database}/schemas/{schema}/mcp-servers/{name}\n916: {\n917: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n918: \"id\": 1,\n919: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n920: \"params\": {\n921: \"name\": \"product-search\",\n922: \"arguments\": {\n923: \"query\": \"Hotels in NYC\",\n924: \"columns\": array of strings,\n925: \"limit\": int\n926: }\n927: }\n928: }\n929: The following example shows the response:\n930: Copy code\n931: {\n932: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n933: \"id\": 1,\n934: \"result\": {\n935: \"results\": {}\n936: }\n937: }\n938: Call an agent tool¶\n939: CORTEX_AGENT_RUN and CODING_AGENT take the same call arguments. text is required. Omit thread_id and parent_message_id on the first turn. The server creates a Cortex thread and returns thread_id and message_id. Pass both on later calls to continue the same conversation. For a coding agent, that thread also keeps the sandbox.\n940: Copy code\n941: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n942: {\n943: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n944: \"id\": 1,\n945: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n946: \"params\": {\n947: \"name\": \"ask_coding_agent\",\n948: \"arguments\": {\n949: \"text\": \"Summarize last quarter.\"\n950: }\n951: }\n952: }\n953: A later turn passes the returned ids:\n954: Copy code\n955: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n956: {\n957: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n958: \"id\": 2,\n959: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n960: \"params\": {\n961: \"name\": \"ask_coding_agent\",\n962: \"arguments\": {\n963: \"text\": \"Break it down by region.\",\n964: \"thread_id\": \"12345\",\n965: \"parent_message_id\": \"67890\"\n966: }\n967: }\n968: }\n969: parent_message_id is required when thread_id is set. Pass the message_id from the previous result to continue from that message. Pass \"0\" to start a new branch at the thread root. Send both ids as strings.\n970: Answer a permission prompt¶\n971: A sandbox tool such as write can end the turn while it waits for approval. Each pending permission has tool_use_id, tool, summary, and options. Clients should take user input, and call the same tool with the thread_id, parent_message_id of the waiting message, and one decision in permission_decisions per pending permission. A decision is the tool_use_id and one offered option, or Deny with an optional reason. If a pending decision is left out, it is treated as a denial.\n972: Copy code\n973: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n974: {\n975: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n976: \"id\": 3,\n977: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n978: \"params\": {\n979: \"name\": \"ask_coding_agent\",\n980: \"arguments\": {\n981: \"thread_id\": \"12345\",\n982: \"parent_message_id\": \"67890\",\n983: \"permission_decisions\": [\n984: {\n985: \"tool_use_id\": \"toolu_write\",\n986: \"decision\": \"Allow this command\"\n987: }\n988: ]\n989: }\n990: }\n991: }\n992: The call automatically runs one more turn of the agent.\n993: Choose which content blocks come back¶\n994: The calling client can set content_cel_filter on a synchronous agent call or on AGENT_RUN_RESULT to select the content blocks returned. To re-read a finished run with a different filter, it can call AGENT_RUN_RESULT.\n995: compact (the default) keeps a compact subset with the information that is likely relevant to most clients.\n996: full keeps every content block.\n997: outline replaces content with one short entry per block (its index, type, and size), so a later call can pick one part, for example content.filter(c, c.type == 'table') or [content[3]].\n998: Any other string is a CEL expression. content is the list of blocks and response is the whole message. The expression’s value replaces content. For example, content.filter(c, c.type == 'text') keeps the text blocks.\n999: If a CEL expression fails to evaluate, the result contains the outline and the error, so the client can retry with a corrected expression.\n1000: Cortex Agent supplementary tools¶\n1001: If the server specification includes at least one CORTEX_AGENT_RUN or CODING_AGENT tool, or tools/list returns at least one generated agent tool, Snowflake automatically adds these tools to tools/list. Snowflake adds exactly these three tools once per server, regardless of how many agent tools the server has. They don’t appear in DESCRIBE MCP SERVER or GET_DDL output, and they don’t count toward the maximum of 50 tools per MCP server.\n1002: ToolPurpose\n1003: ASYNC_AGENT_RUN_STATUSReads one run. Returns in_progress, input_required, completed, cancelled, or failed, and a next_step.\n1004: AGENT_RUN_RESULTReturns the stored assistant message of a finished run, synchronous or background. Accepts content_cel_filter.\n1005: ASYNC_AGENT_RUN_CANCELStops a run that is in_progress. A run that has already finished returns its current status.\n1006: Expand\n1007: Show lessSee more\n1008: Note that all these tools respect Snowflake access control; a user cannot use these tools to access runs or threads that they don’t have access to.\n1009: AGENT_RUN_RESULT is useful without background too. For example, a client can request outline on the agent call, then read only the blocks it needs from the result.\n1010: Run an agent tool in the background¶\n1011: For CORTEX_AGENT_RUN and CODING_AGENT tools, callers can set the background argument to true to return immediately with a run handle. The run can be polled and managed with the Cortex Agent supplementary tools.\n1012: Copy code\n1013: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n1014: {\n1015: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n1016: \"id\": 1,\n1017: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n1018: \"params\": {\n1019: \"name\": \"customer_agent\",\n1020: \"arguments\": {\n1021: \"text\": \"What was revenue last quarter?\",\n1022: \"background\": true\n1023: }\n1024: }\n1025: }\n1026: The result includes run_id, thread_id, status (in_progress), and next_step. run_id is {thread_id}-{user_message_id}.\n1027: Poll status with that id:\n1028: Copy code\n1029: POST /api/v2/databases/<database>/schemas/<schema>/mcp-servers/<name>\n1030: {\n1031: \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n1032: \"id\": 2,\n1033: \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n1034: \"params\": {\n1035: \"name\": \"ASYNC_AGENT_RUN_STATUS\",\n1036: \"arguments\": {\n1037: \"run_id\": \"12345-7\"\n1038: }\n1039: }\n1040: }\n1041: When status is completed, next_step names AGENT_RUN_RESULT. Call it with the same run_id. content[0].text is the JSON text of structuredContent, and that object is the assistant message plus run_id, thread_id, and message_id. To continue that thread, call the same agent tool with thread_id and parent_message_id set to that message_id.\n1042: input_required means the run is waiting for a permission decision. Status and result both return pending_permissions, message_id, and a readable text. Answer by calling the agent tool again with permission_decisions, thread_id, parent_message_id, and background set to true. See Answer a permission prompt.\n1043: To stop a run, call ASYNC_AGENT_RUN_CANCEL with the same run_id.\n1044: Limitations¶\n1045: Snowflake supports Model Context Protocol revision 2025-11-25.\n1046: The Snowflake-managed MCP server is available in government regions, except Azure US Gov Virginia (non-FedRAMP High), and hasn’t yet completed formal FedRAMP assessment. For authorization status, contact your Snowflake account team.\n1047: Agent invocations through the Snowflake-managed MCP server are subject to Cortex Agents rate limits.\n1048: Every MCP request runs as an authenticated Snowflake user. The agent tool arguments don’t include the per-request variables that the Cortex Agents REST API uses to identify end users who don’t have Snowflake accounts. To serve those users, call the REST API instead; see Multi-tenancy for Cortex Agents.\n1049: The Snowflake-managed MCP server supports the MCP lifecycle (initialize with protocol version negotiation, and notifications/initialized), ping, tools, and notifications/progress for in-progress tools/call requests. It doesn’t support the following constructs in the MCP protocol: resources, prompts, roots, and sampling.\n1050: A tools/call request times out after 15 minutes. For agent runs that might take longer, call the agent tool with background set to true.\n1051: Each MCP server supports a maximum of 50 tools. This limit includes all tool types: Cortex Search, Cortex Analyst, Cortex Agents, coding agents, SQL execution, and custom (generic) tools. If you need more tools, create additional MCP servers. Higher tool counts can degrade tool-selection accuracy. Tools generated from tool_config don’t count toward this limit. They have their own limit of 50 per caller. See Serve every agent the caller can use.\n1052: Tool responses are subject to size limits to prevent LLM context window saturation:\n1053: Generic tools: Responses are truncated at 250 KB. This limit applies only to tools called through the Snowflake-managed MCP server. When an agent is invoked directly, custom tool results are limited to 32 KB. See Custom tool result limit.\n1054: SQL execution tool: Responses are truncated at 250 KB.\n1055: If a query result exceeds the size limit, the response is truncated. To work around this limit, use narrower queries that return fewer columns or rows.\n1056: By default, MCP OAuth sessions use the connecting user’s DEFAULT_ROLE as the primary role.\n1057: You can advertise other primary-role scopes with OAUTH_SCOPES_SUPPORTED. Secondary roles are\n1058: controlled by the OAuth security integration; the recommended MCP configuration leaves them\n1059: disabled (OAUTH_USE_SECONDARY_ROLES = NONE). For details, see\n1060: Role behavior in OAuth sessions.\n1061: MCP server objects aren’t replicated in failover groups. If you use replication, you must recreate MCP server objects on the secondary account. OAuth security integrations are replicated.\n1062: Was this page helpful?\n1063: YesNo\n1064: Visit Snowflake\n1065: Join the conversation\n1066: Develop with Snowflake\n1067: Share your feedback\n1068: Read the latest on our blog\n1069: Get your own certification\n1070: Privacy NoticeSite TermsCookies Settings© 2026 Snowflake, Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n1071: On this page\n1072: Overview\n1073: MCP server security recommendations\n1074: Create an MCP server object\n1075: MCP server URL\n1076: Access control\n1077: Set up OAuth authentication\n1078: Connect from common MCP clients\n1079: Connect with a programmatic access token\n1080: Network policies for MCP clients\n1081: Troubleshoot MCP client connections\n1082: Interact with the MCP server using a custom MCP client\n1083: Limitations"
