      "numbered_text": "1: Trial accounts | Snowflake Documentation\n2: DOCUMENTATION\n3: /\n4: EN\n5: Language\n6: English\n7: Français\n8: Deutsch\n9: 日本語\n10: 한국어\n11: Português\n12: Get started\n13: Guides\n14: Developer\n15: Reference\n16: Release notes\n17: Tutorials\n18: Status\n19: For AI agents: documentation index at /llms.txt — fetch to discover all Snowflake documentation pages. Markdown version of this page: /en/user-guide/admin-trial-account.md\n20: Overview\n21: Snowflake Horizon Catalog\n22: Applications and tools for connecting to Snowflake\n23: Virtual warehouses\n24: Databases, Tables, & Views\n25: Data types\n26: Data Integration\n27: Snowflake Openflow\n28: Apache Iceberg™\n29: Apache Iceberg™ Tables\n30: Snowflake Open Catalog\n31: Zero-Copy Connectors\n32: About SAP® and Snowflake\n33: About Salesforce Data Cloud and Snowflake\n34: About Workday Live Data Query for Snowflake\n35: About Workday Data Cloud and Snowflake\n36: Data engineering\n37: Data loading\n38: Dynamic tables\n39: Streams and tasks\n40: Row timestamps\n41: DCM Projects\n42: Pipeline Builder\n43: dbt Projects on Snowflake\n44: Data Unloading\n45: Storage lifecycle policies\n46: Migrations\n47: Queries\n48: Collaboration\n49: Snowflake AI & ML\n50: Snowflake Postgres\n51: Alerts & Notifications\n52: Security\n53: Organizations & Accounts\n54: Organizations\n55: Introduction\n56: Organization administrators\n57: Organization account\n58: Organization Hub\n59: Organization users\n60: Managing accounts\n61: Accounts\n62: Connecting to your accounts\n63: Account identifiers\n64: Trial accounts\n65: Parameter management\n66: User management\n67: Domain verification\n68: Tutorial: Create users and grant roles with a template\n69: Business continuity & data recovery\n70: Performance optimization\n71: Cost & Billing\n72: GuidesOrganizations & AccountsTrial accounts\n73: Trial accounts¶\n74: A Snowflake trial account lets you evaluate/test Snowflake’s full range of innovative and powerful features with no cost or contractual obligations. To sign\n75: up for a trial account, all you need is a valid email address; no payment information or other qualifying information is required.\n76: Note\n77: AI features are disabled by default on self-service trial accounts until you\n78: add a credit card to the account. For more information, see current limitations for trial accounts.\n79: Signing up for a trial account¶\n80: You can sign up for a free trial using the self-service form (on the Snowflake website).\n81: When you sign up for a trial account, you select your cloud platform, region,\n82: and Snowflake Edition. These selections can affect how quickly you exhaust your free usage balance. For example, some features available in the Enterprise Edition consume additional credits.\n83: The balance of your free usage decreases as you consume credits to use compute resources and accrue costs associated with storage. You can track your remaining balance at any time.\n84: The trial continues for 30 days (from the sign-up date) or until you’ve depleted your free usage balance, whichever occurs first. At any time during the trial, you can cancel the trial or upgrade the account to a paid account.\n85: At the end of the trial, the account is suspended. You can still log into a suspended account, but you cannot use any features, such as running a virtual warehouse,\n86: loading data, or performing queries.\n87: To reactivate a suspended trial account, add a credit card if one isn’t already on file, and then upgrade to a paid account.\n88: Using compute resources¶\n89: Virtual warehouses provide the compute power to load data and\n90: perform queries. These warehouses consume credits, which reduces your free usage balance. To begin, simply start a warehouse; any credits consumed by the warehouse will be deducted from your balance. If your credit consumption fully depletes your free usage balance, you must upgrade to a paid account to continue using Snowflake.\n91: Free credits are consumed by the virtual warehouses you create in your account while they’re running. Serverless features,\n92: including AI features, also consume free credits when you use them.\n93: Tip\n94: To prevent unintentional usage of your free credits:\n95: Verify the size of your virtual warehouses before you start/resume them. The larger the warehouse, the more credits it consumes while running.\n96: In many situations, Small or Medium size warehouses are sufficient for evaluating Snowflake’s loading and querying capabilities.\n97: Do not disable auto-suspend when creating a warehouse. Choosing a short auto-suspend time period (e.g. 5 minutes or less) can reduce credit consumption.\n98: For additional tips on using your trial account:\n99: In the left navigation bar, find the tile showing your remaining balance.\n100: Select … » Using your trial credits.\n101: Using storage¶\n102: As you load data into your trial account, the cost of that storage is subtracted from your free usage balance based on the standard On-Demand cost of a TB in your cloud platform and region. In addition to the cost of storage, loading data also consumes credits as it uses the compute resources of a warehouse.\n103: Tutorials for trial accounts¶\n104: The following tutorials are available for trial accounts:\n105: Load and query sample data using SQL\n106: Load data from cloud storage: Amazon S3\n107: Load data from cloud storage: Microsoft Azure\n108: Load data from cloud storage: Google Cloud Storage\n109: Create users and grant roles\n110: Tracking your remaining balance¶\n111: Users with the ACCOUNTADMIN role can track the remaining balance of their trial using a tile in the left navigation bar of\n112: Snowsight.\n113: From this tile you can also:\n114: Select Upgrade to upgrade the trial account to a paid account.\n115: Select … » see organization usage details to access the Usage page, which allows you to drill down into your credit\n116: consumption and storage costs.\n117: Select the … button to access resources that help you get the most out of your trial account.\n118: Upgrading to a paid account¶\n119: To upgrade a trial account to a paid account, first add a credit card to the account:\n120: Sign in to Snowsight.\n121: In the navigation menu, select Admin » Billing.\n122: Click the Snowflake billing tab.\n123: In the Payment method pane, click + Credit Card.\n124: Enter the required information, and then select Add Card.\n125: Adding a credit card doesn’t upgrade your trial account to a paid account. After adding a credit card, you can select Upgrade in the left navigation at any time to upgrade the account.\n126: Snowflake knows trial account subscribers who upgrade to paid\n127: accounts as on-demand, self-service (ODSS) customers. ODSS customers can edit the billing contact information for a self-service account using\n128: Snowsight. For more information, see Update billing contact information.\n129: Note that you can also change the credit card for a trial account, at any time, using the same interface in which you added the card.\n130: Note\n131: Upgrading to a paid account ends the free trial and moves the account to on-demand billing, where you’re charged only for what you use. Any remaining free trial credits still apply.\n132: Threshold billing: Depending on your usage after your free credits are exhausted, you may be automatically billed one or more times before the end of each calendar month if your consumption reaches certain thresholds (threshold payments). Threshold payments can vary in amount and may occur multiple times in a single month. You will receive an email notification each time a threshold payment is charged. Any threshold payments successfully charged during a calendar month are credited toward that month’s total consumption.\n133: If you don’t upgrade before the trial period ends, the account is suspended and any unused free usage balance expires.\n134: For pricing details, see the pricing page (on the Snowflake website).\n135: Warning\n136: If a threshold payment fails, your account may be suspended. If you receive a payment failure notification, take action promptly to update your payment method.\n137: Canceling a trial account¶\n138: You can cancel a trial account at any time by contacting Snowflake Support\n139: and requesting the account to be canceled.\n140: Note\n141: Currently, trial accounts cannot be canceled through the web interface. To cancel an account, you must contact Snowflake Support.\n142: Current limitations for trial accounts¶\n143: The following features are not available for trial accounts:\n144: External network access\n145: Hybrid tables\n146: Outbound private connectivity\n147: Snowflake Openflow\n148: Using Duo for multi-factor authentication (MFA)\n149: AI features are disabled by default on self-service trial accounts. To enable them, add a credit card to the account. Adding a credit card doesn’t upgrade the trial to a paid account or end the trial period.\n150: Was this page helpful?\n151: YesNo\n152: Visit Snowflake\n153: Join the conversation\n154: Develop with Snowflake\n155: Share your feedback\n156: Read the latest on our blog\n157: Get your own certification\n158: Privacy NoticeSite TermsCookies Settings© 2026 Snowflake, Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n159: On this page\n160: Signing up for a trial account\n161: Using compute resources\n162: Using storage\n163: Tutorials for trial accounts\n164: Tracking your remaining balance\n165: Upgrading to a paid account\n166: Canceling a trial account\n167: Current limitations for trial accounts\n168: Related content\n169: Understanding compute cost\n170: Related info\n171: • Getting Started with Snowflake - Zero to Snowflake"
