      "numbered_text": "1: Atlas Programmatic Access - Atlas - MongoDB Docs\n2: For AI agents: a documentation index is available at https://www.mongodb.com/docs/llms.txt — markdown versions of all pages are available by appending .md to any URL path.\n3: Products\n4: Platform\n5: AtlasBuild with the intelligent data platform\n6: Platform Services\n7: DatabasePower critical apps with real-time dataSearchDeliver fast, relevant search at scaleVector SearchBuild AI apps with accurate contextVoyage AIEmbed meaning and rerank resultsAtlas Agent EngineBuild, deploy, and govern agents in production\n8: Self Managed\n9: Enterprise AdvancedManage your apps on your infrastructureCommunity EditionDevelop locally with MongoDB, at no cost\n10: Tools\n11: CompassWork with MongoDB data in a GUIIntegrationsIntegrations with third-party servicesRelational MigratorMigrate to MongoDB with confidence\n12: View All ProductsExplore our full product suitearrow-right\n13: MongoDB 9.0The best version of MongoDB ever builtarrow-right\n14: Build with MongoDB Atlas\n15: Get started for free in minutes\n16: Sign Up\n17: Explore Atlas Deployment Options\n18: Develop with self-managed MongoDB\n19: Learn more\n20: Try Community Edition\n21: Explore the latest version of MongoDB\n22: Download\n23: Resources\n24: DocumentationLearn how to build with MongoDB and get started todaySkills and CertificationsDevelop in-demand skills and earn MongoDB certificationsBooksRead books and e-books about MongoDB\n25: MongoDB for EducatorsTake advantage of educator resourcesMongoDB for StudentsTake advantage of student benefitsCommunityJoin a community of developersEvents and WebinarsFind an event or webinar near you\n26: Resources HubGet help building the next big thing with MongoDBarrow-right\n27: Solutions\n28: Use cases\n29: Artificial IntelligenceModernizationPaymentsServerless DevelopmentGamingIntelligent SearchEdge and Mobile\n30: Industries\n31: Financial ServicesTelecommunicationsHealthcareRetailPublic SectorManufacturing\n32: Solutions LibraryOrganized and tailored solutions to kick-start projectsarrow-right\n33: MongoDB AMP\n34: Modernize applications up to 3x faster\n35: Learn morearrow-right\n36: Startups and AI Innovators\n37: For world-changing ideas and AI pioneers\n38: Learn morearrow-right\n39: Customer Case Studies\n40: Hear directly from our users\n41: See storiesarrow-right\n42: Company\n43: CareersStart your next adventureBlogRead articles and announcementsNewsroomRead press releases and news stories\n44: PartnersLearn about our partner ecosystemLeadershipMeet our executive teamCompanyLearn more about who we are\n45: Contact Us\n46: Reach out to MongoDB\n47: Connect with an expertarrow-right\n48: Investors\n49: Visit our investor portal\n50: Learn morearrow-right\n51: Pricing\n52: Eng\n53: SupportSign In\n54: menu-vertical\n55: Get Started\n56: Products\n57: Platform\n58: AtlasBuild with the intelligent data platform\n59: Platform Services\n60: DatabasePower critical apps with real-time dataSearchDeliver fast, relevant search at scaleVector SearchBuild AI apps with accurate contextVoyage AIEmbed meaning and rerank resultsAtlas Agent EngineBuild, deploy, and govern agents in production\n61: Self Managed\n62: Enterprise AdvancedManage your apps on your infrastructureCommunity EditionDevelop locally with MongoDB, at no cost\n63: Tools\n64: CompassWork with MongoDB data in a GUIIntegrationsIntegrations with third-party servicesRelational MigratorMigrate to MongoDB with confidence\n65: View All ProductsExplore our full product suitearrow-right\n66: MongoDB 9.0The best version of MongoDB ever builtarrow-right\n67: Build with MongoDB Atlas\n68: Get started for free in minutes\n69: Sign Up\n70: Explore Atlas Deployment Options\n71: Develop with self-managed MongoDB\n72: Learn more\n73: Try Community Edition\n74: Explore the latest version of MongoDB\n75: Download\n76: Resources\n77: DocumentationLearn how to build with MongoDB and get started todaySkills and CertificationsDevelop in-demand skills and earn MongoDB certificationsBooksRead books and e-books about MongoDB\n78: MongoDB for EducatorsTake advantage of educator resourcesMongoDB for StudentsTake advantage of student benefitsCommunityJoin a community of developersEvents and WebinarsFind an event or webinar near you\n79: Resources HubGet help building the next big thing with MongoDBarrow-right\n80: Solutions\n81: Use cases\n82: Artificial IntelligenceModernizationPaymentsServerless DevelopmentGamingIntelligent SearchEdge and Mobile\n83: Industries\n84: Financial ServicesTelecommunicationsHealthcareRetailPublic SectorManufacturing\n85: Solutions LibraryOrganized and tailored solutions to kick-start projectsarrow-right\n86: MongoDB AMP\n87: Modernize applications up to 3x faster\n88: Learn morearrow-right\n89: Startups and AI Innovators\n90: For world-changing ideas and AI pioneers\n91: Learn morearrow-right\n92: Customer Case Studies\n93: Hear directly from our users\n94: See storiesarrow-right\n95: Company\n96: CareersStart your next adventureBlogRead articles and announcementsNewsroomRead press releases and news stories\n97: PartnersLearn about our partner ecosystemLeadershipMeet our executive teamCompanyLearn more about who we are\n98: Contact Us\n99: Reach out to MongoDB\n100: Connect with an expertarrow-right\n101: Investors\n102: Visit our investor portal\n103: Learn morearrow-right\n104: Pricing\n105: Eng\n106: SupportSign In\n107: menu-vertical\n108: Get Started\n109: Docs Menu\n110: Ask MongoDB AI\n111: /Docs Home\n112: /Management\n113: Atlas Programmatic Access\n114: Copy page\n115: You can interact with Atlas programmatically to perform administrative tasks or manage your deployments using the following resources.\n116: Atlas Administration API\n117: Programmatically perform administrative tasks on your Atlas cluster outside of the Atlas UI with the Atlas Administration API. Atlas Administration API resources add, edit, or delete administrative objects within Atlas, including projects, users, and clusters.\n118: You can explore the various endpoints available through the Atlas Administration API in MongoDB's Postman workspace.\n119: To configure access for the Atlas Administration API, see Get Started with the Atlas Administration API.\n120: Tip\n121: Learn about the Atlas Admin API.\n122: See the Atlas Admin API Specification.\n123: Atlas Go SDK\n124: The Atlas Go SDK provides platform-specific and Go language-specific tools, libraries, and documentation to help you build applications that interact with Atlas quickly and easily using the Atlas Administration API.\n125: Tip\n126: Atlas Go SDK.\n127: Atlas Embedding and Reranking API\n128: The Embedding and Reranking API provides endpoints for accessing Voyage AI's state-of-the-art embedding and reranking models. Use the REST API and available client SDKs to power search and retrieval in your AI applications.\n129: Learn about the Embedding and Reranking API\n130: See the Embedding and Reranking API Specification\n131: Atlas CLI\n132: The Atlas CLI is a command-line interface built specifically for MongoDB Atlas. You can use Atlas CLI to interact with Atlas from the terminal with short and intuitive commands and accomplish complex operational tasks such as creating a cluster or setting up an access list interactively and programmatically.\n133: Tip\n134: Learn more about the Atlas CLI.\n135: Get Started with the Atlas CLI.\n136: HashiCorp Terraform MongoDB Atlas Provider\n137: HashiCorp Terraform MongoDB Atlas Provider provides refined programmatic access and management of MongoDB Atlas resources. You can define your environment using the HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) and deploy, update, and manage Atlas infrastructure as code through HashiCorp Terraform. This allows you to integrate Atlas into your continuous delivery workflows.\n138: Tip\n139: Learn more about MongoDB Atlas and HashiCorp Terraform.\n140: Managing IaC with MongoDB Atlas and HashiCorp Terraform (Video Walkthrough).\n141: Cloud Development Kit for Terraform\n142: CDK for Terraform leverages the MongoDB Atlas Atlas Administration API to enable provisioning across popular programming languages such as JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java, Go, and C#. Utilizing the powerful capabilities of Terraform, with CDKTF you can now create, manage, and update your Atlas resources with ease and deploy them seamlessly in the cloud without context switching from the native programming language of choice.\n143: Tip\n144: MongoDB Cloud Development Kit for Terraform.\n145: Managing IaC with MongoDB Atlas and the Terraform CDK (Video Walkthrough).\n146: MongoDB Atlas AWS CloudFormation Resources\n147: Atlas CloudFormation Resources helps you to manage MongoDB Atlas. It simplifies provisioning and management of Atlas features on AWS. You can create YAML- or JSON-based templates for the service or application architectures you want and have AWS CloudFormation use those templates for quick and reliable provisioning of the services or applications (called \"stacks\"). You can also easily update or replicate the stacks as needed.\n148: Tip\n149: Learn more about Atlas CloudFormation.\n150: Atlas CloudFormation Resources.\n151: AWS Partner Solutions\n152: AWS Partner Solutions provide pre-built CloudFormation templates to help you provision a group of Atlas resources for specific use cases instead of deploying them one by one. Partner Solutions help you deploy popular technologies to AWS according to AWS best practices.\n153: Tip\n154: AWS Quick Starts.\n155: AWS Cloud Development Kit\n156: If you prefer using familiar programming languages like JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java, C#, and Go rather than using YAML or JSON, you can use the AWS CDK to deploy Atlas resources. When you execute your AWS CDK applications, they automatically generate CloudFormation templates, which in turn utilize the AWS CloudFormation service for resource provisioning. This way, you benefit from the abstraction and flexibility provided by AWS CDK and take advantage of the robustness of CloudFormation for infrastructure management, while still using the programming language of your choice.\n157: Tip\n158: Atlas AWS Cloud Development Kit.\n159: How to Deploy Atlas with AWS CDK (Video Walkthrough).\n160: Atlas Kubernetes Operator\n161: The Atlas Kubernetes Operator enables you to manage Atlas resources through custom resources that you apply in Kubernetes. This allows an infrastructure as code approach to manage declarative configuration for Atlas using a GitOps workflow, allowing developers to configure Atlas resources using the same process and tooling that they use to deploy application components into Kubernetes. The automatic creation of a Kubernetes secret containing the connection string for a database user simplifies the process of connecting an application to the database.\n162: Tip\n163: Learn more about the Atlas Kubernetes Operator.\n164: Quick Start Tutorial.\n165: Back\n166: FAQ: Support\n167: Next\n168: Administration API\n169: Rate this page\n170: On this page\n171: Atlas Administration API\n172: Atlas Go SDK\n173: Atlas Embedding and Reranking API\n174: Atlas CLI\n175: HashiCorp Terraform MongoDB Atlas Provider\n176: Cloud Development Kit for Terraform\n177: MongoDB Atlas AWS CloudFormation Resources\n178: AWS Partner Solutions\n179: AWS Cloud Development Kit\n180: Atlas Kubernetes Operator\n181: On this page\n182: Atlas Administration API\n183: Atlas Go SDK\n184: Atlas Embedding and Reranking API\n185: Atlas CLI\n186: HashiCorp Terraform MongoDB Atlas Provider\n187: Cloud Development Kit for Terraform\n188: MongoDB Atlas AWS CloudFormation Resources\n189: AWS Partner Solutions\n190: AWS Cloud Development Kit\n191: Atlas Kubernetes Operator\n192: Documentation\n193: DatabaseAtlas Agent EngineVoyage AI Models\n194: Get Started\n195: Deployment\n196: Development\n197: Management\n198: MongoDB Atlas\n199: Overview\n200: Database Editions\n201: Atla