      "numbered_text": "1: Atlas Administration API Authentication Methods - Atlas - MongoDB Docs\n2: For AI agents: a documentation index is available at https://www.mongodb.com/docs/llms.txt — markdown versions of all pages are available by appending .md to any URL path.\n3: Products\n4: Platform\n5: AtlasBuild with the intelligent data platform\n6: Platform Services\n7: DatabasePower critical apps with real-time dataSearchDeliver fast, relevant search at scaleVector SearchBuild AI apps with accurate contextVoyage AIEmbed meaning and rerank resultsAtlas Agent EngineBuild, deploy, and govern agents in production\n8: Self Managed\n9: Enterprise AdvancedManage your apps on your infrastructureCommunity EditionDevelop locally with MongoDB, at no cost\n10: Tools\n11: CompassWork with MongoDB data in a GUIIntegrationsIntegrations with third-party servicesRelational MigratorMigrate to MongoDB with confidence\n12: View All ProductsExplore our full product suitearrow-right\n13: MongoDB 9.0The best version of MongoDB ever builtarrow-right\n14: Build with MongoDB Atlas\n15: Get started for free in minutes\n16: Sign Up\n17: Explore Atlas Deployment Options\n18: Develop with self-managed MongoDB\n19: Learn more\n20: Try Community Edition\n21: Explore the latest version of MongoDB\n22: Download\n23: Resources\n24: DocumentationLearn how to build with MongoDB and get started todaySkills and CertificationsDevelop in-demand skills and earn MongoDB certificationsBooksRead books and e-books about MongoDB\n25: MongoDB for EducatorsTake advantage of educator resourcesMongoDB for StudentsTake advantage of student benefitsCommunityJoin a community of developersEvents and WebinarsFind an event or webinar near you\n26: Resources HubGet help building the next big thing with MongoDBarrow-right\n27: Solutions\n28: Use cases\n29: Artificial IntelligenceModernizationPaymentsServerless DevelopmentGamingIntelligent SearchEdge and Mobile\n30: Industries\n31: Financial ServicesTelecommunicationsHealthcareRetailPublic SectorManufacturing\n32: Solutions LibraryOrganized and tailored solutions to kick-start projectsarrow-right\n33: MongoDB AMP\n34: Modernize applications up to 3x faster\n35: Learn morearrow-right\n36: Startups and AI Innovators\n37: For world-changing ideas and AI pioneers\n38: Learn morearrow-right\n39: Customer Case Studies\n40: Hear directly from our users\n41: See storiesarrow-right\n42: Company\n43: CareersStart your next adventureBlogRead articles and announcementsNewsroomRead press releases and news stories\n44: PartnersLearn about our partner ecosystemLeadershipMeet our executive teamCompanyLearn more about who we are\n45: Contact Us\n46: Reach out to MongoDB\n47: Connect with an expertarrow-right\n48: Investors\n49: Visit our investor portal\n50: Learn morearrow-right\n51: Pricing\n52: Eng\n53: SupportSign In\n54: menu-vertical\n55: Get Started\n56: Products\n57: Platform\n58: AtlasBuild with the intelligent data platform\n59: Platform Services\n60: DatabasePower critical apps with real-time dataSearchDeliver fast, relevant search at scaleVector SearchBuild AI apps with accurate contextVoyage AIEmbed meaning and rerank resultsAtlas Agent EngineBuild, deploy, and govern agents in production\n61: Self Managed\n62: Enterprise AdvancedManage your apps on your infrastructureCommunity EditionDevelop locally with MongoDB, at no cost\n63: Tools\n64: CompassWork with MongoDB data in a GUIIntegrationsIntegrations with third-party servicesRelational MigratorMigrate to MongoDB with confidence\n65: View All ProductsExplore our full product suitearrow-right\n66: MongoDB 9.0The best version of MongoDB ever builtarrow-right\n67: Build with MongoDB Atlas\n68: Get started for free in minutes\n69: Sign Up\n70: Explore Atlas Deployment Options\n71: Develop with self-managed MongoDB\n72: Learn more\n73: Try Community Edition\n74: Explore the latest version of MongoDB\n75: Download\n76: Resources\n77: DocumentationLearn how to build with MongoDB and get started todaySkills and CertificationsDevelop in-demand skills and earn MongoDB certificationsBooksRead books and e-books about MongoDB\n78: MongoDB for EducatorsTake advantage of educator resourcesMongoDB for StudentsTake advantage of student benefitsCommunityJoin a community of developersEvents and WebinarsFind an event or webinar near you\n79: Resources HubGet help building the next big thing with MongoDBarrow-right\n80: Solutions\n81: Use cases\n82: Artificial IntelligenceModernizationPaymentsServerless DevelopmentGamingIntelligent SearchEdge and Mobile\n83: Industries\n84: Financial ServicesTelecommunicationsHealthcareRetailPublic SectorManufacturing\n85: Solutions LibraryOrganized and tailored solutions to kick-start projectsarrow-right\n86: MongoDB AMP\n87: Modernize applications up to 3x faster\n88: Learn morearrow-right\n89: Startups and AI Innovators\n90: For world-changing ideas and AI pioneers\n91: Learn morearrow-right\n92: Customer Case Studies\n93: Hear directly from our users\n94: See storiesarrow-right\n95: Company\n96: CareersStart your next adventureBlogRead articles and announcementsNewsroomRead press releases and news stories\n97: PartnersLearn about our partner ecosystemLeadershipMeet our executive teamCompanyLearn more about who we are\n98: Contact Us\n99: Reach out to MongoDB\n100: Connect with an expertarrow-right\n101: Investors\n102: Visit our investor portal\n103: Learn morearrow-right\n104: Pricing\n105: Eng\n106: SupportSign In\n107: menu-vertical\n108: Get Started\n109: Docs Menu\n110: Ask MongoDB AI\n111: /Docs Home\n112: /\n113: /Management\n114: /APIs\n115: /Administration API\n116: Atlas Administration API Authentication Methods\n117: Copy page\n118: Important\n119: We recommend you use service accounts instead of API keys to authenticate to the Atlas Administration API. API keys are a legacy authentication method.\n120: To use the Atlas Administration API to manage your Atlas clusters, you must authenticate your API requests. The Atlas Administration API accepts the following authentication methods:\n121: Service account access tokens (OAuth 2.0)\n122: API keys (HTTP Digest Authentication)\n123: To learn how to use service accounts and API keys to configure programmatic access to your Atlas organizations and projects, see the Atlas Administration API Getting Started guide.\n124: The Atlas Administration API doesn't provide access to the data stored in your clusters. To read or write data in a database, you must authenticate to your cluster using the credentials for a database\n125: user with the appropriate read or write roles. You can use the Atlas Administration API to create and manage database users.\n126: Note\n127: Many Atlas Administration API endpoint URLs follow the format of /api/atlas/<version>/groups/<GROUP-ID>/, where <GROUP-ID> is your project ID. For these resources, the service account or API keys that you use to authenticate the request must be a member of the organization that hosts the project. Otherwise, Atlas responds with a 401 error.\n128: The following sections describe the Atlas Administration API authentication methods:\n129: Service Accounts Overview\n130: Service accounts are the recommended method to manage authentication to the Atlas Administration API. Service accounts provide improved security over API keys by using the industry standard OAuth 2.0 protocol with the Client Credentials flow.\n131: A service account lets you manage permissions and create access tokens that authenticate API requests. Each service account has a client ID and a rotatable secret that function as a username and a password for creating access tokens. To learn how to construct an API request with an access token, see Make an API Request.\n132: Tip\n133: Service Account Token Best Practices\n134: Consider the following best practices when using service account tokens:\n135: Reuse tokens: Service account access tokens have a 1 hour (3600 seconds) lifespan, during which you can reuse the token multiple times. Avoid creating new tokens for every request.\n136: Generate new tokens when needed: While you can't refresh an access token, you can generate a new token when your current token is close to expiry. This prevents service interruptions and allows you to maintain authorized service account access.\n137: Revoke unnecessary tokens: When you no longer need a token, revoke the token's access to minimize security risks and ensure that only active tokens have access to your service account.\n138: Each service account belongs to exactly one organization, and you can grant it access to any number of projects within that organization. To give an organization-level service account access to a project, see Grant Existing Service Account Access to a Project.\n139: Atlas roles limit which operations a service account can authenticate with its access token. You must assign roles to service accounts as you would for users to ensure that the service account generates access tokens with the necessary permissions for the desired API calls.\n140: Important\n141: If a project-level service account has access to the organization or to other projects, you must have the Organization Owner role to manage its lifecycle, including rotating or revoking its secrets.\n142: You can't use a service account or its access token to log into Atlas through the Atlas UI. Service accounts only grant access to the Atlas Administration API, which does not include UI access or access to cluster data. To learn more about the limitations of service accounts, see MongoDB Limits and Thresholds.\n143: API Keys Overview\n144: Important\n145: We recommend you use service accounts instead of API keys to authenticate to the Atlas Administration API. API keys are a legacy authentication method.\n146: API keys are a legacy method of authenticating to the Atlas Administration API that uses HTTP Digest Authentication.\n147: API keys have two parts: a Public Key and a Private Key. These serve the same function as a username and a password to authenticate API requests. To learn how to construct an API request using API keys, see Make an API Request.\n148: Atlas hashes the Public Key and Private Key using a unique value called a nonce. The nonce is only valid for a short amount of time as per the HTTP Digest Authentication specification. This limited lifespan protects against replay attacks, where an attacker caches a Private Key to use it without time restriction.\n149: Each pair of API keys belongs to only one organization, and can grant access to any number of projects in that organization. To give organization-level API keys access to a project, see Grant Existing Service Account Access to a Project.\n150: Atlas roles limit which operations API keys can perform. You must assign roles to API keys as you would for users to ensure the API keys have the necessary permissions for the desired API calls.\n151: You can't use API keys to log into Atlas through the Atlas UI. API keys only grant access to the Atlas Administration API, which does not include UI access or access to cluster data.\n152: Next Steps\n153: To learn how to use and manage service accounts and API keys, see the following procedures:\n154: Grant Programmatic Access to an Organization\n155: Grant Programmatic Access to a Project\n156: Manage Programmatic Access to an Organization\n157: Manage Programmatic Access to a Project\n158: Manage Programmatic Access to Multiple Organizations\n159: Generate Service Account Token\n160: Revoke Service Account Token\n161: Back\n162: Admin API Overview\n163: Next\n164: Rotate Service Account Secrets\n165: Rate this page\n166: On this page\n167: Service Accounts Overview\n168: API Keys Overview\n169: Next Steps\n170: On this page\n171: Service Accounts Overview\n172: API Keys Overview\n173: Next Steps\n174: Documentation\n175: DatabaseAtlas Agent EngineVoyage AI Models\n176: Get Started\n177: Deployment\n178: Development\n179: Management\n180: MongoDB Atlas\n181: Overv