Monitors\n512: Nebius Cloud Integration\n513: Network Device Monitoring\n514: Notebooks\n515: OAuth2 Client Public\n516: Observability Pipelines\n517: OCI Integration\n518: Okta Integration\n519: On-Call\n520: On-Call Paging\n521: OpenAI Codex Integration\n522: Opsgenie Integration\n523: Org Authorized Clients\n524: Org Connections\n525: Org Groups\n526: Organizations\n527: PagerDuty Integration\n528: Powerpack\n529: Processes\n530: Product Analytics\n531: Product Catalog\n532: Reference Tables\n533: Report Schedules\n534: Reporting And Sharing\n535: Restriction Policies\n536: Roles\n537: RUM\n538: Rum Audience Management\n539: RUM Config\n540: RUM Insights\n541: RUM Managed Archives\n542: RUM Metrics\n543: RUM Operations\n544: RUM Recovery\n545: RUM Remote Config\n546: RUM Replay Heatmaps\n547: RUM Replay Playlists\n548: RUM Replay Sessions\n549: RUM Replay Viewership\n550: RUM Retention Filters\n551: RUM Retention Quotas\n552: Rum Teams Ownership\n553: SCIM\n554: Scorecards\n555: Seats\n556: Security Monitoring\n557: Sensitive Data Scanner\n558: Service Accounts\n559: Service Checks\n560: Service Definition\n561: Service Dependencies\n562: Service Level Objective Corrections\n563: Service Level Objectives\n564: ServiceNow Integration\n565: Slack Integration\n566: Snapshots\n567: Snowflake Integration\n568: Software Catalog\n569: Spans\n570: Spans Metrics\n571: Static Analysis\n572: Status Pages\n573: Statuspage Integration\n574: Stegadography\n575: Storage Management\n576: Synthetics\n577: Tag Rules\n578: Tags\n579: Teams\n580: Temporal Cloud OpenMetrics Integration\n581: Test Optimization\n582: Threat Intelligence\n583: Timeseries Anomaly Investigations\n584: Twilio Integration\n585: Usage Metering\n586: User Authorized Clients\n587: Users\n588: Webhooks Integration\n589: Widgets\n590: Work Management\n591: Work Management Attribute\n592: Work Management Type\n593: Workflow Automation\n594: Docs\n595: API\n596: LanguageEnglish\n597: English\n598: Français\n599: 日本語\n600: 한국어\n601: Español\n602: Datadog site\n603: US1\n604: US1\n605: US3\n606: US5\n607: EU\n608: AP1\n609: AP2\n610: UK1\n611: US1-FED\n612: US2-FED\n613: API Reference\n614: Copy pageCopied\n615: The Datadog API is an HTTP REST API. The API uses resource-oriented URLs to call the API, uses status codes to indicate the success or failure of requests, returns JSON from all requests, and uses standard HTTP response codes. Use the Datadog API to access the Datadog platform programmatically.\n616: Getting started\n617: Authenticate to the API with an API key using the header DD-API-KEY. For some endpoints, you also need an Application key, which uses the header DD-APPLICATION-KEY.\n618: To try out the API\n619: Note: To authenticate to the Datadog API through Postman, add your Datadog API and Application key values to the Collection variables of the Datadog API collection.\n620: Using the API is a guide to the endpoints.\n621: Notes:\n622: Add your API and application key values to the Variables tab of the Datadog API Collection.\n623: cURL code examples assume usage of BASH and GNU coreutils. On macOS, you can install coreutils with the Homebrew package manager: brew install coreutils\n624: Client libraries\n625: By default, the Datadog API Docs show examples in cURL. Select one of our official client libraries languages in each endpoint to see code examples from that library. To install each library:\n626: JavaPython [legacy]PythonRuby [legacy]RubyGoTypeScriptRust\n627: Installation\n628: Maven - Add this dependency to your project's POM:\n629: Copy\n630: <dependency>\n631: <groupId>com.datadoghq</groupId>\n632: <artifactId>datadog-api-client</artifactId>\n633: <version>2.62.0</version>\n634: <scope>compile</scope>\n635: </dependency>\n636: Gradle - Add this dependency to your project's build file:\n637: Copy\n638: compile \"com.datadoghq:datadog-api-client:2.62.0\"\n639: Usage\n640: Copy\n641: import com.datadog.api.client.ApiClient;\n642: import com.datadog.api.client.ApiException;\n643: import com.datadog.api.client.Configuration;\n644: import com.datadog.api.<VERSION>.client.api.*;\n645: import com.datadog.api.<VERSION>.client.model.*;\n646: Note: Replace <VERSION> with v1 or v2, depending on which endpoints you want to use.\n647: Examples\n648: Maven pom.xml for running examples:\n649: Copy\n650: <project>\n651: <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>\n652: <groupId>com.example</groupId>\n653: <artifactId>example</artifactId>\n654: <version>1</version>\n655: <dependencies>\n656: <dependency>\n657: <groupId>com.datadoghq</groupId>\n658: <artifactId>datadog-api-client</artifactId>\n659: <version>2.62.0</version>\n660: <scope>compile</scope>\n661: </dependency>\n662: </dependencies>\n663: </project>\n664: Make sure that CLASSPATH variable contains all dependencies.\n665: Copy\n666: export CLASSPATH=$(mvn -q exec:exec -Dexec.executable=echo -Dexec.args=\"%classpath\")\n667: Gradle build.gradle for running examples:\n668: Copy\n669: plugins {\n670: id 'java'\n671: id 'application'\n672: }\n673: repositories {\n674: jcenter()\n675: }\n676: dependencies {\n677: implementation 'com.datadoghq:datadog-api-client:2.62.0'\n678: }\n679: application {\n680: mainClassName = 'Example.java'\n681: }\n682: Execute example by running gradle run command.\n683: Installation\n684: Copy\n685: pip install datadog\n686: Usage\n687: Copy\n688: import datadog\n689: Installation\n690: Copy\n691: pip3 install datadog-api-client\n692: Usage\n693: Copy\n694: import datadog_api_client\n695: Installation\n696: Copy\n697: gem install dogapi\n698: Usage\n699: Copy\n700: require 'dogapi'\n701: Installation\n702: Copy\n703: gem install datadog_api_client -v 2.61.0\n704: Usage\n705: Copy\n706: require 'datadog_api_client'\n707: Installation\n708: Copy\n709: go mod init main && go get github.com/DataDog/datadog-api-client-go/v2/api/datadog\n710: Usage\n711: Copy\n712: import (\n713: \"github.com/DataDog/datadog-api-client-go/v2/api/datadog\"\n714: \"github.com/DataDog/datadog-api-client-go/v2/api/datadog<VERSION>\"\n715: )\n716: Note: Replace <VERSION> with V1 or V2, depending on which endpoints you want to use.\n717: Installation\n718: The package is under @datadog/datadog-api-client and can be installed through NPM or Yarn:\n719: Copy\n720: # NPM\n721: npm install @datadog/datadog-api-client\n722: # Yarn\n723: yarn add @datadog/datadog-api-client\n724: Usage\n725: Copy\n726: import { <VERSION> } from 'datadog-api-client';\n727: Note: Replace <VERSION> with v1 or v2, depending on which endpoints you want to use.\n728: Installation\n729: Run cargo add datadog-api-client, or add the following to Cargo.toml under [dependencies]:\n730: Copy\n731: datadog-api-client = \"0\"\n732: Usage\n733: Try the following snippet to validate your Datadog API key:\n734: Copy\n735: use datadog_api_client::datadog::Configuration;\n736: use datadog_api_client::datadogV1::api_authentication::AuthenticationAPI;\n737: #[tokio::main]\n738: async fn main() {\n739: let configuration = Configuration::new();\n740: let api = AuthenticationAPI::with_config(configuration);\n741: let resp = api.validate().await;\n742: if let Ok(value) = resp {\n743: println!(\"{:#?}\", value);\n744: } else {\n745: println!(\"{:#?}\", resp.unwrap_err());\n746: }\n747: }\n748: Or check out the libraries directly:\n749: Trying to get started with the application instead? Check out Datadog's general Getting Started docs.\n750: Can't find something?\n751: Our friendly, knowledgeable solutions engineers are here to help!\n752: Contact Us\n753: Product\n754: Observability\n755: Infrastructure\n756: Infrastructure Monitoring\n757: Custom Metrics\n758: Network Monitoring\n759: End User Device Monitoring\n760: Container Monitoring\n761: Kubernetes Autoscaling\n762: Serverless Monitoring\n763: Cloud Cost Management\n764: Storage Management\n765: GPU Monitoring\n766: Applications\n767: Application Performance Monitoring\n768: Universal Service Monitoring\n769: Continuous Profiler\n770: