      "numbered_text": "1: # Sentry Toolkit\n2: This repository contains the Sentry CLI, the Sentry MCP server, and shared\n3: packages. For the CLI, see the [CLI guide](docs/cli/README.md) and the published\n4: documentation at <https://cli.sentry.dev/>. The [documentation index](docs/README.md)\n5: covers both products and shared contributor guidance.\n6: The independent [Docs MCP Worker](apps/docs-mcp/README.md) provides documentation\n7: search and AI-generated answers backed by `docs.sentry.io`.\n8: ## Sentry MCP\n9: Sentry's MCP service is primarily designed for human-in-the-loop coding agents. Our tool selection and priorities are focused on developer workflows and debugging use cases, rather than providing a general-purpose MCP server for all Sentry functionality.\n10: This remote MCP server acts as middleware to the upstream Sentry API, optimized for coding assistants like Cursor, Claude Code, and similar development tools. It's based on [Cloudflare's work towards remote MCPs](https://blog.cloudflare.com/remote-model-context-protocol-servers-mcp/).\n11: ## Getting Started\n12: You'll find everything you need to know by visiting the deployed service in production:\n13: <https://mcp.sentry.dev>\n14: If you're looking to contribute, learn how it works, or to run this for self-hosted Sentry, continue below.\n15: ### Claude Code Plugin\n16: Install as a Claude Code plugin for automatic subagent delegation:\n17: ```shell\n18: claude plugin marketplace add getsentry/sentry-mcp\n19: claude plugin install sentry-mcp@sentry-mcp\n20: ```\n21: This provides a `sentry-mcp` subagent that Claude automatically delegates to when you ask about Sentry errors, issues, traces, or performance.\n22: For forward-looking tool variants and features:\n23: ```shell\n24: claude plugin install sentry-mcp@sentry-mcp-experimental\n25: ```\n26: ### Stdio vs Remote\n27: While this repository is focused on acting as an MCP service, we also support a `stdio` transport. It is the easiest way to run the MCP server against a self-hosted Sentry install.\n28: **Note:** The AI-powered search tools (`search_errors`, `search_traces`, `search_logs`, `search_issues`, etc.) require an LLM provider (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, or OpenRouter). These tools use natural language processing to translate queries into Sentry's query syntax. Without a configured provider, these specific tools will be unavailable, but all other tools will function normally.\n29: #### Sentry CLI (recommended)\n30: Run the stdio server with the [Sentry CLI](https://cli.sentry.dev)'s `sentry mcp` command. It uses your CLI login, so the MCP config needs no token:\n31: ```shell\n32: curl https://cli.sentry.dev/install -fsS | bash\n33: sentry auth login\n34: claude mcp add sentry -- sentry mcp\n35: ```\n36: The CLI also installs with `brew install getsentry/tools/sentry`,\n37: `npm install -g sentry`, or `irm https://cli.sentry.dev/install.ps1 | iex` on\n38: Windows. See the [CLI installation guide](https://cli.sentry.dev/getting-started/).\n39: Other clients take the same command:\n40: ```json\n41: {\n42: \"mcpServers\": {\n43: \"sentry\": {\n44: \"command\": \"sentry\",\n45: \"args\": [\"mcp\"]\n46: }\n47: }\n48: }\n49: ```\n50: Pass `<org>/` or `<org>/<project>` to limit every call to one organization or\n51: project (`sentry mcp acme/`, `sentry mcp acme/web`), and `--experimental` for\n52: forward-looking tool variants. Run `sentry mcp --help` for every flag and\n53: environment variable, or see the [`sentry mcp` docs](https://cli.sentry.dev/commands/mcp/).\n54: `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN`, when set, wins over the stored login. See\n55: [docs/operations/stdio-auth.md](docs/operations/stdio-auth.md).\n56: To use a token, create a User Auth Token in Sentry with the necessary scopes. As of writing this is:\n57: ```\n58: org:read\n59: project:read\n60: project:write\n61: team:read\n62: team:write\n63: event:write\n64: ```\n65: Need to connect to a self-hosted deployment? Log in to it with\n66: `sentry auth login --url https://sentry.example.com`, then set\n67: <code>SENTRY_HOST</code> (e.g. <code>SENTRY_HOST=sentry.example.com</code>)\n68: in the MCP server's environment, which also scopes\n69: <code>SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN</code> to that server. For isolated internal\n70: deployments that only expose plain HTTP, use an <code>http://</code>\n71: <code>SENTRY_URL</code> instead. See the\n72: [CLI self-hosted guide](https://cli.sentry.dev/self-hosted/).\n73: Seer is not part of self-hosted Sentry, so the `seer` skill is left out of the\n74: default skill set whenever the host is not `sentry.io`. If your\n75: self-hosted deployment does run Seer, opt back in explicitly:\n76: ```shell\n77: SENTRY_HOST=sentry.example.com sentry mcp --skills=inspect,seer\n78: ```\n79: You can also disable any other skill to prevent unsupported tools from being\n80: exposed:\n81: ```shell\n82: SENTRY_HOST=sentry.example.com sentry mcp --disable-skills=project-management\n83: ```\n84: For self-hosted instances without TLS:\n85: ```shell\n86: SENTRY_URL=http://sentry.internal:9000 sentry mcp\n87: ```\n88: #### Legacy: npx @sentry/mcp-server\n89: [`@sentry/mcp-server`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@sentry/mcp-server) still\n90: ships the same server, bundled without the rest of the CLI, for clients that\n91: launch it through npx. It shares the CLI's login and takes the same flags and\n92: environment variables:\n93: ```shell\n94: npx @sentry/mcp-server@latest auth login # once, to sign in\n95: npx @sentry/mcp-server@latest # uses that login\n96: SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=sentry-user-token npx @sentry/mcp-server@latest\n97: ```\n98: `--access-token` / `SENTRY_ACCESS_TOKEN` from older releases still work but are\n99: deprecated: they act as `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` and print a warning on stderr.\n100: #### Remote with an Explicit Sentry Token\n101: Remote clients that support custom HTTP headers can pass an upstream Sentry API\n102: token directly to the Cloudflare transport:\n103: ```json\n104: {\n105: \"mcpServers\": {\n106: \"sentry\": {\n107: \"url\": \"https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp\",\n108: \"headers\": {\n109: \"Authorization\": \"Sentry-Bearer ${SENTRY_ACCESS_TOKEN}\"\n110: }\n111: }\n112: }\n113: }\n114: ```\n115: `Sentry-Bearer` is intentionally separate from `Bearer`: `Bearer` is reserved\n116: for MCP OAuth access tokens. With `Sentry-Bearer`, the worker does not store,\n117: validate, exchange, or refresh the upstream token. It forwards the token through\n118: the same Sentry API calls used by OAuth-backed sessions, and the client or\n119: upstream provider remains responsible for token lifetime and refresh.\n120: Direct remote auth defaults to all active MCP skills. You can narrow the exposed\n121: tools with `?skills=inspect,triage` or `?disable-skills=seer`.\n122: #### Environment Variables\n123: ```shell\n124: SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN= # Optional after `auth login`; wins over a stored login\n125: # LLM Provider Configuration (required for AI-powered search tools)\n126: EMBEDDED_AGENT_PROVIDER= # Required when multiple provider keys are set: 'openai', 'azure-openai', 'anthropic', or 'openrouter'\n127: OPENAI_API_KEY= # Required if using OpenAI\n128: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY= # Required if using Anthropic\n129: OPENROUTER_API_KEY= # Required if using OpenRouter\n130: OPENROUTER_MODEL= # Optional OpenRouter model, defaults to 'openai/gpt-5.6-luna'\n131: OPENROUTER_REASONING_EFFORT= # Optional OpenRouter reasoning effort, defaults to 'high'\n132: # Optional overrides\n133: SENTRY_HOST= # For self-hosted deployments (drops 'seer' from the default skills)\n134: MCP_SKILLS= # Grant specific skills (comma-separated, e.g. 'inspect,seer')\n135: MCP_DISABLE_SKILLS= # Disable specific skills (comma-separated, e.g. 'project-management')\n136: ```\n137: **Important:** Always set `EMBEDDED_AGENT_PROVIDER` to explicitly specify your LLM provider. Auto-detection based on API keys alone is deprecated and will be removed in a future release. See [docs/operations/embedded-agents.md](docs/operations/embedded-agents.md) for detailed configuration options.\n138: #### Example MCP Configuration\n139: ```json\n140: {\n141: \"mcpServers\": {\n142: \"sentry\": {\n143: \"command\": \"sentry\",\n144: \"args\": [\"mcp\"],\n145: \"env\": {\n146: \"EMBEDDED_AGENT_PROVIDER\": \"openai\",\n147: \"OPENAI_API_KEY\": \"sk-...\"\n148: }\n149: }\n150: }\n151: }\n152: ```\n153: If you leave the host variable unset, the CLI automatically targets the Sentry\n154: SaaS service. Only set the override when you operate self-hosted Sentry.\n155: Setting `SENTRY_HOST` to a self-hosted host also drops the `seer` skill from\n156: the default set, since Seer is not available on self-hosted Sentry. For a\n157: self-hosted deployment that does run Seer, opt in with `MCP_SKILLS`:\n158: ```json\n159: {\n160: \"mcpServers\": {\n161: \"sentry\": {\n162: \"command\": \"sentry\",\n163: \"args\": [\"mcp\"],\n164: \"env\": {\n165: \"SENTRY_HOST\": \"sentry.example.com\",\n166: \"MCP_SKILLS\": \"inspect,seer\"\n167: }\n168: }\n169: }\n170: }\n171: ```\n172: ### Rate Limits\n173: Tool calls are subject to the Sentry API's rate limits. The MCP server retries\n174: rate-limited requests, and transient Sentry server errors on read requests,\n175: after a short wait. When a limit lasts longer, the tool returns a **Rate\n176: Limited** message that says when to try again. Agents hit the per-second\n177: limits most often by running many Sentry tool calls in parallel, so run them\n178: one at a time if you see this message.\n179: ### MCP Inspector\n180: MCP includes an [Inspector](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/tools/inspector), to easily test the service:\n181: ```shell\n182: pnpm inspector\n183: ```\n184: Enter the MCP server URL (<http://localhost:5173>) and hit connect. This should trigger the authentication flow for you.\n185: Note: If you have issues with your OAuth flow when accessing the inspector on `127.0.0.1`, try using `localhost` instead by visiting `http://localhost:6274`.\n186: ## Local Development\n187: To contribute changes, you'll need to set up your local environment:\n188: 1. **Set up environment and agent skills:**\n189: ```shell\n190: make setup-env # Creates .env files and installs shared agent skills\n191: ```\n192: This also runs `npx @sentry/dotagents install` to install shared skills from [getsentry/skills](https://github.com/getsentry/skills) into `.agents/skills/` (symlinked into `.claude/skills` and `.cursor/skills`). If you need to update skills later, run it directly:\n193: ```shell\n194: npx @sentry/dotagents install\n195: ```\n196: 2. **Create an OAuth App in Sentry** (Settings => API => [Applications](https://sentry.io/settings/account/api/applications/)):\n197: - Homepage URL: `http://localhost:5173`\n198: - Authorized Redirect URIs: `http://localhost:5173/oauth/callback`\n199: - Note your Client ID and generate a Client secret\n200: 3. **Configure your credentials:**\n201: - Edit `.env` in the root directory and add either `OPENAI_API_KEY` or `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n202: - Edit `packages/mcp-cloudflare/.env` and add:\n203: - `SENTRY_CLIENT_ID=your_development_sentry_client_id`\n204: - `SENTRY_CLIENT_SECRET=your_development_sentry_client_secret`\n205: - `COOKIE_SECRET=my-super-secret-cookie`\n206: 4. **Start the development server:**\n207: ```shell\n208: pnpm dev\n209: ```\n210: ### Verify\n211: Run the server locally to make it available at `http://localhost:5173`\n212: ```shell\n213: pnpm dev\n214: ```\n215: To test the local server, enter `http://localhost:5173/mcp` into Inspector and hit connect. Once you follow the prompts, you'll be able to \"List Tools\".\n216: ### Tests\n217: There are three test suites included: unit tests, evaluations, and manual testing.\n218: **Unit tests** can be run using:\n219: ```shell\n220: pnpm test\n221: ```\n222: **Evaluations** require a `.env` file in the project root with some config:\n223: ```shell\n224: # .env (in project root)\n225: OPENAI_API_KEY= # Use OpenAI-backed AI-powered tools\n226: OPENROUTER_API_KEY= # Or use OpenRouter-backed AI-powered tools\n227: ```\n228: Note: The root `.env` file provides defaults for all packages. Individual packages can have their own `.env` files to override these defaults during development.\n229: Once that's done you can run them using:\n230: ```shell\n231: pnpm eval\n232: ```\n233: **Manual testing** (preferred for testing MCP changes):\n234: ```shell\n235: # Test with local dev server (default: http://localhost:5173)\n236: pnpm -w run cli \"who am I?\"\n237: # Test against production\n238: pnpm -w run cli --mcp-host=https://mcp.sentry.dev \"query\"\n239: # Test with local stdio mode (requires SENTRY_ACCESS_TOKEN)\n240: pnpm -w run cli --access-token=TOKEN \"query\"\n241: ```\n242: Note: The CLI defaults to `http://localhost:5173`. Override with `--mcp-host` or set `MCP_URL` environment variable.\n243: **Comprehensive testing playbooks:**\n244: - **Stdio testing:** See `docs/testing/stdio.md` for complete guide on building, running, and testing the stdio implementation (IDEs, MCP Inspector)\n245: - **Remote testing:** See `docs/testing/remote.md` for complete guide on testing the remote server (OAuth, web UI, CLI client)\n246: ## Development Notes\n247: ### Automated Code Review\n248: This repository uses automated code review tools (like Cursor BugBot) to help identify potential issues in pull requests. These tools provide helpful feedback and suggestions, but **we do not recommend making these checks required** as the accuracy is still evolving and can produce false positives.\n249: The automated reviews should be treated as:\n250: - ✅ **Helpful suggestions** to consider during code review\n251: - ✅ **Starting points** for discussion and improvement\n252: - ❌ **Not blocking requirements** for merging PRs\n253: - ❌ **Not replacements** for human code review\n254: When addressing automated feedback, focus on the underlying concerns rather than strictly following every suggestion.\n255: ### Contributor Documentation\n256: Looking to contribute? Start at the [Toolkit documentation index](docs/README.md)\n257: for CLI, MCP, and shared guides. See `AGENTS.md` for repository rules and\n258: `packages/cli/AGENTS.md` for CLI-specific rules."
